/* Guided-mode focus packs: big idea, why it matters, plain-words gist per part, core/extra flag, things to remember. Guidance only; the lesson text is unchanged. */
window.FOCUS=Object.assign(window.FOCUS||{},{"p26m3":{"big":"Endpoint support means putting a working, secured device and account in someone's hands and keeping it working, and every step of it has a security reason.","why":"Endpoint work is most of an entry-level day, and every step has a security reason, which is how a help-desk technician turns into a security person.","parts":[{"t":"Imaging and provisioning","gist":"Provisioning turns a box into a ready, secured device. The modern way, Windows Autopilot with Intune, skips the image: the device enrolls itself, gets policy and apps, and turns on BitLocker. The checklist stays the same whatever the tool.","core":true},{"t":"Accounts and MFA","gist":"Accounts are created when someone joins, changed when they move roles, and disabled within hours when they leave. Access goes to groups, not people. MFA varies in strength; only FIDO2 keys and passkeys resist phishing.","core":true},{"t":"Printers, peripherals and mobile","gist":"Printers fail in three places: the device, the path to it, or the driver. Dock problems are usually firmware, cable or power budget. Phones are managed endpoints, and a lost one is a security incident with a 15-minute wipe clock.","core":true},{"t":"Remote-support tools","gist":"Remote tools let you see and drive a user's screen, but only with consent. Ask before clicking, never ask for a password, log the session, and never expose RDP (port 3389) to the internet.","core":true},{"t":"The common failures and the first move","gist":"Each common Windows, macOS and Linux failure has a known first move, such as sfc /scannow for a slow Windows boot or df -h when Linux says no space left. A wrong clock can break logins.","core":true},{"t":"Security hygiene at the desk","gist":"The desk is part of security. Make phishing reports one click and thank every reporter, verify a caller before resetting anything, and never share accounts or type your admin password on a user's machine.","core":true},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"Where this module shows up in Google IT Support, CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2, and Security+.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Modern provisioning (Autopilot with Intune, or Apple ADE) enrolls a clean OEM install, applies policy and apps, and turns on BitLocker with the recovery key escrowed.","part":0},{"p":"SMS codes are phishable and SIM-swappable; FIDO2 security keys and passkeys are the only phishing-resistant MFA factors.","part":1},{"p":"Printers fail at the device, the path (a print queue or direct-IP printing to port 9100) or the driver.","part":2},{"p":"RDP uses TCP 3389 and must never face the internet; use a VPN or a gateway instead.","part":3},{"p":"Verify a caller before any reset: a ticket from the real account, a callback to the directory number, or a manager's confirmation.","part":5}]},"p26m4":{"big":"Documentation records exactly what exists and how to fix it, for a stranger who was not there, so the answer keeps working when you are asleep.","why":"One hour of writing saves a hundred hours of rediscovery. Documentation is the one entry-level product a director reads, which makes it the fastest route to promotion.","parts":[{"t":"Four documents and what they are for","gist":"There are four main documents: a runbook (exact steps for operators), a KB article (how to spot and fix a known issue), a postmortem (the blame-free story of an outage), and a network diagram, one layer per drawing.","core":true},{"t":"Anatomy of a KB article","gist":"A KB article has symptoms, cause, numbered fix, verification, escalation, and an owner with a review date. Title it with the words a technician would search, and say what should appear after each step.","core":true},{"t":"Tools","gist":"Your working tools: Markdown (a plain-text format you can learn in an hour), a wiki, diagram tools like diagrams.net or Mermaid, annotated screenshots, and short recordings under two minutes, always backed by written steps.","core":false},{"t":"The style guide","gist":"House rules that make articles easy to follow under stress: write to 'you' in active voice, one action per step starting with a verb, expand acronyms, cut 'simply' and 'just', lead with the 80% fix, and never include a password.","core":true},{"t":"Why documentation promotes you","gist":"Documentation is entry-level work that is both visible and multiplying: a KB article with 400 views is 400 tickets you half-solved. It also protects you in a postmortem, so make it visible on purpose.","core":true},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"Where documentation shows up on CompTIA A+ Core 2, in ITIL, in Google IT Support and in Project+.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"A runbook is an operator procedure: trigger, exact commands, expected output at each step, rollback, verification and escalation.","part":0},{"p":"Draw a network diagram one layer per drawing (physical, layer 2 or layer 3), with hostnames, IPs and VLAN IDs on the lines.","part":0},{"p":"Every KB article needs symptoms in users' words, cause, numbered fix, verification, escalation, and an owner and review date. An article nobody owns is a rumor.","part":1},{"p":"Screenshots support text but never replace it: they go stale with every UI change and nobody can search a picture.","part":1},{"p":"Never put a password, token or private key in a document; link to the vault entry instead.","part":3}]},"p26m5":{"big":"A hiring manager cannot see your brain, so you build a trail of proof (résumé, LinkedIn, GitHub, home lab, certifications), each piece earning a look at the next.","why":"These artifacts are what get you the interview, and the certification ladder is the order in which US job postings actually gate roles.","parts":[{"t":"The home lab, with what you own","gist":"Your gaming PC can host virtual machines. Add up their RAM, about 16–22 GB, so 32 GB is comfortable. Build in order, starting with an Active Directory domain, attack only what you own, and keep a build log.","core":true},{"t":"GitHub as a résumé","gist":"Your GitHub is evidence: a profile README, six pinned repos of lab docs, scripts, write-ups of retired Hack The Box machines and KB articles, and small frequent commits. Never commit secrets, and give every repo a README.","core":true},{"t":"A LinkedIn that recruiters find","gist":"Recruiters search LinkedIn by keywords, title and location, so write the profile for search: a 220-character headline with role, skills and certs, skills copied from job postings, every badge linked, and Open to Work turned on.","core":true},{"t":"An ATS-friendly résumé with real bullets","gist":"Hiring software (an ATS) reads your résumé into fields, so keep it to one column with standard headings and the posting's exact words. Each bullet is action verb, what, how and a measurable result, and field work translates directly.","core":true},{"t":"The certification ladder","gist":"Certifications are hiring filters, not education. The ladder runs Google IT Support and ITIL, then A+, Network+, Security+, then Linux+ or CCNA, and on to CySA+ or PenTest+, OSCP+ and GREM. Pay bands are markets, not promises.","core":true},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"No exam tests this module, but every exam depends on it; the Google certificate and CompTIA cover résumés and interviews only at a surface level.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Budget lab RAM honestly: about 16–22 GB of guests, so 32 GB on the host is comfortable and 16 GB means running fewer at once.","part":0},{"p":"An Active Directory domain with two clients and group policy is the single most interview-relevant lab for help desk and sysadmin roles.","part":0},{"p":"An ATS-friendly résumé is one column, standard headings, no tables or text boxes, and the posting's exact words: 'Microsoft Intune', not 'MDM'.","part":3},{"p":"Write each résumé bullet as action verb + what + how + measurable result.","part":3},{"p":"Ladder order: Google IT Support and ITIL, A+, Network+, Security+, then Linux+ or CCNA. Year-one certifications cost roughly $1,600–2,300, plus books and a mini PC.","part":4}]},"p26m6":{"big":"Getting hired is a funnel in which every stage, from application to offer, has one job, to earn the next, and every stage can be prepared for.","why":"The interview is the real exam. Preparing for it like one turns your certificates and lab into a salary, and a plan for the first 90 days earns the second job.","parts":[{"t":"Applying strategically","gist":"Aim at roles that hire career changers, like help desk, desktop support and field service. Apply within 48 hours, apply if you meet 60% of the requirements, send 10–15 tailored applications a week, and ask for referrals.","core":true},{"t":"Technical screens","gist":"Entry-level screens test your method, not trivia, so think aloud. Expect DNS, printing, switch versus router, Active Directory, a slow PC and common ports. Saying you would check the KB and escalate with evidence is a correct tier-one answer.","core":true},{"t":"Behavioral questions and STAR stories","gist":"'Tell me about a time' questions are answered with STAR: Situation, Task, Action, Result, in about ninety seconds. Prepare six true field stories; the same six cover conflict, pressure, failure, initiative, teamwork and learning.","core":true},{"t":"Take-home tasks and practicals","gist":"Take-home tasks are things like a KB article, a script that creates users from a CSV, or a network diagnosis. Stay within the time they asked, state your assumptions and show your method. Unpaid production work is a red flag.","core":true},{"t":"Offers and negotiation","gist":"Negotiate from sourced data, not hope. Deflect an early salary question once, get the offer in writing, then counter once with a reason. Entry-level gains are typically 3–8%, and if base is fixed, negotiate the rest of the package.","core":true},{"t":"The first 90 days","gist":"A 30-60-90 plan makes a new hire promotable: learn in the first 30 days, contribute by day 60 (own a queue, write three KB articles), and own one measurable improvement by day 90. Tell your manager in week one.","core":true},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"No exam covers this directly; Google IT Support and Project+ overlap a little. The real exam is the one in the room.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Apply within 48 hours of a posting, and apply if you meet 60% of the requirements; requirement lists are wish lists.","part":0},{"p":"At tier one, 'I would check the knowledge base, then escalate with these facts' is a correct answer when you don't know.","part":1},{"p":"Know the ports: HTTP 80, HTTPS 443, RDP 3389, SSH 22, and why RDP never faces the internet.","part":1},{"p":"STAR means Situation, Task, Action, Result: about ninety seconds, then stop.","part":2},{"p":"Counter an offer once, with a sourced reason. Entry-level gains are typically 3–8%; if base is fixed, ask for exam reimbursement, a review or a title.","part":4}]},"p26m7":{"big":"The path from help desk to reverse engineer is real but takes years, and the whole security career rests on one word: authorization.","why":"This is the map for the next ten years. Staying inside the law, testing only what you own or have written permission for, protects the whole career.","parts":[{"t":"The main path and its alternatives","gist":"The usual climb runs help desk, sysadmin or network, security analyst, penetration tester, then reverse engineer; most people reach pentester in five to seven years. Cloud, DevOps, data and GRC are branches, not detours.","core":true},{"t":"Ethics and the law","gist":"Test only systems you own or have written permission for; anything else can be a crime under the CFAA, which outlaws unauthorized access. Report flaws through responsible disclosure, and practice only on sanctioned platforms and your own VMs.","core":true},{"t":"Communities and conferences","gist":"Jobs in this field move through people. BSides conferences make the best first event, and local chapters and Discords keep you connected. Volunteer, talk to strangers, follow up within a week; mentors are usually found this way.","core":true},{"t":"Burnout and sustainable study","gist":"Your study system has to survive bad weeks. Spaced repetition beats re-reading, practice exams beat highlighting, and 60–90 minute phone-away blocks plus sleep make real progress. Treat burnout as a signal to cut load.","core":true},{"t":"Mentoring others","gist":"Start mentoring as soon as you are one step ahead of someone. Teaching is the deepest form of retrieval practice, it makes you visible to the people who promote, and it is how you get handed the team.","core":true},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"Where ethics, scoping and continuing education show up on Security+, PenTest+, the Google certificate, ISC2 and GIAC.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Typical timing: help desk 0–1.5 years, sysadmin or network 1.5–4, security analyst 3–5, penetration tester 5–7, reverse engineer 7+.","part":0},{"p":"The CFAA (18 U.S.C. § 1030) criminalizes access without authorization or exceeding authorized access; the DOJ's good-faith policy is not a shield.","part":1},{"p":"Written permission names the scope, the dates, the methods and an emergency contact; stop and report anything you find out of scope.","part":1},{"p":"Responsible disclosure: contact the vendor, give a deadline (CERT/CC uses 45 days, Project Zero 90), and don't publish exploit details before the fix.","part":1},{"p":"CompTIA certifications renew every three years with CEUs, and a higher cert renews the lower ones; GIAC renews every four.","part":3}]},"p27m1":{"big":"An enterprise network is built in layers with one job each, access, distribution and core, so it survives any one failure and the next engineer can read it.","why":"Every CCNA question with a topology assumes you know which layer does what, and every lab item assumes the IOS command-line modes are reflex.","parts":[{"t":"The campus hierarchy","gist":"Access switches are where devices plug in, distribution is where routing and policy live, and the core moves traffic fast with no policy. Small sites merge core and distribution into a collapsed core.","core":true},{"t":"Spine-leaf: the data-center shape","gist":"Data centers use spine-leaf instead: every leaf switch, where servers attach, connects to every spine. Any server is exactly two hops from any other, and adding a spine adds capacity to every leaf at once.","core":false},{"t":"WAN and branch designs","gist":"Branches connect in a few named shapes: point-to-point, hub-and-spoke (cheap, but headquarters is a single point of failure), full mesh, or the partial mesh most companies run. SOHO puts routing, switching, Wi-Fi, NAT and firewall in one box.","core":true},{"t":"Cabling, optics and PoE","gist":"Pick cable by distance and speed: Cat 6A carries 10 Gbps to 100 m, OM4 multimode fiber 400 m, single-mode 10–80 km. Match the optic to the fiber. PoE powers devices over the cable from a finite switch budget.","core":true},{"t":"Device roles","gist":"Each box has a job: routers forward between networks and terminate the WAN, Layer 2 switches forward by MAC address inside a VLAN, Layer 3 switches route between VLANs at hardware speed, and firewalls enforce policy.","core":true},{"t":"The Cisco IOS CLI","gist":"The Cisco command line has modes, and the prompt shows where you are: > only looks, # runs show and copy, and configuration modes change the device. Nothing survives a power loss until you save it.","core":true},{"t":"Where to practice","gist":"Practice in Packet Tracer, CML-Free (real IOS, up to five nodes), GNS3 or EVE-NG with images you own, or used Catalyst and ISR gear kept off the internet edge. Then the CCNA exam mapping.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Access is Layer 2 with PoE and port policies; distribution is the Layer 3 boundary with gateways and ACLs; the core carries no policy.","part":0},{"p":"Oversubscription: 48 × 1 Gbps access ports against 2 × 10 Gbps uplinks is 48:20, or 2.4:1.","part":0},{"p":"Cat 6 does 10 Gbps only to 55 m; Cat 6A reaches 100 m. A 10GBASE-SR optic on single-mode fiber lights nothing.","part":3},{"p":"PoE at the port: 802.3af 15.4 W, 802.3at PoE+ 30 W, 802.3bt 60 W or 90 W. Budget by each device's actual class.","part":3},{"p":"The running-config lives in RAM; copy running-config startup-config saves it to NVRAM. User EXEC shows >, privileged EXEC shows #.","part":5}]},"p27m2":{"big":"A switch learns where each device sits; VLANs carve one switch fabric into separate networks, and Spanning Tree stops redundant links from forming a loop.","why":"Network Access is 20% of the exam and its most common lab item, and it is where most real outages begin: loops, native VLAN mismatches and pruned VLANs.","parts":[{"t":"MAC learning and forwarding","gist":"A switch learns each frame's source MAC and port. To deliver, it forwards out the known port, drops the frame if the destination is on the arrival port, or floods the whole VLAN when the destination is unknown or broadcast.","core":true},{"t":"VLANs and 802.1Q trunking","gist":"A VLAN groups ports into one broadcast domain. A trunk carries many VLANs between switches by tagging each frame with a 12-bit VLAN ID, except the native VLAN, which travels untagged and must match on both ends.","core":true},{"t":"Inter-VLAN routing: router-on-a-stick versus SVI","gist":"Traffic between VLANs needs routing. Router-on-a-stick uses one router link with a subinterface per VLAN, fine for a small branch. At scale a Layer 3 switch routes with an SVI, a virtual interface per VLAN.","core":true},{"t":"Spanning Tree","gist":"Redundant switch links create loops that can take a floor down in seconds. Spanning Tree elects one root bridge, keeps the lowest-cost paths to it and blocks the rest. Set the root deliberately and guard the edge ports.","core":true},{"t":"EtherChannel","gist":"EtherChannel bundles two to eight matching links into one logical port that Spanning Tree treats as a single link. LACP negotiates the bundle, and each flow stays on one member, so a single conversation never gets the full total.","core":true},{"t":"Layer 2 security: port security, DHCP snooping, DAI","gist":"Three switch features stop three attacks: port security limits MACs per port against MAC flooding, DHCP snooping blocks rogue DHCP servers on untrusted ports, and Dynamic ARP Inspection checks ARP against snooping's table to stop spoofing.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"802.1Q inserts a 4-byte tag with a 12-bit VLAN ID; the native VLAN travels untagged and must match on both ends of the trunk.","part":1},{"p":"The root bridge has the lowest bridge ID: priority (default 32,768) plus the VLAN number, with the lowest MAC breaking ties.","part":3},{"p":"BPDU Guard err-disables a PortFast port the instant it hears a BPDU, which stops a rogue switch plugged into a wall jack.","part":3},{"p":"EtherChannel balances per flow: two 1 Gbps links give 2 Gbps in aggregate but 1 Gbps per conversation.","part":4},{"p":"Port security stops MAC flooding, DHCP snooping stops rogue DHCP servers, and Dynamic ARP Inspection stops ARP spoofing.","part":5}]},"p27m3":{"big":"A router always uses the most specific matching route; administrative distance only breaks ties between identical prefixes, and metric only compares routes from the same protocol.","why":"IP Connectivity is the exam's biggest domain at 25%, and reading a routing table correctly is the core skill of every network job.","parts":[{"t":"Reading the routing table","gist":"show ip route lists every known network with a code for its source. The most specific (longest) matching prefix wins; administrative distance, a trust ranking of sources, breaks ties only between identical prefixes.","core":true},{"t":"Static routes","gist":"Static routes are typed by hand: precise and cheap, but blind to failures beyond the directly connected link. A floating static is a backup with a worse administrative distance that appears only when the main route disappears.","core":true},{"t":"OSPF, single area, in depth","gist":"OSPF routers share a map of every link and cost, and each computes shortest paths from it. Neighbors form only when area, subnet, timers and authentication match. On shared segments, a designated router is elected.","core":true},{"t":"BGP: where it fits","gist":"Inside a company you run OSPF; between companies and ISPs you run BGP, which chooses paths by policy, not shortest distance. For CCNA you only need to know what it is.","core":false},{"t":"IPv6 addressing","gist":"IPv6 addresses are 128 bits in hex; drop leading zeros and write one run of zero groups as ::. Global is 2000::/3, link-local fe80::/10, multicast ff00::/8, with no broadcast. SLAAC lets hosts build their own address.","core":true},{"t":"First-hop redundancy: HSRP","gist":"Hosts know only one gateway, so HSRP lets two routers share a virtual IP and MAC, one active and one standby. Priority decides the active router only if preempt is on; tracking moves the role when an uplink fails.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Administrative distance: connected 0, static 1, eBGP 20, EIGRP 90, OSPF 110, RIP 120. It only breaks ties between identical prefixes.","part":0},{"p":"A floating static route has a deliberately worse administrative distance, so it enters the table only when the primary route disappears.","part":1},{"p":"OSPF cost is reference bandwidth ÷ interface bandwidth; the 100 Mbps default makes 1 Gbps and 10 Gbps both cost 1, so set auto-cost reference-bandwidth 100000.","part":2},{"p":"EUI-64 builds the interface ID from the MAC: split it in half, insert fffe, and flip the seventh bit of the first byte.","part":4},{"p":"HSRP priority defaults to 100, and the highest wins the active role only if preempt is configured.","part":5}]},"p27m4":{"big":"One edge router runs the services a site depends on: it hands out addresses, shares one public address, filters traffic, keeps time and logs, prioritizes voice and tunnels to headquarters.","why":"These are the services every edge router runs, and NAT and ACL configuration are the exam's classic hands-on items.","parts":[{"t":"DHCP and DNS on the router","gist":"Clients get addresses in four DHCP steps: Discover, Offer, Request, Acknowledge. Broadcasts stop at the router, so ip helper-address relays them to a central server. DNS on a router is almost always just a client.","core":true},{"t":"NAT and PAT","gist":"Private addresses can't cross the internet, so NAT rewrites them at the edge. Static NAT maps one address to one; PAT lets hundreds of hosts share one public address by also rewriting ports. NAT hides addresses but is not security.","core":true},{"t":"Access control lists","gist":"An ACL is a list of permit and deny rules read top-down, first match wins, with a hidden deny at the end. Wildcard masks are inverted subnet masks. Put extended ACLs near the source and standard ones near the destination.","core":true},{"t":"NTP, syslog, SNMP and file transfer","gist":"Logs are evidence only if clocks agree, so NTP syncs time, ranking sources by stratum. Syslog messages carry severities 0 to 7, SNMPv3 lets monitoring poll devices securely, and TFTP, FTP or SCP move backups.","core":true},{"t":"QoS basics","gist":"QoS decides who waits when the link is full. Voice is marked EF 46 near the source and sent through a strict-priority queue, while bulk traffic waits. Prioritizing never creates bandwidth.","core":true},{"t":"WAN technologies and SD-WAN","gist":"WAN links range from leased lines and carrier MPLS to Metro Ethernet, internet and 4G/5G backup. SD-WAN puts a controller over all of them, measuring each path and steering each application to one that meets its SLA.","core":false},{"t":"IPsec and remote-access VPNs","gist":"IPsec is a framework: IKE authenticates the two ends and agrees keys, then ESP encrypts each packet. Site-to-site uses tunnel mode; remote-access clients like Cisco Secure Client tunnel a laptop to a firewall with MFA.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"DHCP is DORA: Discover, Offer, Request, Acknowledge. ip helper-address relays the broadcast to a server on another subnet.","part":0},{"p":"PAT (NAT overload) lets many hosts share one public address by rewriting source ports too.","part":1},{"p":"Wildcard masks invert subnet masks: /24 is 0.0.0.255, /30 is 0.0.0.3. Every ACL ends with an invisible deny any.","part":2},{"p":"Syslog severities run 0 emergencies to 7 debugging; logging trap warnings sends levels 0 through 4, not just warnings.","part":3},{"p":"IKE uses UDP 500, or 4500 with NAT traversal; ESP is IP protocol 50 and encrypts each packet.","part":6}]},"p27m5":{"big":"In an enterprise, wireless, AAA and device hardening are one system: identity decides what each port, wired or wireless, is allowed to do.","why":"Security Fundamentals is 15% of the exam, wireless appears in three domains, and defended networks are the ones you will be hired to test.","parts":[{"t":"WLAN architectures","gist":"Enterprise access points are lightweight: they handle the real-time radio work while a controller handles authentication, roaming and policy, talking through CAPWAP tunnels. APs plug into access ports, controllers into trunks. Modes include local, FlexConnect and monitor.","core":true},{"t":"Building a WLAN","gist":"In the controller GUI you create a WLAN with its SSID, tie it to a client VLAN and QoS level, choose WPA2 or WPA3 security, and decide which APs broadcast it. The exam's hands-on item is a WPA2 PSK WLAN.","core":false},{"t":"WPA2, WPA3, 802.1X and RADIUS","gist":"Personal Wi-Fi shares one passphrase with everyone; Enterprise checks each user with 802.1X against a RADIUS server. WPA2 uses AES-CCMP, and WPA3 switches to SAE so a captured handshake can't be guessed offline. EAP-TLS with certificates is strongest.","core":true},{"t":"AAA: TACACS+ versus RADIUS","gist":"TACACS+ guards device administration: TCP 49, the whole payload encrypted, and per-command authorization. RADIUS guards network access such as 802.1X, Wi-Fi and VPN, over UDP 1812/1813, encrypting only the password. Configure AAA with a local fallback.","core":true},{"t":"Hardening the device itself","gist":"The management plane is the first target, so harden every device: scrypt-hashed secrets, SSH version 2 only, a VTY ACL allowing only the management subnet, login rate limits, timeouts, and unused ports shut down.","core":true},{"t":"Threats at the network layer, and the controls that blunt them","gist":"Each common attack has a matching control: port security for MAC flooding, DHCP snooping for rogue DHCP, Dynamic ARP Inspection for ARP spoofing, native VLAN off 1 for VLAN hopping, BPDU Guard for Spanning Tree attacks. Practice only where authorized.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"CAPWAP uses UDP 5246 for control (always DTLS-encrypted) and UDP 5247 for client data.","part":0},{"p":"802.1X has three parties: supplicant (the client), authenticator (AP/WLC or switch port) and authentication server (RADIUS). The authenticator never sees the credential.","part":2},{"p":"TACACS+ is TCP 49, encrypts the whole payload and authorizes per command; RADIUS is UDP 1812/1813 and encrypts only the password.","part":3},{"p":"Type 7 from service password-encryption is reversible in seconds; use enable algorithm-type scrypt secret (type 9) instead.","part":4},{"p":"VLAN hopping by double tagging is stopped by moving the native VLAN off 1, disabling DTP and shutting unused ports.","part":5}]},"p27m6":{"big":"Automation makes a repository, not each box, the source of truth, and tools like Ansible push only what differs, so running them twice changes nothing.","why":"Hand-configured networks drift, and every change typed by hand is a chance to mistype. Automation brings speed, consistency and fewer errors; it is 10% of the exam and a growing part of the job.","parts":[{"t":"Why networks are automated","gist":"Hand-configured devices drift until nobody knows the intended state. Automation keeps that state in a repository and pushes it out. Idempotency, meaning a second run changes nothing, makes it safe to run nightly and spot drift.","core":true},{"t":"Planes, SDN and controllers","gist":"Every device has a data plane that forwards, a control plane that decides how, and a management plane you talk to. A controller centralizes some of that control: apps talk to it northbound, and it programs devices southbound.","core":true},{"t":"REST APIs","gist":"A REST API gives each resource a URI and uses HTTP verbs: GET reads, POST creates, PUT replaces, PATCH modifies, DELETE removes. Each request carries its own token, and status codes like 200, 401 and 404 say what happened.","core":true},{"t":"Data formats: JSON and YAML","gist":"JSON has six value types: object, array, string, number, boolean and null, with double quotes and no comments. YAML writes the same tree with indentation, allows comments, and is what Ansible playbooks use.","core":true},{"t":"Ansible and Terraform","gist":"Ansible is agentless: it connects over SSH, runs a YAML playbook and pushes only the difference. Terraform is declarative: you describe the end state, plan shows the changes, apply makes them, and a state file tracks it.","core":true},{"t":"Python with Netmiko","gist":"Netmiko lets a Python script log in over SSH and collect show output from every device. Start read-only, keep credentials in environment variables, and push changes only behind a dry-run, after lab tests and a backup.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Idempotency: running the same playbook twice yields the same end state and changes nothing the second time.","part":0},{"p":"The data plane forwards, the control plane decides how (OSPF, STP, ARP), and the management plane is how humans and tools reach the device.","part":1},{"p":"REST status codes: 200 OK, 201 created, 401 unauthenticated, 403 forbidden, 404 not found, 429 rate-limited.","part":2},{"p":"JSON's six value types are object, array, string, number, boolean and null.","part":3},{"p":"Ansible is agentless and push; Terraform declares an end state and keeps a state file. Puppet and Chef, agent-based and pull, were dropped from the exam.","part":4}]},"p27m7":{"big":"Good troubleshooting is not guessing: follow a method, halve the problem with each test, and read the show commands until the fault has nowhere left to hide.","why":"Troubleshooting is the skill interviews actually test and the verb inside half the exam's objectives, and a plan for the exam turns a year of study into a credential.","parts":[{"t":"Structured troubleshooting","gist":"Follow the method: identify, theorize, test, fix, verify, document. Choose an approach on purpose, such as bottom-up, top-down or divide and conquer, where one ping to the gateway halves the search. Change one thing at a time.","core":true},{"t":"The show commands that matter, and what to read in them","gist":"Each symptom has a show command that answers it, from interface status words and error counters to trunks, Spanning Tree, routes, OSPF neighbors and ACL hit counts. Debugs are a last resort and must be narrow.","core":true},{"t":"The outage drill, worked","gist":"A worked outage: the PC's APIPA address pointed to DHCP, the gateway checked out fine, and show interfaces status found an uplink err-disabled by BPDU Guard. The fix removed the cause, then was saved and documented.","core":true},{"t":"The exam lab blueprint","gist":"The exam includes live IOS lab items designed for five to seven minutes each. Thirteen configurations, from VLANs and trunks to OSPF, ACLs, PAT and HSRP, should be drilled until your fingers know them.","core":true},{"t":"The exam itself","gist":"Facts and strategy for CCNA 200-301 v1.1: 120 minutes, US$300, six weighted domains. Check Cisco's current exam topics before booking. Bank time for labs, read the question before the exhibit, and subnet on paper.","core":false},{"t":"A Packet Tracer practice plan","gist":"A six-week, forty-minutes-a-day plan: rebuild the labs, grow one mega lab, find planted faults with show commands alone, then take full practice exams. Book the exam at the start of week 5.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Divide and conquer: ping the gateway. If that works, the problem is beyond it; if not, it is local.","part":0},{"p":"Type reload in 10 before a risky remote change, so the device reverts if you lock yourself out; reload cancel when it works.","part":0},{"p":"show ip interface brief: up/up is healthy, up/down means Layer 1 is fine but Layer 2 is dead, down/down is the cable or far side.","part":1},{"p":"Late collisions mean a duplex mismatch; CRC and input errors point to the cable, the optic or duplex.","part":1},{"p":"show interfaces status err-disabled names why a port died, such as bpduguard or psecure-violation. Fix the cause, not just the symptom.","part":2}]},"p28m1":{"big":"The CLI does not scale, so networks are now changed by code that works from a source of truth saying what every device is supposed to be.","why":"At hundreds of devices, automation is the difference between a change you can make and one you avoid making, and it lets you see when a device has drifted from its intended state.","parts":[{"t":"Configuration drift","gist":"Drift is the gap between what a device is supposed to be and what it runs, often from fixes nobody wrote down. Detect it by comparing with a golden config; prevent it by letting only the pipeline write to devices.","core":true},{"t":"The automation maturity model","gist":"Five levels: 0 manual CLI, 1 read-only scripts, 2 templated pushes, 3 a source of truth plus infrastructure as code, 4 closed-loop intent. Most enterprises sit between 1 and 2, and you earn pushing by first reading reliably.","core":true},{"t":"SDN and controllers","gist":"Software-defined networking moves the decision of where traffic goes into software that sees the whole network. At work that means controllers like Catalyst Center, ACI, Meraki and SD-WAN Manager, with REST APIs northbound and device protocols southbound.","core":true},{"t":"Intent-based networking","gist":"With intent-based networking you declare an outcome, and the system translates it into configuration, activates it, then assures it by checking reality against the declaration. That loop is the top of the maturity model.","core":true},{"t":"Source of truth","gist":"A source of truth is the database of what the network is supposed to be: devices, interfaces, cables, prefixes, VLANs. NetBox is the open-source standard and holds desired state; automation never reads a device and calls that truth.","core":true},{"t":"The role and the market","gist":"Network automation engineers own the path from source of truth to devices with Python, Git, APIs and Ansible. US pay averaged near $109,000 in August 2026, and physical-layer experience plus code is a rare mix.","core":false},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"Where this shows up on Cisco's 200-901 CCNAAUTO exam: the Infrastructure and Automation domain and the Cisco Platforms and Development domain.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Configuration drift is the divergence of a device's running configuration from its intended, documented state.","part":0},{"p":"Maturity levels: 0 manual CLI, 1 read-only scripts, 2 templated pushes, 3 source of truth plus IaC, 4 closed loop or intent.","part":1},{"p":"SDN at work is controller-based management: Catalyst Center (DNA Center until 2023), ACI's APIC, the Meraki Dashboard and Catalyst SD-WAN Manager.","part":2},{"p":"Intent-based networking runs a loop of translate, activate, assure.","part":3},{"p":"NetBox models the desired state of the network, not the operational state. The running config is what a device believes, not the truth.","part":4}]},"p28m2":{"big":"With Python, Netmiko and a parser, one script can talk to fifty switches reliably and bring back structured data you can act on.","why":"Read-only audit scripts are the safest, most immediately useful code a network engineer can write, and the foundation every later module pushes from.","parts":[{"t":"Virtual environments","gist":"Give each project its own virtual environment, a private package space, so tool versions don't collide. Pin what you install with pip freeze into requirements.txt, and commit that file, never the .venv folder.","core":true},{"t":"Paramiko and Netmiko","gist":"Paramiko speaks raw SSH; Netmiko sits on top and handles prompts, paging and config mode for more than a hundred device types. Its core calls are ConnectHandler, send_command, send_config_set and save_config. Credentials never go in the script.","core":true},{"t":"NAPALM: vendor-neutral facts and config","gist":"NAPALM returns the same data structure from any vendor through getters like get_facts. Its safe change workflow is: load a candidate, compare_config to see the diff, then commit or discard.","core":true},{"t":"Parsing: from screen text to data","gist":"CLI output is written for eyes, so parse it into Python structures you can count, filter and compare. TextFSM templates, regular expressions or Genie do the parsing; JSON, YAML and XML load with their own libraries.","core":true},{"t":"Errors and idempotency","gist":"With fifty devices some will fail, so catch the specific exceptions, record each failure as data and keep going. Idempotent means a second run changes nothing: read the state, compute the difference, act only on that.","core":true},{"t":"Worked script: audit 50 switches","gist":"A short script audits 50 switches with ten threads in about 40 seconds instead of 400, because it spends its time waiting on the network. Ten workers is a polite default.","core":true},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"Where this shows up on CCNAAUTO: comparing and parsing JSON, XML and YAML, and reading Python that calls a library.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"One virtual environment per project; pin dependencies with pip freeze > requirements.txt and commit that file, not .venv.","part":0},{"p":"Netmiko's two key exceptions: NetmikoTimeoutException (unreachable, wrong port, ACL) and NetmikoAuthenticationException (bad credentials).","part":1},{"p":"NAPALM's safe change: load_merge_candidate, compare_config (an empty diff means nothing would change), then commit_config or discard_config.","part":2},{"p":"Use yaml.safe_load, never yaml.load, on untrusted input; json.loads maps JSON straight to dicts and lists.","part":3},{"p":"Threads help I/O-bound scripts: 50 switches at about 8 seconds each take 400 seconds in sequence, roughly 40 with ten threads.","part":5}]},"p28m3":{"big":"Every controller and cloud speaks REST: HTTP verbs act on resources at URIs, status codes say what happened, and a credential travels with every request.","why":"Learn REST once and every platform becomes the same exercise with different nouns. It is the largest CCNAAUTO domain at 20 percent, and a status code turns a guess into a diagnosis.","parts":[{"t":"REST in five constraints","gist":"In REST everything is a resource with a URI, exchanged as JSON, and every request stands alone with its own credentials. HTTP verbs map to create, read, update and delete; a repeated PUT is harmless, a repeated POST is not.","core":true},{"t":"Status codes you will actually see","gist":"The first digit tells you whose fault it is: 2xx success, 4xx your request, 5xx the server. 401 means not authenticated, 403 means authenticated but not allowed, and 429 means slow down.","core":true},{"t":"Authentication","gist":"Basic auth is encoded, not encrypted, so use it only over TLS. API keys are long secrets sent in a header; bearer tokens are short-lived, from a login call; OAuth 2.0 grants scoped access without sharing a password.","core":true},{"t":"Tools: curl, Postman, API docs","gist":"curl is the universal command-line probe, Postman saves the same requests in a GUI, and reading API docs means finding the base URL, authentication, resources, parameters and errors. Never use -k against production.","core":false},{"t":"Rate limits and pagination","gist":"APIs cap how fast you may call; Meraki allows ten calls per second per organization and answers 429 with a Retry-After header. Large answers come in pages. Use the widest endpoint: 3 org-wide calls can replace 600 per-network ones.","core":true},{"t":"The requests library and a worked Meraki inventory","gist":"A short Python script pulls every Meraki device using a Session, timeouts, raise_for_status, the Link header for the next page and a wait on 429. Understanding this raw version lets you debug Cisco's official library.","core":true},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"Where this shows up on CCNAAUTO: the APIs domain, the largest at 20 percent, plus Meraki and Catalyst Center in the Cisco Platforms domain.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"GET reads, POST creates and is not idempotent, PUT replaces and is safe to retry, PATCH modifies part, DELETE removes.","part":0},{"p":"401 means not authenticated (bad or missing credentials); 403 means authenticated but not authorized for this resource.","part":1},{"p":"Catalyst Center: POST basic credentials to /dna/system/api/v1/auth/token, then send the token in X-Auth-Token; it expires after about an hour.","part":2},{"p":"On a 429, wait the Retry-After seconds; otherwise back off exponentially (1, 2, 4, 8 seconds) with jitter.","part":4},{"p":"Meraki allows ten calls per second per organization; one org-wide devices call at perPage=1000 beats 600 per-network calls.","part":4}]},"p28m4":{"big":"A data model gives every device the same typed, labeled fields, so code reads and changes structured data directly instead of parsing screen text that can silently break.","why":"Models replace fragile parsing with contracts and transactions, and streaming telemetry is how modern networks are watched; both are explicit exam objectives.","parts":[{"t":"Why models beat screen scraping","gist":"A parsing template bets the screen format never changes, and when it does you silently get an empty list. A data model is a contract naming every field, its type and path, and it makes all-or-nothing transactions possible.","core":true},{"t":"YANG","gist":"YANG is the schema language. A container groups nodes, a list holds entries found by a key, a leaf is one typed value, and rw marks configuration while ro marks state. IETF, OpenConfig and vendor-native families exist.","core":true},{"t":"NETCONF","gist":"NETCONF sends XML requests over SSH on port 830. Operations include get-config and edit-config, datastores include running and candidate, and lock and commit make changes transactional. ncclient is the Python client.","core":true},{"t":"RESTCONF","gist":"RESTCONF exposes the same YANG data over HTTPS as JSON or XML, with the YANG path as the URL after /restconf/data/. It is simpler than NETCONF but has no lock or commit, so use it for reads and small edits.","core":true},{"t":"gNMI, gRPC and streaming telemetry","gist":"gNMI runs over gRPC with binary encoding and has four calls: Capabilities, Get, Set and Subscribe. With Subscribe the device pushes data on a timer or on change, far finer than SNMP's five-minute polling, at a storage cost.","core":true},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"Where model-driven programmability shows up on CCNAAUTO, including reading a RESTCONF or NETCONF reply and recognizing NETCONF's port 830.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"In a YANG tree, rw is configuration, ro (config false) is operational state, and interface* [name] is a list keyed by name.","part":1},{"p":"NETCONF is XML RPCs over SSH on TCP 830, with running, startup and candidate datastores; it is transactional.","part":2},{"p":"RESTCONF URLs follow /restconf/data/module:container/list=key with Accept: application/yang-data+json; RESTCONF has no lock, candidate or commit.","part":3},{"p":"gNMI's four RPCs are Capabilities, Get, Set and Subscribe; IANA assigned it port 9339, though vendor defaults vary.","part":4},{"p":"For 9,600 interfaces × 8 counters, SNMP every five minutes gives 256 values per second; gNMI every 10 s gives 7,680, thirty times the resolution.","part":4}]},"p28m5":{"big":"Ansible lets you declare what the network should look like and changes only what is missing, so a second run reporting changed=0 proves the first one worked.","why":"Ansible is the industry's default network automation tool and an explicit exam topic, and the two-run idempotency test is the evidence change managers trust.","parts":[{"t":"How network Ansible differs","gist":"Switches can't run Ansible's Python, so network modules run on your control machine and connect over SSH (network_cli), NETCONF or a REST API. Vendor logic comes in collections like cisco.ios. Nothing is installed on the device.","core":true},{"t":"Inventory and variables","gist":"The inventory lists hosts and groups, and group_vars and host_vars hold their variables, with host values overriding group ones. ansible_network_os says which platform each group is, and secrets live in vault-encrypted files.","core":true},{"t":"Playbooks and resource modules","gist":"A playbook runs tasks in order against hosts. Resource modules take data plus a state: merged adds or corrects, replaced matches listed items, overridden deletes everything not listed. Run --check --diff first; changed=3 then changed=0 proves idempotency.","core":true},{"t":"Jinja2 templates","gist":"For features without a resource module, render CLI from a Jinja2 template fed by variables: {{ }} prints, for loops repeat, if branches, and filters transform. One template gives each device its own correct config, and it can be unit-tested.","core":true},{"t":"Roles, AWX and the Automation Platform","gist":"Roles package reusable tasks, templates and variables. AWX adds a web UI, role-based access, scheduling and an audit log, and is the upstream of Red Hat's Automation Platform; check AWX's current status before relying on it.","core":false},{"t":"Nornir: the Python alternative","gist":"Nornir is Ansible's idea in pure Python: a YAML inventory, tasks as ordinary functions and a threaded runner. Pick it for Python's debugger and speed; pick Ansible when colleagues must run it without learning Python.","core":false},{"t":"Terraform for cloud networking","gist":"Terraform is declarative with a state file: describe resources, plan shows the diff, apply makes it so. Ansible is procedural and strong on devices; Terraform tracks what it created and is strong on cloud. Teams use both.","core":true},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"Where this shows up on CCNAAUTO: describing Ansible, Terraform and Cisco NSO, reading a playbook, and agent-based versus agentless tools.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Network modules run on the control node, not the switch; network_cli means SSH to the device's CLI.","part":0},{"p":"Host variables override group variables; set ansible_network_os per group, such as cisco.ios.ios or arista.eos.eos.","part":1},{"p":"Resource module states: merged adds or corrects, replaced matches the listed items, overridden makes the whole table match and deletes the rest.","part":2},{"p":"Run --check --diff first; a second real run that reports changed=0 proves idempotency.","part":2},{"p":"Ansible is procedural with no state file and strong on devices; Terraform is declarative with a state file and strong on cloud.","part":6}]},"p28m6":{"big":"A NetDevOps pipeline makes every network change a reviewed pull request that machines lint, test, analyze and prove in a lab before it reaches production.","why":"This turns automation from a risky power tool into an engineering discipline, and it is the workflow modern network teams hire for.","parts":[{"t":"Git for network configuration","gist":"Everything that defines the network, except secrets, lives in Git. main is protected, every change is a branch and pull request, and the diff of rendered configs becomes the change record a reviewer can read.","core":true},{"t":"Continuous integration","gist":"CI runs on every pull request and fails fast: lint, unit tests that render templates, a full render of every device's config, Batfish analysis, then lab tests. Only then does a human approve and Ansible push.","core":true},{"t":"Batfish: analysis without devices","gist":"Batfish reads a folder of config files and models the whole network, with no devices. It finds parse errors, undefined references like a missing ACL, sessions that will never come up, and what a change would break or newly permit.","core":true},{"t":"pyATS and Genie: testing live state","gist":"pyATS and Genie capture structured state from live devices before and after a change, and genie diff shows exactly what moved. Batfish asks what the configuration would do; pyATS asks what the network is doing.","core":true},{"t":"Lab validation: CML and containerlab","gist":"Test changes in a lab first. CML runs real Cisco images, with a free five-node tier; containerlab builds a network of containers from a YAML file in seconds and can run inside CI. Packet Tracer can't be scripted.","core":true},{"t":"Change management, integrated","gist":"A change that passed the pipeline and peer review counts as an ITIL standard change: pre-approved and low risk. The pipeline can open and close the ticket itself, rollback is planned ahead, and you deploy to a canary first.","core":true},{"t":"The NetDevOps pipeline, end to end","gist":"The whole flow in order: edit data, pull request, lint, tests, render, Batfish, lab tests, approval, canary deploy, full deploy, post-checks and telemetry, then a closed ticket. About six minutes of machine time per change.","core":true},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"Where this shows up on CCNAAUTO: Git operations, reading a unified diff, infrastructure as code and CI/CD concepts, and reading a Dockerfile.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"A secret that ever reached Git history is considered leaked and must be rotated.","part":0},{"p":"CI stage order: lint, unit tests, render, Batfish analysis, lab deployment and tests, then human approval and push.","part":1},{"p":"Batfish's undefinedReferences catches an ACL or prefix-list that is applied but never defined.","part":2},{"p":"Batfish analyzes configuration files offline; pyATS tests live devices. One predicts, the other verifies.","part":3},{"p":"A pipelined, peer-reviewed change qualifies as an ITIL standard change: pre-approved, repeatable, low risk.","part":5}]},"p28m7":{"big":"Automation holds credentials that can reconfigure the whole network at machine speed, so keep secrets out of code, split read from write accounts, and log everything it does.","why":"Credentials at machine speed are the biggest risk in automation. Handling them well is what makes you trustworthy, and the CCNAAUTO exam makes the skill visible to employers.","parts":[{"t":"API keys and secrets","gist":"Treat an API key like a password: never in code, Git history, logs or chat. Store it in a git-ignored .env, Ansible Vault, a secrets manager or CI secrets. If one leaks, revoke it first, then investigate and rotate.","core":true},{"t":"Least privilege for automation accounts","gist":"Scripts get their own accounts, split by job: a read-only account for audits and telemetry, a write account used only by the pipeline's deploy stage, and a monitored emergency human account. Restrict where keys work, and keep TLS verification on.","core":true},{"t":"Logging and telemetry pipelines","gist":"Keep two streams, what automation did and what the network did, in one observability stack. Then 'pipeline deployed change at 02:14:07' lines up with 'OSPF neighbor down at 02:14:09', and you see the cause in seconds.","core":true},{"t":"The exam: 200-901 CCNAAUTO","gist":"DevNet Associate was renamed CCNA Automation on February 3, 2026. The exam, 200-901 CCNAAUTO v1.1, is 120 minutes, US$300, with six domains, and it asks you to read and interpret code and replies, not write them.","core":true},{"t":"The DevNet sandbox","gist":"Cisco's free sandbox runs every lab against real Cisco software: shared always-on boxes with limited rights, or private reservations over VPN for up to seven days. Check each sandbox's card for current credentials.","core":false},{"t":"A ten-week study plan","gist":"A week-by-week plan that pairs each module with a hands-on lab and ends with practice exams and booking the test.","core":false},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"Application Deployment and Security and Network Fundamentals are each 15 percent. Score practice exams by domain and give your remaining weeks to the two weakest.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"A key committed and then deleted is still in Git history and every clone, so it must be rotated.","part":0},{"p":"Split read from write: a read-only svc-netaudit for audits, a svc-netdeploy used only by the deploy stage, and named human accounts with MFA.","part":1},{"p":"verify=False and curl -k are lab-only habits; the key in your header is exactly what an interceptor wants.","part":1},{"p":"Plain syslog uses UDP 514; syslog over TLS uses TCP 6514, so configuration lines in logs don't cross the network in clear.","part":2},{"p":"200-901 CCNAAUTO v1.1: 120 minutes, US$300; APIs and Infrastructure and Automation are 20% each, the other four domains 15%.","part":3}]},"p29m1":{"big":"A RHEL system boots through a chain, firmware, GRUB2, kernel and initramfs, then systemd, and knowing each link lets you repair it, including resetting a lost root password.","why":"The exam's first task and the job's worst day are both 'the system will not let me in'. Knowing the boot chain and the rd.break drill makes both routine.","parts":[{"t":"The ecosystem: Fedora → CentOS Stream → RHEL → the rebuilds","gist":"RHEL is built as a pipeline: new technology lands in Fedora, previews in CentOS Stream, then ships in RHEL with a ten-year lifecycle. Rocky Linux and AlmaLinux are free rebuilds; RHEL 10 and Rocky 10 need an x86-64-v3 CPU.","core":true},{"t":"Subscriptions and the no-cost options","gist":"RHEL is entitled by a subscription, not a license key: you pay for support and content delivery. subscription-manager register connects a system, and the free Developer Subscription covers up to 16 systems. Rocky and Alma need no registration.","core":false},{"t":"Packages: rpm, dnf, repositories and Flatpak","gist":"dnf installs signed RPM packages and resolves dependencies across repositories like BaseOS and AppStream, defined in /etc/yum.repos.d/. rpm queries and verifies what's installed. Keep gpgcheck=1. Flatpak handles sandboxed desktop apps.","core":true},{"t":"The boot chain, link by link","gist":"Boot is a relay: UEFI runs shim, which hands to GRUB2; GRUB loads the kernel and initramfs; the initramfs finds and mounts the real root, then systemd starts the default target.","core":true},{"t":"Targets, rescue and emergency","gist":"A systemd target is a named set of units, like multi-user or graphical. The rescue and emergency targets help repair a system, but both ask for the root password, so neither helps if that is what you lost.","core":true},{"t":"Resetting a lost root password: the drill","gist":"Add rd.break in GRUB to stop in the initramfs before any login. Remount /sysroot read-write, chroot into it, run passwd, then touch /.autorelabel so SELinux relabels the new shadow file, and exit twice.","core":true},{"t":"journald: where the evidence lives","gist":"journalctl reads the system journal: -b for this boot, -b -1 for the previous one, -u for one unit. By default the journal vanishes at reboot; creating /var/log/journal makes it persistent.","core":true},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"Which EX200 objectives this module covers, from managing software to interrupting the boot and preserving journals, plus the matching LFCS domain.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"rpm -qf names the package that owns a file; rpm -V shows which of a package's files changed since install.","part":2},{"p":"Boot chain: UEFI → shim → GRUB2 → kernel + initramfs → systemd → default target.","part":3},{"p":"Lost root password: rd.break, mount -o remount,rw /sysroot, chroot /sysroot, passwd root, touch /.autorelabel, exit twice.","part":5},{"p":"Skip /.autorelabel and login fails even with the right password, because the new /etc/shadow has no SELinux label.","part":5},{"p":"Make the journal persistent by creating /var/log/journal (or setting Storage=persistent) and restarting systemd-journald.","part":6}]},"p29m2":{"big":"systemd manages every RHEL service through unit files that say what starts, in what order, on what schedule, within what resource budget, and where it logs.","why":"Every RHEL service, container and timer is a systemd unit. Mastering units, journals and limits separates a user who restarts things from an administrator who understands them.","parts":[{"t":"Units and where they live","gist":"A unit file describes something systemd manages: a service, socket, timer, target and more. Vendor files live in /usr/lib/systemd/system, yours in /etc/systemd/system, which always wins. Run systemctl daemon-reload after any edit.","core":true},{"t":"Dependencies: requirement versus order","gist":"Wants= and Requires= say what must also start; After= and Before= say in what order. They are separate ideas, so a service that needs a database needs both Wants= and After= for it.","core":true},{"t":"Writing your own service","gist":"A service unit has [Unit], [Service] and [Install] sections. Pick the right Type, such as simple or oneshot, add Restart=on-failure for self-healing, and use sandboxing lines like ProtectSystem=strict for free security.","core":false},{"t":"Drop-ins: change a vendor unit without touching it","gist":"Never edit vendor unit files; package updates overwrite them. systemctl edit creates a drop-in override under /etc. To replace a list setting like ExecStart=, write an empty ExecStart= line first, then the new one.","core":true},{"t":"Timers versus cron","gist":"A systemd timer pairs a .timer saying when with a .service saying what. It can catch up missed runs, use limits and log failures you can alert on. Cron is still on the exam and fine for simple one-liners.","core":true},{"t":"journalctl mastery","gist":"The journal is a database, so filter instead of grepping: by unit with -u, by boot with -b, by priority with -p, by time with --since, or by fields like _PID. Keep it persistent and cap its size.","core":true},{"t":"Resource control: cgroups v2","gist":"Every service runs in its own control group, so you can cap it: MemoryMax, CPUQuota and TasksMax set limits, and CPUWeight shares a busy CPU. A leaking service then dies alone instead of taking sshd with it.","core":true},{"t":"TuneD profiles and time with chrony","gist":"TuneD applies tuning profiles, such as virtual-guest on a VM. Correct time is a security control, because Kerberos, TLS and log correlation depend on it. chrony keeps time, and chronyc sources and timedatectl confirm sync.","core":true},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"Which EX200 objectives this covers: services, process scheduling, tuning profiles, logs and journals, scheduled tasks and time service clients.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Requires= and Wants= pull a unit in; After= and Before= only order it. After= alone never starts the other service.","part":1},{"p":"In a drop-in, clear a list setting with an empty ExecStart= line before giving the new command.","part":3},{"p":"OnCalendar=*-*-* 02:30:00 with Persistent=true runs nightly and catches up a missed run; check it with systemctl list-timers.","part":4},{"p":"CPUQuota=50% is half of one CPU; MemoryMax= is the hard line where the OOM killer acts inside that group only.","part":6},{"p":"Kerberos rejects tickets more than five minutes off; in chronyc sources, ^* marks the time source in use.","part":7}]},"p29m3":{"big":"Security on RHEL answers who may do what, and how you prove it afterwards, through layered controls: accounts, sudo, permissions, SELinux, firewalld, SSH and auditd.","why":"These are the controls a penetration tester is later paid to test. Configuring them correctly is the heaviest part of the exam and the daily work of a Linux administrator.","parts":[{"t":"Accounts, groups and password aging","gist":"/etc/passwd holds seven fields per account and /etc/shadow nine, including the hash and aging dates. Manage users with useradd and usermod (always -aG to add groups), and set password aging with chage.","core":true},{"t":"PAM and authselect","gist":"Every login passes through PAM, a stack of modules for passwords, complexity and lockout. On RHEL, authselect generates those files from a profile, so turn on features like faillock through authselect; hand edits get overwritten.","core":false},{"t":"sudo: policy, not a password","gist":"Members of the wheel group get full sudo with their own password. Write narrower rules in /etc/sudoers.d/ using visudo, which checks syntax. Every sudo command is tied to a named person, which a shared root password destroys.","core":true},{"t":"umask, special bits and ACLs","gist":"New files start at 666 and directories at 777, minus the umask, so 027 gives 640 and 750. Setgid on a directory makes new files inherit its group. ACLs add named users or groups for exceptions.","core":true},{"t":"SELinux, properly","gist":"SELinux labels every process and file with a type and allows only what policy says, like httpd_t reading httpd_sys_content_t. Fix labels with semanage fcontext plus restorecon, label ports with semanage port, flip Booleans with setsebool -P.","core":true},{"t":"firewalld: zones, services, rich rules","gist":"firewalld groups interfaces and sources into zones and opens services or ports per zone. Use --permanent and then --reload, or the rule won't survive a reboot or won't apply yet. Rich rules add source filters and logging.","core":true},{"t":"SSH hardening and auditd","gist":"Use ed25519 keys, then put server policy in a drop-in like 01-hardening.conf, since sshd keeps the first value it reads. Test with sshd -t before restarting. auditd watches privileged files and keeps a tamper-evident record.","core":true},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"Which exam objectives this covers. It is the heaviest part of the exam, and almost every task ends with a SELinux and firewall step.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"usermod -G without -a replaces every supplementary group; use usermod -aG to add one.","part":0},{"p":"umask math: 666 − 027 = 640 for files, 777 − 027 = 750 for directories.","part":3},{"p":"Fix SELinux contexts with semanage fcontext -a, then restorecon -Rv; chcon is undone by the next relabel. mv keeps old labels, cp creates fresh ones.","part":4},{"p":"SELinux troubleshooting: ausearch -m AVC,USER_AVC -ts recent, then sealert; audit2allow is the last resort.","part":4},{"p":"firewall-cmd without --permanent vanishes at reboot; without --reload a permanent rule isn't active yet. A web server on 8080 needs the port opened and labeled.","part":5}]},"p29m4":{"big":"RHEL storage is a stack, partition, physical volume, volume group, logical volume, filesystem, mount, and LVM lets you add space to a running server without downtime.","why":"Adding space without downtime and surviving a reboot are what every exam and on-call rotation tests, and a bad fstab line is the most common way to make a server unbootable.","parts":[{"t":"Devices and partitions","gist":"lsblk -f maps every disk, partition, filesystem and UUID. Use GPT for new disks: it holds 128 partitions and reaches far past MBR's 2 TiB limit. Start partitions at 1 MiB so they align with modern disks' sectors.","core":true},{"t":"Filesystems: XFS and ext4","gist":"XFS, RHEL's default, grows online but never shrinks; ext4 can shrink offline. Mount persistently in /etc/fstab by UUID, never /dev/sdb1, because device names can shift. Then run daemon-reload and mount -a to catch typos before a reboot.","core":true},{"t":"LVM: the layer that makes resizing boring","gist":"LVM pools disks (physical volumes) into a volume group cut into 4 MiB extents, then carves logical volumes from it. lvextend -r grows a volume and its filesystem live; without -r, only the volume grows.","core":true},{"t":"Swap","gist":"Swap is disk standing in for memory: slower, but it keeps the OOM killer away. Create it on a logical volume with mkswap and swapon, persist it in fstab, and size it by Red Hat's table.","core":true},{"t":"Stratis and VDO, briefly","gist":"Stratis manages thin-provisioned XFS pools, filesystems and snapshots with one command each. VDO adds deduplication and compression as an LVM type, which pays off on VM images and backups but not on already-compressed media.","core":false},{"t":"NFS and autofs","gist":"Mount an NFS export directly, or persist it in fstab with _netdev. autofs mounts shares on demand and unmounts them after idle, so a dead server at boot doesn't hang the client. Maps say what mounts where.","core":true},{"t":"Quotas and LUKS","gist":"Quotas cap each user's storage: the soft limit warns, the hard limit refuses writes. LUKS encrypts a block device so a lost disk is unreadable, unlocked at boot through /etc/crypttab. Back up the header before touching key slots.","core":false},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"Which EX200 objectives this covers: GPT partitions, LVM, swap, mounting by UUID or label, filesystems, NFS, autofs and extending logical volumes.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"GPT holds 128 partitions and is what UEFI boots from; MBR holds four primary partitions and addresses 2 TiB.","part":0},{"p":"Mount by UUID in fstab, add nofail for non-critical disks, then run systemctl daemon-reload and mount -a before any reboot.","part":1},{"p":"XFS grows online but cannot shrink at all; ext4 shrinks only offline.","part":1},{"p":"Extent math: a 20 GiB volume group with 4 MiB extents holds 5,120 extents, and a 2 GiB volume uses 512 of them.","part":2},{"p":"lvextend -r -L +1G grows the volume and the filesystem live; without -r the filesystem stays the same size.","part":2}]},"p29m5":{"big":"A RHEL server needs an address that survives reboots, a way to find names and a properly published service, and when something fails you check from the bottom up.","why":"Most 'the server is down' tickets are one rung of the troubleshooting ladder, and the exam's networking, web server and firewall tasks are the ladder's top rungs.","parts":[{"t":"NetworkManager and nmcli","gist":"NetworkManager is RHEL's only network stack, managed with nmcli. A device is the hardware; a connection is a profile applied to it. nmcli con mod writes the profile but changes nothing live until nmcli con up.","core":true},{"t":"Names: hostname, hosts, DNS","gist":"hostnamectl sets the static hostname. Lookups check /etc/hosts first, then DNS, as nsswitch.conf says. NetworkManager generates resolv.conf, so set DNS on the connection, not in that file. getent hosts shows what programs see.","core":true},{"t":"Bonding: two cables, one address","gist":"A bond makes two NICs act as one, for redundancy or bandwidth. Active-backup works with any switch; 802.3ad (LACP) needs the switch configured. Teaming is gone in RHEL 10, so bonding is the only option.","core":false},{"t":"Publishing a service: web server, firewall, SELinux, time","gist":"A web server on a custom port touches every control: set the listen port, label port and content for SELinux, open the port in the right firewalld zone, restart, test from another machine. Chrony servers follow the same recipe.","core":true},{"t":"Troubleshooting: the ladder","gist":"Climb from the bottom and stop at the first failing rung: link, address, route, name, socket, firewall, SELinux, service. tcpdump shows whether packets even arrive. Use these tools only on networks you own or may test.","core":true},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"Which EX200 objectives this covers: IPv4 and IPv6 addressing, name resolution, services at boot, firewall-cmd, and the web-server and time-client tasks.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"nmcli con mod only edits the profile; the change goes live when you run nmcli con up.","part":0},{"p":"Don't edit /etc/resolv.conf, because NetworkManager regenerates it; set ipv4.dns on the connection instead.","part":1},{"p":"Teaming was removed in RHEL 10. Active-backup bonding needs no switch cooperation; 802.3ad (LACP) does.","part":2},{"p":"Web server on 8080: semanage port -a -t http_port_t -p tcp 8080, plus firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=8080/tcp and --reload.","part":3},{"p":"If curl works on localhost but fails from another VM, it is the firewall or zone almost every time.","part":4}]},"p29m6":{"big":"Rootless Podman runs a service safely as an ordinary user, Quadlet makes systemd start it at boot, and Ansible turns a runbook into code you can run again.","why":"Containers and automation are what employers mean by 'modern Linux administrator', and Ansible is the direct path from RHCSA to the Red Hat Certified Engineer tier.","parts":[{"t":"Podman: daemonless, rootless","gist":"Podman runs containers without a root-owned daemon. Rootless, your UID becomes root inside the container, so an escape lands as an unprivileged user. Use fully qualified image names, and add :Z to volumes so SELinux allows access.","core":true},{"t":"Containers as systemd services: Quadlet","gist":"Write a Quadlet .container file and systemd turns it into a service at daemon-reload. Its [Install] section enables it, and loginctl enable-linger lets a user's services start at boot without a login.","core":true},{"t":"Shell scripting for administrators","gist":"Admin scripts share a frame: set -euo pipefail, check the arguments, test with [[ ]], loop, and return meaningful exit codes, 0 for success. grep, sed and awk do the text work; grep returns 1 when nothing matched.","core":true},{"t":"Ansible: the bridge to RHCE","gist":"Ansible connects over SSH from a control node, pushes small modules and runs them against an inventory. Modules are idempotent, so a second run reports changed=0. Collections add modules for firewalld, SELinux and storage.","core":true},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"Shell scripting is an EX200 objective, containers were on the RHEL 9 exam and remain a job skill, and Ansible is all of EX294, the step to RHCE.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Rootless containers map the container's root to your own UID and its other UIDs to your /etc/subuid range, so an escape is unprivileged.","part":0},{"p":"Add :Z to a bind-mounted volume so it gets container_file_t; without it SELinux denies every read and the container serves 403s.","part":0},{"p":"Without loginctl enable-linger, user services die at logout and never start at boot.","part":1},{"p":"Exit codes: 0 success, 1 general failure, 2 usage error, 126 not executable, 127 command not found, 128 plus the signal number for a kill.","part":2},{"p":"Ansible modules are idempotent: run the same play twice and the second run reports changed=0.","part":3}]},"p29m7":{"big":"Fix a sick Linux host with a method, not a mood: ask what changed, find the layer, change one thing, verify and persist, then document.","why":"Method and clock discipline turn what you know into a pass on a three-hour practical graded after a reboot, and into calm on a real outage.","parts":[{"t":"A method, not a mood","gist":"Seven moves: state the symptom exactly, check what changed, locate the layer, form one hypothesis and change one thing, verify with a command, make it persist and reboot, write it down. Snapshot before changing anything.","core":true},{"t":"Performance basics: which resource is starving?","gist":"For each resource ask about utilization, saturation and errors. Read load average against CPU count, %wa and iostat for disk, si and so for swapping, and free's available column for memory. High load with idle CPUs points at storage.","core":true},{"t":"Recovering a broken boot","gist":"Each boot failure has a known fix: repair a bad fstab line from emergency mode, boot the previous kernel from GRUB, rebuild a broken loader from the rescue ISO, and run restorecon on an unlabeled shadow file.","core":true},{"t":"The EX200 objectives mapped to this domain","gist":"A table placing each official RHEL 10 EX200 objective in this domain's modules. Containers were on the RHEL 9 list only; bonding, Stratis, VDO, LUKS, quotas, auditd and Ansible sit above the exam.","core":false},{"t":"The lab blueprint","gist":"Two VMs cover every drill: server1 with spare disks and two NICs, server2 smaller, on a host-only network. Install Rocky or Alma 10, and take snapshots before every destructive drill so you can revert in seconds.","core":false},{"t":"The exam itself: format, clock, tactics","gist":"EX200 is a three-hour hands-on exam graded on what survives a reboot; 210 of 300 passes. Read all tasks first, do foundation tasks first, verify like the grader, and keep the last twenty-five minutes to reboot and check.","core":true},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"This module is the exam itself: the method, recovery skills and time discipline behind the EX200 and LFCS operations objectives.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Check df -h and df -i: a full disk or exhausted inodes explains a startling number of 'weird' failures.","part":0},{"p":"Load average only means something next to nproc: 4 on 2 vCPUs is saturation, 4 on 16 is idle.","part":1},{"p":"lsof | grep deleted finds a log that was deleted but is still held open and still filling the disk.","part":1},{"p":"Bad fstab: give the root password, mount -o remount,rw /, fix the line or add nofail, then mount -a and systemctl daemon-reload.","part":2},{"p":"EX200: 3 hours, hands-on, 210 of 300 to pass. Credit is per task, and a perfect task lost at reboot scores nothing.","part":5}]},"p30m1":{"big":"A server is a computer built to run unattended for years, warn you before a part fails and keep running when one dies.","why":"Everything you will practice, from Windows domains to local AI, runs on hardware you choose here, and the power and cost arithmetic decides whether the lab survives your electricity bill.","parts":[{"t":"What is different about a server","gist":"A server adds what a desktop lacks: ECC memory that fixes single flipped bits, CPUs built to feed many drives and cards at once, spare hot-swap power supplies, and a BMC, a small always-on computer for remote management.","core":true},{"t":"The rack and the data center","gist":"Rack gear is measured in units of 1.75 inches, and depth matters more than people expect. Data centers use hot and cold aisles and are graded by uptime tiers, the language of service agreements.","core":false},{"t":"Power and cooling arithmetic","gist":"Three equations run a home lab: a 15 A, 120 V circuit carries 1,440 W continuously, every watt becomes heat, and idle draw sets your yearly bill. A UPS exists to shut machines down cleanly.","core":true},{"t":"Three ways to buy","gist":"Used enterprise servers are the best way to learn but loud and power-hungry, mini PCs give the most compute per watt, and a self-built box is the right NAS (network storage). Most good labs end up with one of each.","core":true},{"t":"A bill of materials for three budgets","gist":"Three sample shopping lists from about $300 to $3,000, each split into compute, storage, network and power. The cheapest tier already teaches every skill in this domain, and the prices are approximations to verify.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"ECC memory stores 72 bits for every 64 bits of data, so it can correct any single flipped bit and detect double flips.","part":0},{"p":"The BMC (iDRAC, iLO) management port lives on its own VLAN and never faces the internet.","part":0},{"p":"A 15 A, 120 V circuit carries 1,800 W but continuous load is capped at 80%, 1,440 W. Every watt becomes heat: 1 W = 3.412 BTU/h.","part":2},{"p":"Yearly energy is W × 8,760 ÷ 1,000 kWh: a machine idling at 150 W costs about $197 a year at $0.15/kWh.","part":2},{"p":"Size a UPS by its watt rating so your load is 40–70% of it; its job is riding out blips and clean shutdowns.","part":2}]},"p30m2":{"big":"A hypervisor shares one machine's CPU, memory, disk and network among many virtual machines, and knowing how they share lets you predict, size and roll back every lab.","why":"Every lab in this curriculum, from a Windows domain to a malware sandbox, is a set of virtual machines whose behavior you must predict. Get it right and you can size, isolate and roll back anything.","parts":[{"t":"Hypervisors and the hardware that makes them honest","gist":"A hypervisor splits real hardware among guest systems. Type 1 runs directly on the hardware; type 2 runs as an app on a host OS. CPU features VT-x/AMD-V and EPT/NPT make it efficient and must be enabled in BIOS.","core":true},{"t":"CPU and memory: the overcommit arithmetic","gist":"You can promise more virtual CPUs than real threads, often 3:1 or 4:1. Memory is less forgiving: ballooning and KSM (merging identical pages) help, but guest maximums plus 10–20% for the host must fit in RAM.","core":true},{"t":"Virtual networking","gist":"A VM's network card is software plugged into a Linux bridge, which acts like a simple switch. A VLAN-aware bridge tags each VM's traffic, and NAT or host-only networks keep type 2 labs isolated.","core":true},{"t":"Virtual disks","gist":"Virtual disks come in formats like raw, qcow2, VMDK and VHDX. Thin disks take real space only when written, so you must alert at 80% full, because a full thin pool pauses every VM at once.","core":true},{"t":"Snapshots, and what they cost","gist":"A snapshot freezes a disk at one moment; later writes go to a separate delta. Long chains slow reads and are risky to merge, and since it sits on the VM's disks, it is a return point, not a backup.","core":true},{"t":"Templates, cloning and the golden image","gist":"Build one clean VM, turn it into a template and clone it. Full clones stand alone; linked clones are faster but depend on the base forever. Regenerate anything meant to be unique, like SSH host keys or Windows SIDs.","core":true},{"t":"Nested virtualization, and containers vs VMs","gist":"Nested virtualization lets a VM run its own hypervisor at a 10–30% penalty. Containers share the host's kernel, so they are lighter but less isolated; for malware or a pentest range, use a VM on a host-only network.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Type 1 hypervisors run on bare hardware (ESX, Hyper-V, KVM, Xen); type 2 run as apps on a host OS (VirtualBox, Workstation).","part":0},{"p":"Memory budget: the sum of guest maximums plus 10–20% for the host must fit in physical RAM, or you are relying on the balloon.","part":1},{"p":"A thin-provisioned pool that fills up pauses every VM at once, so alert when it passes 80%.","part":3},{"p":"A snapshot lives in the same failure domain as the VM: it is a return point measured in days, not a backup.","part":4},{"p":"Containers share the host kernel, so a kernel exploit in one is an exploit of all; that is why risky samples go in VMs.","part":6}]},"p30m3":{"big":"Learn Proxmox VE deeply as your lab's hypervisor, know vSphere's vocabulary for employers, and know where Hyper-V, KVM and XCP-ng each fit.","why":"You will run Proxmox for years and be asked about vSphere in infrastructure interviews. Installing it correctly the first time means you never have to rebuild it in shame.","parts":[{"t":"Proxmox VE: the lab's hypervisor","gist":"Proxmox VE is a free type 1 platform running VMs and containers behind one web interface. Install it on a mirrored ZFS boot pool, use a VLAN-aware bridge and schedule backups. A cluster needs three votes, never two nodes alone.","core":true},{"t":"VMware vSphere: the vocabulary of the enterprise","gist":"vSphere is what most data centers still run. vCenter manages hosts and provides vMotion (live migration), HA (restart elsewhere after a failure) and DRS (load balancing). Since Broadcom's purchase, licensing is a per-core subscription.","core":true},{"t":"Hyper-V, KVM/libvirt, XCP-ng","gist":"Hyper-V comes with Windows Pro and Server and suits Windows labs. KVM with libvirt is the Linux engine under Proxmox and the clouds. XCP-ng is Xen-based and feels most like vSphere.","core":true},{"t":"Choosing for the home lab","gist":"Proxmox is the primary lab host because every hour in it is also Linux, ZFS and networking practice. Hyper-V fits Windows labs, ESXi is worth running nested for familiarity, and XCP-ng is worth installing once.","core":true},{"t":"A worked Proxmox build","gist":"The exact commands for a real install: mirrored boot pool, no-subscription repositories, VLAN-aware bridge, a cloud-init template and clones, a non-root admin with two-factor login, a firewall, and daily backups.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Proxmox's free no-subscription repository has every feature; a paid subscription buys the enterprise-tested repository and support.","part":0},{"p":"A Proxmox cluster needs quorum: three nodes, or two plus a QDevice vote, never two alone.","part":0},{"p":"Live migration needs the disk on both nodes, through shared storage or ZFS replication; otherwise the whole disk streams first and the move is slow.","part":0},{"p":"HA restarts a VM on another host after a failure, which means minutes of downtime; only Fault Tolerance approaches zero.","part":1},{"p":"Broadcom made vSphere a per-core subscription with a 16-core minimum per CPU and ended perpetual licenses.","part":1}]},"p30m4":{"big":"Storage design is arithmetic about capacity, speed and failure, and ZFS adds checksums and redundancy so the array notices and repairs bad data.","why":"Storage is where data actually lives and dies. The arithmetic lets you design an array that survives a 3 a.m. disk failure, and Server+ tests RAID and interfaces directly.","parts":[{"t":"Devices and interfaces","gist":"Hard drives win on cost per terabyte and lose on everything else. Buy CMR drives, never SMR, which collapse under rebuilds. ZFS wants an HBA, a card that passes the raw disks straight through.","core":false},{"t":"Performance: IOPS, throughput, latency","gist":"IOPS is operations per second, throughput is bytes per second, and latency is the wait for one operation. A hard drive manages roughly 80–120 random IOPS, NVMe 500,000 or more, and the network is often the real ceiling.","core":true},{"t":"RAID levels with the arithmetic","gist":"Each RAID level trades space for failures survived. RAID 5 is risky on big disks: a 12 TB rebuild on consumer drives has about a 62% chance of an unrecoverable read, so use RAID 6, RAIDZ2 or mirrors.","core":true},{"t":"ZFS from first principles","gist":"ZFS never overwrites data in place and checksums every block, so it detects bad data and repairs it from another copy. Redundancy lives in each vdev (disk group); losing one vdev loses the pool. ECC memory covers what checksums cannot.","core":true},{"t":"NAS vs SAN and the protocols","gist":"A NAS serves files (SMB for Windows and macOS, NFS for Linux); a SAN serves raw blocks the client formats itself, as iSCSI does. Run TrueNAS so ZFS owns the real disks, never on a RAID card's virtual disk.","core":true},{"t":"Capacity planning","gist":"Convert the vendor's decimal TB to the binary TiB your OS shows, keep the pool under 80% full, and buy for what you will need in year three, not today.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Throughput = IOPS × block size. 1 GbE moves about 115 MB/s, so a single hard drive can saturate gigabit.","part":1},{"p":"Usable space: RAID 5 is (n−1)×S and survives one failure, RAID 6 is (n−2)×S and survives two, RAID 10 is n×S÷2.","part":2},{"p":"RAID is availability, not backup: it does nothing against deletion, ransomware, fire or a bad controller.","part":2},{"p":"Schedule a ZFS scrub monthly: it reads every block and repairs any that fail their checksum.","part":3},{"p":"Plan in TiB and keep a ZFS pool under 80% full; 12 TB is 10.9 TiB.","part":5}]},"p30m5":{"big":"A backup counts only once you have restored it: keep three copies on two kinds of media, one offsite and one immutable, and prove the plan by testing.","why":"A tested, immutable, documented plan turns a dead disk, a wrong command or ransomware from a catastrophe into an afternoon, for your lab and for any business that hires you.","parts":[{"t":"The threat model","gist":"Data dies in six ways: hardware failure, human error, ransomware, theft or disaster, software bugs and bit rot. RAID handles only hardware failure, so each other threat needs its own defense.","core":true},{"t":"3-2-1, and the numbers behind it","gist":"Keep at least three copies, on two kinds of media or systems, one offsite. RPO is how much data you can lose, the time between backups; RTO is how long until the service is back.","core":true},{"t":"Full, incremental, differential — and forever-incremental","gist":"A full copies everything, an incremental copies changes since the last backup, and a differential copies changes since the last full. Deduplicating tools store each chunk once, so every backup restores as a complete copy.","core":true},{"t":"The tools","gist":"Proxmox Backup Server, restic and borg all make encrypted, deduplicated backups. Encrypt every offsite copy and back up the key separately, because an encrypted backup without its key cannot be restored.","core":false},{"t":"Immutability and ransomware resistance","gist":"An immutable backup cannot be changed or deleted before its retention ends, even with stolen admin credentials. The backup server should pull from production, never be domain-joined, and keep copies longer than an attacker may have been inside.","core":true},{"t":"Testing, monitoring, documentation","gist":"A backup is unproven until you restore it. Restore a VM monthly, rebuild the backup server quarterly, rehearse the full disaster yearly, alert on jobs that fail to check in, and write the runbook down.","core":true},{"t":"Two plans","gist":"Two complete example plans, one for the home lab and one for a 15-person business, each with three copies, RPO and RTO targets and a testing schedule.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"3-2-1 means three copies, on two kinds of media or systems, one offsite; 3-2-1-1-0 adds one offline or immutable copy and zero restore errors.","part":1},{"p":"RPO is the most data you accept losing, equal to the time between backups; RTO is how long until the service is back.","part":1},{"p":"An incremental copies what changed since the last backup of any kind; a differential copies what changed since the last full.","part":2},{"p":"Back up the encryption key separately, printed and stored elsewhere: an encrypted backup without its key is a very secure brick.","part":3},{"p":"A job that says 'success' proves nothing about a restore; restore one VM monthly into an isolated VLAN.","part":5}]},"p30m6":{"big":"Split the lab into VLANs with a default-deny firewall between them, and reach it from outside only through a VPN tunnel, never a forwarded port.","why":"This network lets you practice offensive skills legally and safely, keeps your family's devices out of the blast radius, and lets you reach the lab from your truck without exposing anything to the internet.","parts":[{"t":"Principles","gist":"Give each trust level its own VLAN and let the firewall decide what crosses, denying by default. Management gear gets its own network, the household network stays untouched, and nothing from the internet is forwarded in.","core":true},{"t":"The reference topology","gist":"Six VLANs, from management to a sealed malware network, each with its subnet, hosts and rules. OPNsense, a free open-source firewall, runs as a Proxmox VM or a small box behind the house router.","core":true},{"t":"DNS and DHCP for the lab","gist":"OPNsense hands out addresses on each VLAN with DHCP and answers names with Unbound, its DNS resolver. Use lab.home.arpa as the local domain, give every server a static lease, and name everything.","core":true},{"t":"Remote access without port forwards","gist":"Never forward RDP, SSH or a web UI to the internet. Use WireGuard, a modern VPN that needs one UDP port, or Tailscale, a WireGuard-based mesh that needs no inbound ports at all.","core":true},{"t":"Isolating the range and the malware lab","gist":"The range may reach the internet but no private network, and everything in it is yours, which is what makes attacking it legal. The malware network gets no gateway, no shared folders and a snapshot before every sample.","core":true},{"t":"Monitoring and visibility","gist":"Tools that show what the lab is doing: Uptime Kuma checks that services answer and alerts your phone, Prometheus and Grafana chart resources, and NetBox records every IP, VLAN, device and cable.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"The management plane (Proxmox UI, switch, BMCs, backup server) lives on a VLAN that only your admin workstation and the VPN can reach.","part":0},{"p":"The house keeps its existing router and the lab firewall sits behind it, so a lab failure cannot take the household network down.","part":0},{"p":"WireGuard opens one UDP port, 51820 by default; Tailscale as a subnet router reaches the lab with zero inbound ports.","part":3},{"p":"RANGE (VLAN 66) reaches the internet but no private network outside itself; MALWARE (VLAN 99) has no gateway, or only a route to INetSim.","part":4},{"p":"Unauthorized access is a crime under the CFAA regardless of intent; 'I was practicing' is not authorization.","part":4}]},"p30m7":{"big":"A lab keeps working only through operating habits: monthly patching, monitoring with thresholds, written runbooks and labels, and knowing what it really costs.","why":"Operations habits are what employers hire for and the largest part of the Server+ exam. They keep the lab working and tell you why when it does not.","parts":[{"t":"Patching","gist":"Patch everything monthly, firmware included, in a set window. Update one node first and watch it for a day, snapshot or back up before anything hard to undo, and keep a change log.","core":true},{"t":"Monitoring and capacity","gist":"For each resource, watch utilization, saturation and errors. Alert at 80% pool capacity, 90% RAM, any rising SMART count (the drive's own health data), a DEGRADED pool, a missed backup or a UPS on battery.","core":true},{"t":"Incident runbooks for the lab","gist":"A runbook is the written procedure for a known failure: trigger, impact, diagnosis commands, fix, verification and rollback. Write one for each of the lab's five known failures, and add to them after every real incident.","core":true},{"t":"Documentation and labels","gist":"Label both ends of every cable and every drive bay. Keep inventory and runbooks in a wiki with an offline copy, credentials in a password manager with MFA, and a sealed break-glass sheet in a safe.","core":true},{"t":"Cost of ownership","gist":"Total cost is hardware spread over its useful life, plus power, replacement drives, subscriptions and your time: about $830 a year for the Serious tier. For always-on workloads the lab beats cloud inside a year.","core":true},{"t":"The Server+ exam","gist":"Server+ SK0-005 has up to 90 questions in 90 minutes and needs 750 of 900 to pass. Troubleshooting is 28% of it and follows CompTIA's method word for word.","core":false},{"t":"The Genius Protocol home lab, sequenced","gist":"The build plan from Stage 0, VirtualBox on your gaming PC, to Stage 8, a hybrid cloud link. Each stage is a weekend or two, serves specific later phases, and is documented before the next begins.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Patch one node first and watch it for a day (a canary), and snapshot or back up before anything you cannot trivially undo.","part":0},{"p":"SMART reallocated, pending and uncorrectable sector counts should read zero; a rising count means order a replacement now.","part":1},{"p":"Keep an offline copy of your documentation, because the wiki itself runs on the lab.","part":3},{"p":"Total cost = hardware ÷ useful life + electricity + replacement drives + subscriptions and cloud storage + your time.","part":4},{"p":"Server+ SK0-005: up to 90 questions, 90 minutes, 750 to pass on a 100–900 scale; renew every three years with 30 CEUs.","part":5}]},"p31m1":{"big":"Every byte a web server receives was written by someone it cannot trust, so security happens wherever data crosses a trust boundary.","why":"Web applications are the largest market in penetration testing. Seeing each request the way the server should makes most of the Top 10 predictable, and written authorization is the difference between a career and a crime.","parts":[{"t":"The request is the attack surface","gist":"Nearly every part of a request is attacker-controlled: method, path, headers, cookies, body and uploaded files. Hidden fields and JavaScript checks are only suggestions, so nothing the client does is a security control.","core":true},{"t":"Trust boundaries","gist":"A trust boundary is where data moves from a less trusted zone to a more trusted one. There you validate input on entry, encode it on exit, and re-check authorization on every request from server-held state.","core":true},{"t":"The OWASP Top 10:2025, by root cause","gist":"The OWASP Top 10:2025 is an awareness list, not a checklist. Learn each category by its cause. Broken Access Control is number one and now includes SSRF, where the server fetches an attacker-chosen URL.","core":true},{"t":"Threat modeling with STRIDE","gist":"A threat model asks what you are building, what can go wrong, what you will do about it and whether you did a good job. STRIDE names six kinds of threat to check at every element and boundary.","core":true},{"t":"A scanner is not a tester","gist":"Scanners find patterns like reflected input or missing headers, but they cannot know that user A should not see user B's invoice. Use a scanner as a fast, literal intern; the tester supplies the intent.","core":false},{"t":"Bug bounties, authorization and the law","gist":"Authorization is a document: written rules of engagement or a bug bounty's published scope. The CFAA makes unauthorized access a federal crime, so without permission for this exact asset and this exact test, send no request.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"The client is hostile by definition; the server is the only place a security decision can be enforced.","part":1},{"p":"Validate on entry, encode on exit, and authorize every request from server-held state, never from client-supplied claims.","part":1},{"p":"A01 Broken Access Control is number one in the OWASP Top 10:2025, and SSRF is now folded into it.","part":2},{"p":"STRIDE stands for Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Information disclosure, Denial of service and Elevation of privilege.","part":3},{"p":"A bug bounty program authorizes only its listed assets and permitted test types; anything else is unauthorized access.","part":5}]},"p31m2":{"big":"Injection is one bug in many costumes: untrusted data reaches an interpreter as code because it was glued into a string, and the fix keeps code and data apart.","why":"These are the hands-on vulnerability classes the BSCP, eWPT and OSWA exams test, and knowing the shared root cause lets you predict and fix them in any language, even one you have never used.","parts":[{"t":"SQL injection","gist":"Gluing user input into a SQL string lets the attacker write part of your query. A parameterized query sends the SQL first and the values separately, as data only. Table and column names need an allowlist instead.","core":true},{"t":"Command injection","gist":"Handing user input to a shell lets characters like ; or | run extra commands. Call the program directly with an argument list and no shell, then validate the input against what it must be.","core":true},{"t":"Cross-site scripting","gist":"XSS (cross-site scripting) makes a victim's browser run attacker script as them. It comes reflected, stored or DOM-based; the fix is encoding output for its exact context, with a Content Security Policy as a second wall.","core":true},{"t":"Template injection","gist":"When user input becomes the template itself rather than a value inside it, the user can write template syntax that reaches the host language. Pass user input only as values into a fixed template.","core":false},{"t":"Deserialization","gist":"In many formats the incoming bytes decide which classes are built and which methods run, so deserializing untrusted data is code execution by design. Use a data-only format like JSON with schema validation.","core":true},{"t":"Path traversal and file upload","gist":"A filename like ../../etc/passwd escapes the intended folder, so map IDs to stored files instead of accepting names. For uploads, check the real content, rename, store outside the web root and serve as an attachment.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Parameterized queries fix SQL injection structurally: values are bound as data and never parsed as syntax. Escaping quotes is a fragile filter.","part":0},{"p":"Table and column names and ORDER BY direction cannot be parameters, so map user choices through an allowlist.","part":0},{"p":"Reflected XSS echoes request data, stored XSS shows saved data to other users later, and DOM-based XSS happens in client-side JavaScript alone.","part":2},{"p":"Python's pickle.loads on untrusted input is remote code execution by documented design.","part":4},{"p":"A file's extension and Content-Type are client-controlled; validate uploads by magic bytes and a real decode.","part":5}]},"p31m3":{"big":"Authentication proves who you are, sessions remember it, and access control decides what you may touch: three different mechanisms, each enforced on the server.","why":"Half the BSCP exam is exactly this: stage one is getting into an account and stage two is reaching the admin area, through authentication, session and access-control flaws.","parts":[{"t":"Password storage","gist":"Never store or encrypt passwords; hash them with a slow, salted function like Argon2id, scrypt or bcrypt. The slowness is the point: it makes a stolen database very expensive to crack.","core":true},{"t":"Multi-factor authentication","gist":"MFA adds a second kind of proof. Passkeys are unphishable because they are tied to the site's origin, authenticator-app codes are strong, and SMS is weakest. MFA does not help once a session token is stolen.","core":false},{"t":"Sessions and cookies","gist":"After login the server hands out a random session ID in a cookie. Set Secure, HttpOnly and SameSite on it, issue a fresh ID at login, and add anti-CSRF tokens so other sites cannot forge requests for the user.","core":true},{"t":"JWT pitfalls","gist":"A JWT is a signed token carrying claims. Classic mistakes are accepting alg none, letting the token pick its algorithm, weak secrets, and assuming it can be revoked. Pin the algorithm on the server and keep expiry short.","core":true},{"t":"OAuth 2.0 and OIDC","gist":"OAuth 2.0 lets an app act on your behalf; OIDC adds proof of who you are. Use the authorization code flow with PKCE, match redirect URIs exactly, validate the state parameter, and never use the implicit flow.","core":true},{"t":"Broken access control and IDOR","gist":"An IDOR is the server returning an object by its ID without checking that it belongs to you. The fix is a server-side ownership check on every request, deny by default, written once so no endpoint can forget it.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Store passwords with a slow salted hash (Argon2id, scrypt or bcrypt); a per-user random salt makes rainbow tables useless.","part":0},{"p":"Session cookies need Secure, HttpOnly and SameSite flags, and a fresh session ID at login defeats fixation.","part":2},{"p":"A stateless JWT stays valid until it expires; instant logout needs a server-side denylist or short-lived access tokens with refresh.","part":3},{"p":"The OAuth rule of thumb: authorization code + PKCE + exact redirect matching + a validated state.","part":4},{"p":"Fix an IDOR with a server-side ownership check such as AND owner_id = ?; hidden or random IDs are not the control.","part":5}]},"p31m4":{"big":"Web testing centers on an intercepting proxy that pauses every request so you can read and change it, used only on targets you are authorized to test.","why":"The free PortSwigger Academy and Burp are the exact tools of the day job and of the BSCP exam, so learning the toolkit and earning the certification are the same activity.","parts":[{"t":"Burp Suite","gist":"Burp Suite is the standard intercepting proxy. Proxy shows traffic, Repeater resends one edited request (where most real testing happens), Intruder automates inputs, and the Pro-only Scanner flags leads. Its CA certificate goes only on your own machine.","core":true},{"t":"Browser dev tools and ZAP","gist":"Browser dev tools show every request, the cookies and their flags, and the page's own JavaScript, which helps trace DOM-XSS. OWASP ZAP is a free, open-source proxy and scanner that does the same jobs as Burp.","core":true},{"t":"Recon of your own scope","gist":"Before testing, map what exists, within scope only: robots.txt, sitemaps, the site map your proxy builds, and the app's own JavaScript, which often reveals endpoints and parameters. Automate only where your authorization allows it.","core":true},{"t":"API testing: REST and GraphQL","gist":"Most targets are APIs. For REST, check ownership on every object and look for fields the client should not be able to set. For GraphQL, check introspection, query depth limits and per-field authorization.","core":true},{"t":"How the BSCP exam works","gist":"The BSCP is a four-hour practical exam: two vulnerable apps, three stages each, and you must capture all six flags to pass. It requires Burp Professional and costs US$99 per attempt.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Repeater, resending one request with small edits, is where most real web testing happens.","part":0},{"p":"Burp Community is free; Professional adds the Scanner, full-speed Intruder and project files, and is required for the BSCP exam.","part":0},{"p":"Installing Burp's CA certificate only makes your own machine trust your own proxy so you can read your own HTTPS traffic.","part":0},{"p":"Enabled GraphQL introspection hands you the entire schema, and unlimited query depth is a denial-of-service risk.","part":3},{"p":"BSCP stages for each app: access any user account, reach the admin interface, then read /home/carlos/secret.","part":4}]},"p31m5":{"big":"Today's attack surface is the API, the cloud it runs in and the supply chain that built it, and each layer needs explicit server-side checks and allowlists.","why":"APIs and the supply chain are the fastest-growing part of the AppSec engineer's job, and they connect straight to the pipeline and SBOM work of Domains 19 and 20.","parts":[{"t":"The OWASP API Security Top 10","gist":"APIs have their own OWASP Top 10, led by authorization problems. BOLA is IDOR for APIs, and BFLA is a normal user calling an admin function. Authorize every object and function on the server, deny by default.","core":true},{"t":"Mass assignment","gist":"Mass assignment is a framework copying every field the client sent onto an object, so a user can quietly set role to admin. The fix is an allowlist of exactly which fields each role may set.","core":true},{"t":"SSRF and cloud metadata (conceptual)","gist":"SSRF is the server fetching a URL the attacker chose, pointed inward at internal services or the cloud metadata endpoint, which can return credentials. Allowlist destinations, block private ranges, require IMDSv2 and segment the network.","core":true},{"t":"CORS","gist":"CORS headers tell the browser which other sites may read your responses. Allow a short list of exact trusted origins, and never reflect the request's Origin back while allowing credentials.","core":true},{"t":"The software supply chain","gist":"Most of your code is other people's. Scan dependencies continuously, keep an SBOM (a list of every component and version), keep secrets out of Git, and give pipelines least-privilege, short-lived tokens.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"BOLA (API1) is IDOR for APIs: the endpoint returns an object without checking the caller owns it. It is the most common API bug.","part":0},{"p":"Fix mass assignment with an explicit allowlist of fields per role; never bind whatever keys the client sent.","part":1},{"p":"The cloud metadata endpoint is 169.254.169.254; SSRF that reaches it is how a web bug becomes cloud-account compromise.","part":2},{"p":"Reflecting Origin together with Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true lets any site make and read authenticated requests.","part":3},{"p":"An SBOM lets you answer 'are we affected?' in minutes instead of weeks when a new CVE lands.","part":4}]},"p31m6":{"big":"A mobile app is just another untrusted client: the server must still authorize everything, while the app keeps secrets in the platform's secure store and traffic on TLS.","why":"Mobile is its own specialty with its own standards and certification. Its most common findings are insecure storage, insecure transport and backend API flaws, and you practice them in a self-contained lab you own.","parts":[{"t":"MASVS and MASTG","gist":"MASVS lists what a secure mobile app must do, grouped into families like storage, crypto and network. MASTG is the how: concrete tests for Android and iOS. Start from MASVS, then test with MASTG.","core":true},{"t":"The platform security models","gist":"Android and iOS sandbox each app so it cannot read another's data. Each has a hardware-backed secret store, the Android Keystore and the iOS Keychain, and that is where keys and credentials belong.","core":true},{"t":"Insecure storage and transport","gist":"The two most common findings are secrets saved in plain files, databases or logs, and traffic without proper TLS. Store little, keep secrets in the Keystore or Keychain, and let the platform forbid cleartext.","core":true},{"t":"Certificate pinning","gist":"Pinning means the app trusts only a specific certificate it ships with, which resists interception. Testers disable it only on their own or authorized builds. It does not make a hardcoded secret safe.","core":true},{"t":"A lawful mobile lab","gist":"Build the lab from things you own: an official emulator, a deliberately vulnerable practice app such as DIVA or InsecureBankv2, and your proxy's CA installed in that emulator. Nothing touches a device or app you do not own.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"MASVS is the requirements standard (the what); MASTG is the testing guide (the how).","part":0},{"p":"Keys, tokens and credentials belong in the Android Keystore or iOS Keychain, never in plaintext files or SharedPreferences/UserDefaults.","part":1},{"p":"Most mobile vulnerabilities live in the backend API, because a modified client will send anything; the server must authorize every request.","part":2},{"p":"Android's Network Security Config and iOS App Transport Security let an app forbid cleartext HTTP.","part":2},{"p":"Certificate pinning protects transport, not hardcoded secrets: a determined owner can still reverse the binary.","part":3}]},"p31m7":{"big":"The deliverable is a report, not an exploit: each finding must let a busy developer reproduce the problem and fix it.","why":"A report a developer can act on, plus a lab portfolio and the BSCP, is what turns this skill into a paid, employer-recognized profession.","parts":[{"t":"Writing a finding","gist":"A finding stands on its own: title, CVSS score, affected asset, evidence, numbered reproduction steps, business impact and a specific fix. Prove the bug with one record or a redacted screenshot; never pull the whole table.","core":true},{"t":"CVSS, severity and business risk","gist":"CVSS gives a repeatable 0–10 technical severity score. Business risk is that severity in context: how exposed the system is, how likely the bug is to be exploited, and the value of what sits behind it.","core":true},{"t":"Working with developers","gist":"Security succeeds when developers fix things. Secure code review finds what black-box tests miss because the code states intent, and a security champions program puts a trained developer on each team.","core":true},{"t":"DAST, SAST and IAST in the lifecycle","gist":"SAST reads source code on every commit, DAST tests the running app on deploy, and IAST watches data flow during QA tests. None of them catches logic or authorization flaws, so human review and pentests stay necessary.","core":true},{"t":"Two careers, and the certifications","gist":"The skill forks into the pentester, who finds and proves bugs in time-boxed engagements, and the AppSec engineer, who builds the program that prevents them. Start with the free PortSwigger Academy and the BSCP, then eWPT or OSWA.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Numbered reproduction steps a developer can follow on a test instance are the field that gets bugs fixed.","part":0},{"p":"Prove, do not exfiltrate: one record or a redacted screenshot demonstrates the bug, and dumping data can breach your rules of engagement.","part":0},{"p":"CVSS bands: 0.1–3.9 Low, 4.0–6.9 Medium, 7.0–8.9 High, 9.0–10.0 Critical.","part":1},{"p":"Priority is business risk, not the score: a CVSS 6.5 IDOR on billing records can outrank a 9.8 on a demo with no real data.","part":1},{"p":"SAST runs on every commit, DAST against a deployed build, IAST during QA; none replaces a human for authorization and logic flaws.","part":3}]},"p32m1":{"big":"Intelligence is a loop that starts with a decision-maker's question, ends with a calibrated judgment they can act on, and uses feedback to sharpen the next question.","why":"Facts are never scarce; what a company pays for is a defensible judgment, delivered in time, to the person who can change something. Every SOC and red-team role is downstream of this loop.","parts":[{"t":"The six stages","gist":"The cycle has six stages: planning and direction, collection, processing, analysis and production, dissemination, and feedback. Intelligence also comes at three levels, strategic, operational and tactical, set by who uses it and how far ahead.","core":true},{"t":"Requirements: the PIR","gist":"A PIR (priority intelligence requirement) is a question tied to a decision, phrased so you can tell when it has been answered. It breaks into specific requirements, each mapped to sources in a collection plan.","core":true},{"t":"Analytic standards and estimative language","gist":"State how likely a judgment is with calibrated words, such as likely meaning 55–80%, and separately state your confidence in the evidence. Grade every source with the Admiralty code for reliability and credibility.","core":true},{"t":"Structured analytic techniques and the biases they fight","gist":"The analyst's main enemy is cognitive bias. Analysis of Competing Hypotheses fights it: mark each piece of evidence against every hypothesis, then reject those with the most inconsistencies instead of crowning the best-supported one.","core":true},{"t":"Where the function sits","gist":"In a SOC, intelligence feeds detection engineering, enriches alerts and prioritizes vulnerabilities. Beside a red team, it supplies the scenarios a test should emulate, so the test imitates adversaries that actually target the firm.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Strategic intelligence serves the board over quarters to years, operational serves managers over weeks to months, and tactical serves SOC analysts over hours to days.","part":0},{"p":"A good PIR names a population, a time window, a mechanism and the decision it informs.","part":1},{"p":"Likely means 55–80%; confidence (high, moderate or low) separately describes the quality and corroboration of the evidence.","part":2},{"p":"The Admiralty code grades source reliability A–F and information credibility 1–6; F and 6 mean 'cannot be judged'.","part":2},{"p":"In ACH you reject the hypotheses with the most inconsistent evidence, because consistent evidence is cheap: it fits several stories.","part":3}]},"p32m2":{"big":"OSINT is intelligence from information anyone can lawfully obtain without deceiving, hacking or buying stolen goods, gathered inside clear legal, ethical and safety lines.","why":"These rules decide whether you can hold this job: they keep you employable and out of court, and the GOSI exam has whole objectives on methodology, privacy and OPSEC.","parts":[{"t":"Passive versus active collection","gist":"Passive collection reads what is already published, through third-party services, so the target never sees you. Active collection touches the target or its people, like scans or messages, and needs written authorization.","core":true},{"t":"The legal lines","gist":"Reading public pages is generally not a CFAA problem; using someone else's credentials or bypassing a block is. Terms of service, stalking and harassment law, privacy law and FCRA still apply to public data.","core":true},{"t":"Ethics beyond the law","gist":"Legal is only the floor. Ask whether the data is necessary and the intrusion proportionate, collect the least, verify, never deceive or engage individuals, and keep a record. Never investigate a private individual.","core":true},{"t":"OPSEC for the analyst","gist":"OPSEC protects you and the case: work from a dedicated VM with separate browser profiles and no personal logins. Research accounts exist only under written policy and never message or befriend anyone.","core":true},{"t":"Documentation and evidence","gist":"Capture every page as you saw it, with its URL, a UTC timestamp and a SHA-256 hash proving it was not altered. An investigation you cannot reproduce is only an opinion.","core":true},{"t":"Your first lab: your own footprint","gist":"Your first target is yourself: find your usernames, breached accounts, location-tagged photos and data-broker listings. Then write a cleanup plan to delete, rotate reused passwords, add MFA and opt out.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Open source means lawfully, publicly available without deception, bypass or stolen goods; content behind a login or a friend request is not.","part":0},{"p":"Passive collection touches only third-party services; active collection touches the target or its people and needs written authorization.","part":0},{"p":"Van Buren (2021) narrowed 'exceeding authorized access' to areas you may not enter, not misuse of data you may access.","part":1},{"p":"Stalking and harassment law judges the conduct and its effect, not how public the data was.","part":1},{"p":"Capture evidence with the URL, a UTC timestamp and a SHA-256 hash of the saved file.","part":4}]},"p32m3":{"big":"You can map most of an organization's external attack surface passively, from sources that already exist, as long as you stay in written scope and study assets, not people.","why":"This is the reconnaissance phase of every authorized penetration test and the attack-surface-management job on every blue team. Attackers map the surface first, so defenders should too.","parts":[{"t":"Domains, registration and DNS history","gist":"Company filings reveal forgotten domains. WHOIS is now mostly redacted, so registrar, dates and name servers matter, and passive DNS databases show what pointed where over years without querying the target.","core":true},{"t":"Certificate transparency: the richest passive source","gist":"Publicly trusted TLS certificates must be logged in public, so searching crt.sh lists subdomains admins thought were obscure. Combine passive sources first; brute-forcing names against the target's DNS is active.","core":true},{"t":"Internet-wide scanners","gist":"Shodan and Censys already scan the whole IPv4 internet, so searching them is passive. Look up your own organization to see what the world sees, such as an exposed RDP port or an expired certificate.","core":true},{"t":"Code, cloud storage and credentials","gist":"Secrets leak through public code, open cloud buckets and breached corporate emails. Find them with GitHub search, secret scanners and Have I Been Pwned's domain search, never by downloading breach dumps.","core":true},{"t":"Job postings and the tech-stack leak","gist":"Job postings, case studies and talks often name the exact security stack and the end-of-life systems. Collect this as part of the map and advise recruiters to describe skills generically.","core":false},{"t":"From the map to action","gist":"The result is a table of assets with source, first-seen date, owner and risk note. It defines what an authorized pentest may touch, and gives defenders a reduction backlog and a metric to drive toward zero.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Certificate transparency logs, searched through crt.sh, are the single best passive host list.","part":1},{"p":"Searching Shodan or Censys is passive because the scanning already happened; scanning hosts yourself needs authorization.","part":2},{"p":"Check breach exposure with Have I Been Pwned's domain search and force resets; never download the dumps.","part":3},{"p":"Email formats are inferred only to judge exposure to spraying or spoofing, never to build a list of named staff.","part":3},{"p":"The blue-team metric is the count of unexpected internet-facing assets per quarter, driven toward zero.","part":5}]},"p32m4":{"big":"Question a photo like a witness, through earlier copies, metadata, scene clues and shadows, until independent features agree on where and when it was taken.","why":"Verification is how journalists, human-rights investigators and security teams stop being fooled by fakes, such as recycled footage or a deepfaked video call. You apply it only to public events, your own media and practice sets.","parts":[{"t":"Reverse image search","gist":"Reverse image search shows where else an image has appeared. Each engine has its strengths, and TinEye finds the earliest copy, which answers the most common question: is this old footage recycled for a new event?","core":true},{"t":"EXIF and other metadata","gist":"EXIF is metadata a camera writes into the file: device, capture time and sometimes GPS. Most platforms strip it, so its absence means little, and any field can be edited, so treat it as a lead.","core":true},{"t":"Geolocation: reading the scene","gist":"Work from coarse clues like script, road markings and driving side down to fine ones like rooflines, then confirm against satellite and street imagery. A location is confirmed when several independent features align and none contradicts.","core":true},{"t":"Chronolocation: the sun as a clock","gist":"Shadows act as a clock: tan(elevation) = height ÷ shadow length, and the shadow's direction gives the sun's bearing. With a known date and place, SunCalc turns that into a narrow time window.","core":true},{"t":"Video verification and synthetic media","gist":"The commonest fake is real footage from another place or year, so extract keyframes and check upload times. Deepfake detectors lag behind generators, so rely on provenance such as C2PA Content Credentials and independent corroboration.","core":true},{"t":"Practice that stays ethical","gist":"Train only on sanctioned sets: free OSINT exercises, ranked challenges, Bellingcat write-ups and your own photos with their EXIF as the answer key. Record each solve as a case file.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"TinEye finds the earliest indexed copy of an image, which is how you catch old footage recycled for a new event.","part":0},{"p":"Missing EXIF means little because platforms strip it; present EXIF is a lead, never proof, because every field can be edited.","part":1},{"p":"A location is confirmed when multiple independent features align and no feature contradicts it.","part":2},{"p":"tan(e) = h / L: a 2.0 m signpost with a 2.7 m shadow gives a solar elevation of about 36.5°. Shadows point away from the sun.","part":3},{"p":"Judge suspected deepfakes by provenance (C2PA Content Credentials, first appearance) and independent corroboration, not by detectors alone.","part":4}]},"p32m5":{"big":"Indicators like hashes and IPs are cheap for attackers to change while their behaviors (TTPs) are durable, so useful intelligence describes how adversaries operate, in shared structured forms.","why":"This is the daily toolkit of a CTI analyst and the core of EC-Council's CTIA. It tells your red team which adversary to emulate and your SOC what to hunt.","parts":[{"t":"The Pyramid of Pain","gist":"The Pyramid of Pain ranks indicators by how much it costs the attacker when you detect them. Hashes, IPs and domains are trivial to change; tools are harder, and TTPs, their behavior, are hardest of all.","core":true},{"t":"Kill chain and MITRE ATT&CK","gist":"MITRE ATT&CK is a matrix of observed attacker behavior: tactics are goals, techniques are how each goal is reached. You tag each behavior in a report with an ID like T1078, then compare against your detection coverage.","core":true},{"t":"The Diamond Model","gist":"The Diamond Model links the four corners of any intrusion: adversary, capability, infrastructure and victim. Analysts pivot along the edges, for example from the victim's logs to the infrastructure, then to the malware.","core":true},{"t":"STIX 2.1 and TAXII 2.1","gist":"STIX 2.1 is a JSON language for threat information, such as indicators, malware and actors and how they relate. TAXII 2.1 is the HTTPS transport that lets clients pull new objects from a server.","core":true},{"t":"Platforms: MISP and OpenCTI","gist":"MISP stores, tags, correlates and shares threat events, and comes up in minutes with Docker. OpenCTI is a knowledge-graph alternative built on STIX, and many teams run both.","core":false},{"t":"Feeds and their quality","gist":"Measure feeds by timeliness, overlap, context and false-positive rate. A 50,000-indicator daily feed at 2% false positives makes about 1,000 false alerts a day; filtering to your requirements cuts that to about 10.","core":true},{"t":"Threat actor profiling","gist":"A profile answers who, why, how, against whom and what changed, for nation-state, criminal or hacktivist actors. Build it from graded public reporting, state attribution as a judgment, and end with the actor's favored ATT&CK techniques.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"The Pyramid of Pain from bottom to top: hashes, IP addresses, domains, network and host artifacts, tools, TTPs.","part":0},{"p":"In ATT&CK, tactics are the adversary's goals and techniques are how each goal is achieved, for example T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact.","part":1},{"p":"The Diamond Model's four corners are adversary, capability, infrastructure and victim.","part":2},{"p":"STIX is the JSON language for sharing threat intelligence; TAXII is its HTTPS transport.","part":3},{"p":"Attribution is an analytic judgment with a confidence level, never a fact; vendors use different names for overlapping groups.","part":6}]},"p32m6":{"big":"When authority, urgency and a request to skip normal process arrive together, treat it as suspicious and verify by calling a number you already have.","why":"Most breaches begin with a person being persuaded rather than a system being exploited, so these controls are what actually move risk. They are also tested in Security+, CySA+ and CTIA.","parts":[{"t":"The psychology","gist":"Attackers use Cialdini's principles of influence, such as authority, social proof and scarcity, plus urgency and fear, which narrow attention and suppress careful checking. Any request combining authority, urgency and a process change is suspicious.","core":true},{"t":"The vectors, for recognition","gist":"Each channel has its own tells: lookalike domains in email, callers who refuse a callback, short links in texts, strangers with full hands at the door. Pretexting, a detailed made-up story, drives most of them.","core":true},{"t":"Business email compromise: the case file","gist":"BEC (business email compromise) is a convincing instruction to move money, with no malware. The pattern never varies: authority, urgency and a payment change outside the normal channel. The fix: call back a number on file.","core":true},{"t":"Awareness programs that work","gist":"Good awareness programs are short, frequent and role-based, teach the cues rather than specific lures, and never blame people for being fooled. Measure report rate and time-to-report, not click rate.","core":true},{"t":"Authorized phishing simulations","gist":"A phishing simulation needs written authorization, a policy staff have acknowledged, respectful lures and no capture of real passwords. Pair it with structural fixes like phishing-resistant MFA and DMARC, because training alone plateaus.","core":true},{"t":"Verification protocols and physical basics","gist":"Write the protocols down so people can follow them under pressure: callback for any payment or credential change, two approvers above a threshold, real help-desk identity checks, and no urgency exception. Physical security follows the same logic.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Cialdini's seven principles: reciprocity, commitment and consistency, social proof, authority, liking, scarcity and unity.","part":0},{"p":"Confirm any payment-change or wire request by calling a number already on file, never one in the message, and require two approvers above a threshold.","part":2},{"p":"FIDO2 security keys made Cloudflare's phished credentials useless in 2022, because the keys are bound to the real origin.","part":2},{"p":"Measure awareness by report rate and time-to-first-report; click rate mostly tracks how hard the lure was.","part":3},{"p":"Simulations never capture real passwords: the landing page records the attempt, not the secret.","part":4}]},"p32m7":{"big":"Intelligence writing puts the bottom line first: the judgment, how sure you are and what to do, so a reader who stops after one paragraph still gets the decision.","why":"Writing and briefing are what separate an analyst who is promoted from one who is tolerated, and the cost picture lets you plan the GOSI realistically on a technician's budget.","parts":[{"t":"The report: BLUF first","gist":"Open with the BLUF (bottom line up front): the judgment, its confidence and what to do. Then come key judgments, scope, analysis, graded sourcing, recommendations and appendices, with the whole report marked under TLP 2.0.","core":true},{"t":"Briefing executives","gist":"Five minutes, four parts: bottom line and ask, evidence, what happens if nothing is done, and what you need. Be ready for three questions: how sure you are, who else this has happened to, and what it costs.","core":true},{"t":"Metrics for an intelligence program","gist":"Prove the program earns its budget with numbers: requirements coverage, timeliness, decisions influenced, detections produced and consumer feedback, plus attack-surface and phishing-resilience trends.","core":true},{"t":"Careers","gist":"Two paths: the CTI analyst beside a SOC or at a vendor, and the OSINT investigator in corporate security, fraud, trust and safety or journalism. A portfolio of real write-ups is what gets interviews.","core":false},{"t":"The credentials, honestly priced","gist":"GOSI is 75 questions in 2 hours, 69% to pass, open-book, at 999 dollars. The SANS course is optional and costly, so self-study against the published objectives is realistic, and cheaper credentials exist.","core":false},{"t":"A twelve-week plan","gist":"A week-by-week route through this domain's modules and labs, ending with a GIAC practice test, a refined open-book index and the exam.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"BLUF means the opening paragraph states the judgment, its confidence and what the reader should do.","part":0},{"p":"TLP 2.0: RED for named recipients, AMBER for the organization and clients on need-to-know, AMBER+STRICT for the organization alone, GREEN for the community, CLEAR unlimited.","part":0},{"p":"Never say 'probably' without the percentage band behind it in your notes, and never present an IOC list to a board.","part":1},{"p":"Track decisions influenced: the count of control, patch or budget changes traced to a report.","part":2},{"p":"GOSI: 75 questions, 2 hours, 69% to pass, open-book; the exam is 999 dollars and does not require the SANS course.","part":4}]},"p33m1":{"big":"Governance turns security into decisions: the business sets how much risk it will take, writes the rules down, and names an owner for everything worth protecting.","why":"Nothing gets fixed until governance turns a finding or alert into an owner, a deadline and a budget, and the GRC analyst role is one of the most reliable entry doors for someone who writes clearly.","parts":[{"t":"Risk appetite: the number behind every control","gist":"Risk appetite is how much risk the organization will take on, set by the board. Tolerances make it measurable for each objective, like a maximum outage, and capacity is the loss it could survive at all.","core":true},{"t":"The document hierarchy","gist":"A policy says what is required and why, a standard gives the testable specifics, a procedure gives the steps, a guideline recommends, and a baseline sets a platform's minimum configuration.","core":true},{"t":"Roles: who owns what","gist":"The data owner is the business executive accountable for a data set, who decides its classification, access and retention. IT is the custodian that carries out those decisions, not the owner.","core":true},{"t":"The three lines model","gist":"The first line runs operations and owns the risks, the second line (risk, compliance, GRC) oversees and challenges, and the third line, internal audit, independently checks that both work.","core":true},{"t":"Security as an enabler","gist":"Governance lets the business do what it wants, like signing a customer who demands a SOC 2 report or qualifying for cyber insurance. Compliance drives security budgets, and breach costs are the counterweight.","core":false},{"t":"From a pentest finding to a risk decision","gist":"A pentest finding is restated as a risk to a named asset and owner. The owner picks a treatment, the decision and due date go in the risk register, and a retest provides the evidence that closes it.","core":true},{"t":"The GRC analyst job","gist":"The GRC analyst runs the risk register, maps controls to frameworks, collects audit evidence, answers security questionnaires and drafts policies. Security+ plus clear writing and a defensible spreadsheet is the entry route.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Appetite sits below capacity, and tolerances make appetite testable, for example 'unavailable no more than 4 hours per quarter'.","part":0},{"p":"An auditor tests the standard, an engineer follows the procedure, and the executive signs the policy.","part":1},{"p":"IT is the custodian of data; the business executive accountable for it is the owner and decides classification, access and retention.","part":2},{"p":"Three lines: operations own risk, risk and compliance oversee, and internal audit gives independent assurance.","part":3},{"p":"The four risk treatments are mitigate, transfer, avoid and accept.","part":5}]},"p33m2":{"big":"Risk combines likelihood and impact, and putting it in money, as expected annual loss before and after a control, shows whether the control is worth buying.","why":"This is how you convince a CFO with a number instead of fear, how CRISC and CISA questions are written, and how a pentester ranks findings so they get fixed.","parts":[{"t":"The parts of a risk","gist":"A threat exploits a vulnerability in an asset; risk combines how likely that is with how much harm it does. Inherent risk is before controls, residual is after, and only residual risk is compared against tolerance.","core":true},{"t":"Qualitative risk: the heat map and its traps","gist":"Heat maps score likelihood and impact from 1 to 5 and multiply them. That is fine for sorting, but the numbers are only rankings, so anchor the scales and quantify the risks that matter.","core":true},{"t":"Quantitative risk: SLE, ARO, ALE","gist":"SLE = asset value × exposure factor, and ALE = SLE × ARO, the expected events per year. A control is worth buying when the drop in ALE beats its annual cost; ROSI expresses that as a ratio.","core":true},{"t":"FAIR: risk as a distribution","gist":"FAIR uses ranges instead of single guesses for how often losses happen and how big they are, then runs a Monte Carlo simulation to show the chance that annual losses exceed each amount.","core":true},{"t":"The risk register","gist":"The risk register is the system of record: each risk with a named owner, scores, controls, treatment, due dates and a review date. It should drive real work, not sit unopened between audits.","core":true},{"t":"Treatment options","gist":"There are four treatments: mitigate, transfer or share, avoid, or accept. Acceptance is legitimate when a named owner signs within tolerance; silent acceptance, where nobody decided, is the failure.","core":true},{"t":"Controls by function and category","gist":"Controls are sorted by function (preventive, detective, corrective, deterrent or directive) and by category (technical, administrative, physical). A compensating control must meet the intent and rigor of the one it replaces.","core":true},{"t":"Residual risk, the NIST RMF and ISO 31000","gist":"The NIST RMF is the federal lifecycle: Prepare, Categorize, Select, Implement, Assess, Authorize, Monitor. The official who authorizes a system accepts its residual risk in writing. ISO 31000 is the generic risk standard.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Residual risk, never inherent risk, is compared against tolerance and either accepted by a named owner or escalated.","part":0},{"p":"Heat-map scales are ordinal: 'likelihood 4' is not twice 'likelihood 2', so their product is only a sorting convention.","part":1},{"p":"SLE = asset value × exposure factor; ALE = SLE × ARO. A control pays off when the ALE reduction exceeds its annual cost.","part":2},{"p":"ROSI = (ALE reduction − control cost) ÷ control cost.","part":2},{"p":"Transferring risk does not transfer accountability: customers, regulators and the press still hold you responsible.","part":5}]},"p33m3":{"big":"Frameworks are security's shared building codes: assess and map each control once, then show customers, auditors and regulators your work in the language they ask in.","why":"Customers, auditors and regulators ask in framework language. Knowing which framework fits and how they map is the GRC analyst's core skill and the core content of the ISO 27001 Lead Implementer exam.","parts":[{"t":"NIST CSF 2.0","gist":"NIST CSF 2.0 organizes security outcomes into six functions: Govern, Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond and Recover. Tiers describe how rigorous you are, and the gap between your Current and Target Profiles is your roadmap.","core":true},{"t":"NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5","gist":"SP 800-53 is the detailed control catalog: more than a thousand controls in 20 families with two-letter IDs, like AC for access control. Low, Moderate and High baselines are the starting points federal systems tailor.","core":true},{"t":"CIS Controls v8.1","gist":"CIS Controls v8.1 is a free, prioritized list of 18 controls and 153 safeguards. Start with IG1, the 56 safeguards of essential hygiene. Asset and software inventory come first because you cannot protect what you have not listed.","core":true},{"t":"ISO/IEC 27001:2022 and the ISMS","gist":"ISO 27001 specifies an ISMS, a management system for running security, and you can be certified against it. Clauses 4–10 are mandatory; the Statement of Applicability says which of Annex A's 93 controls apply and why.","core":true},{"t":"SOC 2","gist":"SOC 2 is not a certification but a CPA firm's attestation report against the Trust Services Criteria. Type I judges control design at one point in time; Type II judges operation over a period, and enterprise customers want Type II.","core":true},{"t":"Mapping between frameworks","gist":"Map each control once to every framework in a control library, and the next questionnaire becomes a lookup. But a mapping only claims coverage; only evidence shows the control is implemented and working.","core":true},{"t":"Choosing a framework for a small business","gist":"Choose by business driver: CIS IG1 plus a CSF profile with no regulator, SOC 2 Type II to sell to US enterprises, ISO 27001 for international customers, PCI DSS for card payments, and FedRAMP or CMMC for government work.","core":true},{"t":"A worked gap assessment","gist":"For each safeguard, record implemented, partial or missing, plus the evidence, owner and effort. Then build a roadmap ordered by effort and risk, closing the cheapest, most exploitable gaps first.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"CSF 2.0's six functions are Govern, Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond and Recover; Govern is new in 2.0.","part":0},{"p":"CIS implementation groups: IG1 is 56 safeguards, IG2 is 130 cumulative, IG3 is all 153.","part":2},{"p":"ISO 27001:2022 Annex A has 93 controls in four themes: 37 organizational, 8 people, 14 physical and 34 technological.","part":3},{"p":"ISO 27001 does not require all 93 controls; the Statement of Applicability justifies which apply, based on the risk assessment.","part":3},{"p":"SOC 2 Type I covers control design at a point in time; Type II covers operating effectiveness over usually 3 to 12 months.","part":4}]},"p33m4":{"big":"Compliance regimes reach you through law, contract or standard, and each one translates into specific controls, evidence and a breach clock you must know.","why":"Compliance is where budgets, deadlines and penalties come from. Knowing what each regime technically demands lets you scope a pentest, design a system or answer an auditor without guessing.","parts":[{"t":"PCI DSS 4.0.1","gist":"PCI DSS reaches you through your bank's contract and covers every system that stores, processes or transmits card data, plus anything connected to it. Shrinking that scope with segmentation, tokenization or outsourcing is the biggest cost lever.","core":true},{"t":"HIPAA","gist":"HIPAA's Security Rule sets administrative, physical and technical safeguards for electronic health data, and a missing risk analysis is the most-cited failure. 'Addressable' never means optional, and breach notices are due within 60 days.","core":true},{"t":"GDPR","gist":"GDPR protects people in the EU wherever the company sits. Every processing activity needs a lawful basis, people have rights over their data, high-risk processing needs a DPIA, and breaches go to the regulator within 72 hours.","core":true},{"t":"CCPA/CPRA and the US state patchwork","gist":"The US has no comprehensive federal privacy law. California's CCPA/CPRA and around twenty state laws fill the gap, and all fifty states have breach-notification laws, so build one program to the strictest common rules.","core":true},{"t":"SOX","gist":"SOX makes public-company executives certify their financial reports, so IT general controls over financial systems carry legal weight: access reviews, segregation of duties, approved and tested changes, and reliable operations.","core":true},{"t":"FedRAMP and CMMC","gist":"FedRAMP authorizes cloud services for federal agencies against SP 800-53 baselines. CMMC protects defense-supply-chain data at three levels; the phase requiring third-party certification was suspended in July 2026 pending review, but self-assessments stay in force.","core":true},{"t":"Sector rules: NERC CIP and GLBA","gist":"NERC CIP binds operators of the North American power grid, with requirements scaled by each system's impact rating. GLBA's Safeguards Rule binds financial institutions and requires FTC notice within 30 days for events affecting 500 or more consumers.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"PCI DSS applies to every merchant that accepts cards; size changes only how you validate, by SAQ or QSA assessment, not whether you comply.","part":0},{"p":"PCI DSS 4.0.1 requires MFA for all access into the cardholder data environment and passwords of at least 12 characters.","part":0},{"p":"In HIPAA, 'addressable' means implement it, implement a documented equivalent, or document why not; individuals hear of a breach within 60 days.","part":1},{"p":"GDPR: notify the supervisory authority within 72 hours of becoming aware; fines reach €20 million or 4% of worldwide annual turnover.","part":2},{"p":"SOX IT general controls cover logical access, change management and computer operations for financially relevant systems.","part":4}]},"p33m5":{"big":"Privacy engineering builds systems that protect people even when authorized processing works exactly as designed: collect less, blur details, honor choices and forget on schedule.","why":"This is the technical core of the CIPT and the privacy-engineer job. It is threat modeling and cryptography applied to people instead of servers, the part of GRC a hacker finds most natural.","parts":[{"t":"Privacy by design","gist":"Privacy by design means building privacy in from the start and making it the default. Hoepman's eight strategies turn that into engineering; prefer minimize, because data you never collected cannot leak.","core":true},{"t":"Data mapping, inventory, classification, retention","gist":"List every data set: what it is, where it lives, why you hold it, who owns it, how long you keep it and where it flows. Then engineer deletion that reaches backups, indexes and analytics copies, not just a flag.","core":true},{"t":"Pseudonymization, anonymization and re-identification","gist":"Pseudonymized data, using a keyed hash or token vault, is still personal data. True anonymization is hard because details like ZIP code, birth date and sex can be linked to outside data. k-anonymity and l-diversity are only a floor.","core":true},{"t":"Differential privacy","gist":"Differential privacy adds calibrated noise so one person's presence changes the chance of any answer by at most a factor of e^ε. Smaller ε means stronger privacy, and repeated queries use up a privacy budget.","core":true},{"t":"Consent and preference management","gist":"Consent must be freely given, specific, informed, as easy to withdraw as to give, and provable. Store it as data per person and purpose, push it to every downstream system, and honor Global Privacy Control.","core":true},{"t":"Privacy threat modeling: LINDDUN","gist":"LINDDUN is privacy's STRIDE: seven threat types, from Linking and Identifying to Non-compliance. Draw the data flows, check each type against each element, then prioritize and mitigate with Hoepman's strategies.","core":true},{"t":"Privacy-enhancing technologies","gist":"Technologies that let you compute while revealing less: secure enclaves, homomorphic encryption, multiparty computation, federated learning, differential privacy and zero-knowledge proofs. Their maturity ranges from production to niche.","core":false},{"t":"The CIPT body of knowledge","gist":"The CIPT body of knowledge 4.0.0 has five domains, and this module covers Domains II, IV and V plus the impact-assessment part of III.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Soft deletes are not erasure; backups need rotation or crypto-shredding, which destroys a per-user encryption key.","part":1},{"p":"A plain SHA-256 of an email is not pseudonymization, because attackers hash a dictionary and match; use a keyed HMAC with the key held separately.","part":2},{"p":"Sweeney showed ZIP code, birth date and sex uniquely identify about 87% of Americans; later estimates put it near 63%.","part":2},{"p":"The Laplace mechanism adds noise of scale Δf/ε; a count has sensitivity 1, and k queries at ε each cost kε of the budget.","part":3},{"p":"Withdrawing consent must be as easy as giving it, and a withdrawal must actually stop processing in every downstream system.","part":4}]},"p33m6":{"big":"A control that cannot be shown to operate does not exist for a customer or regulator, so design dated, system-generated evidence and produce it automatically.","why":"The person who can design evidence and automate it is the GRC engineer every company now wants, and the audit becomes a by-product of operations instead of a quarterly fire drill.","parts":[{"t":"The audit lifecycle","gist":"Every audit runs four phases: planning, fieldwork, reporting and follow-up. In fieldwork the auditor tests a sample they choose from the whole population, and each finding states condition, criteria, cause, effect and recommendation.","core":true},{"t":"Evidence: what proves a control","gist":"System-generated, dated records from the system of record beat screenshots, which beat written promises. Good evidence covers the whole population, falls inside the audit period, and can be re-run by the auditor.","core":true},{"t":"Compliance as code and continuous control monitoring","gist":"Compliance as code turns a control into a machine-checked rule run continuously, so failures open tickets and the audit samples a year of results. Automation tests configuration, not intent.","core":true},{"t":"Vendor and third-party risk","gist":"Your vendors are your attack surface. Tier them, use standard questionnaires, read their SOC 2 reports critically for period, scope, exceptions and the controls you must run yourself, and contract for breach notice and audit rights.","core":true},{"t":"SBOMs and supply-chain attestations","gist":"An SBOM is a software ingredient list of components, versions, suppliers and dependencies, answering 'where do we run this component?'. VEX says whether a listed vulnerability is exploitable, and attestations show how the build was made.","core":true},{"t":"Incident reporting obligations","gist":"Each regime has its own reporting clock, from 4 business days for an SEC 8-K to 60 days under HIPAA, and every one starts at awareness, not at root cause. Record the awareness time when the incident is declared.","core":true},{"t":"Writing policies that engineers will actually follow","gist":"Write policies like code: short, one requirement per sentence in MUST, SHOULD and MAY words, each mapped to evidence, kept in Git and reviewed through pull requests. A requirement with no evidence is a wish.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"An audit finding states condition (what is), criteria (what should be), cause, effect and recommendation.","part":0},{"p":"Evidence hierarchy: system-generated, dated records beat screenshots, which beat written attestations.","part":1},{"p":"Automation tests configuration, not intent: a rule can prove MFA is enabled, not that the right people have access.","part":2},{"p":"Read a vendor's SOC 2 for its period and bridge letter, scope, exceptions, complementary user-entity controls and carved-out subservice organizations.","part":3},{"p":"SEC 8-K Item 1.05 allows 4 business days after an incident is judged material; GDPR and DFARS 7012 allow 72 hours.","part":5}]},"p33m7":{"big":"A security program runs on per-domain maturity targets, owned metrics with thresholds, business cases in money terms, rehearsed response, and recovery targets from a business impact analysis.","why":"This is how security knowledge becomes a program a company funds and a board understands, and it gives you a clear order for which certification to sit next.","parts":[{"t":"Program maturity","gist":"A maturity model rates how reliably a capability is performed, not how many tools you own. Set a target per domain from risk appetite; 'level 5 everywhere' means nobody did the risk analysis.","core":true},{"t":"Metrics: KPIs and KRIs","gist":"KPIs measure how well a control works; KRIs warn early that risk is nearing tolerance. Good metrics are precisely defined, owned, trended and given green, amber and red thresholds. Drop vanity numbers like 'attacks blocked'.","core":true},{"t":"Budgets and business cases","gist":"A business case speaks the CFO's language: the risk in dollars, options with total cost of ownership, a recommendation and a deadline. In the example, a $30,000 managed service beats an in-house analyst costing about $110,000.","core":true},{"t":"Security awareness that works","gist":"Annual compliance training changes almost nothing by itself. Behavior changes with role-based, short, frequent training, a one-click report button and a no-blame culture, measured by how many people report phishing.","core":true},{"t":"Tabletop exercises","gist":"A tabletop is a talk-through rehearsal: a facilitator reads timed injects, short scenario updates, and each team says what it would do. The gaps it exposes become an after-action report with owners and dates.","core":true},{"t":"Business continuity and disaster recovery","gist":"A business impact analysis works out how long each process can be down. From it come RTO, how fast a service must be back, and RPO, how much data, measured in time, you can lose.","core":true},{"t":"The GRC and privacy career ladder","gist":"The ladder runs from analyst and auditor roles up to CISO, chief privacy officer or chief risk officer, with pay rising at each rung. Being able to quantify, write, automate and read technical work makes you rare.","core":false},{"t":"Exam strategy: CIPT, ISO/IEC 27001, then ISACA","gist":"Start with the CIPT, which needs no experience, then ISO/IEC 27001 Foundation or Lead Implementer. CISA and CRISC need five and three years of experience, though you can pass the exam first and certify later.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Set maturity targets per domain from risk appetite; 'level 5 everywhere' is a sign nobody did the risk analysis.","part":0},{"p":"A KPI measures how well a control performs; a KRI is a leading signal that exposure is approaching tolerance.","part":1},{"p":"Measure phishing awareness by report rate and time-to-report, not click rate, which mostly measures how good the lure was.","part":1},{"p":"RTO is how quickly a service must be restored; RPO is how much data, measured in time, may be lost.","part":5},{"p":"Certification order: Security+, CIPT, ISO/IEC 27001 Foundation or Lead Implementer, a GRC job, then CISA or CRISC once the years exist.","part":7}]},"p34m1":{"big":"Every model call is tokens in and tokens out, so its cost, speed and reliability are things you can calculate before you build.","why":"Every design decision, from which model to how long the prompt to hosted or local, is a token-and-dollar calculation you should be able to do on a napkin before you build.","parts":[{"t":"Tokens and the context window","gist":"A token is the chunk of text a model reads and writes, roughly three to four characters of English. You pay for every token in the context window on every call, and material in the middle is recalled worse.","core":true},{"t":"Sampling: temperature and top-p","gist":"Temperature reshapes the model's odds for the next token: near 0 the favorite always wins, higher is more random. Use 0 for extraction and code, 0.7–1.0 for brainstorming, and change temperature or top-p, not both.","core":true},{"t":"Structured outputs and tool calling","gist":"Asking for JSON in the prompt can fail; structured outputs constrain decoding to your schema, though you still validate and retry. Tool calling uses the same idea: the model requests a function, and your code runs it.","core":true},{"t":"Streaming and latency arithmetic","gist":"Wait time is time to first token plus output tokens divided by tokens per second. Streaming doesn't make the model faster, it shows words sooner; when no human is watching, the Batch API is the better deal.","core":true},{"t":"Cost arithmetic","gist":"Cost is input tokens times input price plus output tokens times output price, priced per million tokens. Prompt caching, the Batch API and a cheaper model tier are the big levers for cutting it.","core":true},{"t":"Hosted API or local model?","gist":"Hosted models win on top quality and zero ops; local models win on privacy, offline use and predictable cost at high volume. Most professional systems are hybrid, sending only the hard 5% to a frontier model.","core":true},{"t":"How this maps to the exams","gist":"Where inference settings, token economics and cost control show up on the Databricks Generative AI Engineer Associate, NVIDIA NCA-GENL and AWS AI Practitioner exams.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"In English, one token is roughly three to four characters, so 100 tokens is about 75 words.","part":0},{"p":"Use temperature 0 for extraction, classification, code and anything you parse; 0.7–1.0 for brainstorming and prose.","part":1},{"p":"JSON mode guarantees valid JSON but not your fields; only schema-constrained structured outputs guarantee the schema, and you still validate and retry.","part":2},{"p":"A 300-token answer at 80 tokens per second with a 0.6 s time to first token takes about 4.4 s; streamed, text appears at 0.6 s.","part":3},{"p":"Prompt caching reads a fixed prefix at 0.05–0.1× input price and the Batch API takes 50% off, so put the stable prompt part first.","part":4}]},"p34m2":{"big":"A prompt is a specification that becomes reliable only when structured, shown examples, versioned and tested, and no wording can stop prompt injection.","why":"It is how you get prompts that behave the same on the thousandth call as on the first, prove it with tests, and face the one security problem no prompt can solve.","parts":[{"t":"Instructions versus examples","gist":"Instructions say what to do; examples show how. Models copy two to five worked examples far more faithfully than they obey words like 'concisely', and wrapping data in tags keeps it apart from instructions.","core":true},{"t":"Chain-of-thought and its limits","gist":"Asking a model to reason step by step helps ordinary models on multi-step problems, but it costs tokens, the written reasoning may not reflect what really happened, and reasoning models don't need the instruction.","core":true},{"t":"System prompts","gist":"The system prompt is the standing rulebook: role, tone, format and what to do when unsure. It strongly shapes behavior but is not a security boundary, because the model reads everything as one stream of text.","core":true},{"t":"Prompt injection: the core security problem","gist":"Prompt injection is untrusted text the model treats as instructions, typed by a user or hidden in fetched content. Better wording can't stop it, so the defenses live in your design: least privilege, approval, validation and monitoring.","core":true},{"t":"Templates, versioning and evaluation-driven prompting","gist":"Treat prompts as code: keep them in the repo, review changes, pin the model snapshot, and run an eval set of realistic test inputs on every change, changing one thing at a time.","core":true},{"t":"Case study: ADA, the tutor in this app","gist":"ADA, this app's tutor, is a structured system prompt plus lesson context and the last ten turns. Reviewing it shows the fixes: add few-shot examples, build an eval set, log prompt versions, and don't treat 'never reveal' as security.","core":true},{"t":"How this maps to the exams","gist":"Where prompt design, guardrails, prompt versioning and injection defense show up on the Databricks, AWS AIF-C01 and NVIDIA NCA-GENL exams.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Few-shot prompting puts two to five worked input-to-output examples in the prompt; the model copies their format, length and edge-case handling.","part":0},{"p":"With reasoning models, skip 'think step by step'; describe the goal and constraints and let the model budget its own thinking.","part":1},{"p":"A system prompt is a strong prior, not a security boundary: assume it will be read and keep secrets out of it.","part":2},{"p":"The lethal trifecta is private data, untrusted content and a channel to send data out; remove any one leg.","part":3},{"p":"Never change a prompt without running the eval set: 30 to 300 realistic inputs with expected outputs or rubrics.","part":4}]},"p34m3":{"big":"RAG lets a model answer from your own documents by finding the few right passages, putting them in the prompt and making it cite them.","why":"Retrieval is how a model learns about your data without retraining. It is the center of the Databricks exam and the first portfolio project interviewers ask about.","parts":[{"t":"Embeddings and cosine similarity","gist":"An embedding turns text into a list of numbers so texts with similar meaning land close together. Closeness is measured by cosine similarity: 1 for the same direction, 0 for unrelated. Index and query with the same model.","core":true},{"t":"Chunking","gist":"Documents are split into passages small enough to be specific and big enough to make sense alone, with a baseline of 300–800 tokens and 10–15% overlap. Smaller chunks gain precision but lose context; bigger ones cost tokens.","core":true},{"t":"Vector databases","gist":"A vector database stores chunks as embeddings and quickly finds the ones nearest a query. pgvector, Chroma, FAISS and managed services all do this; choose the one closest to where your data already lives.","core":true},{"t":"Hybrid search and reranking","gist":"Vector search handles paraphrase but misses exact strings like error codes, which keyword search (BM25) catches. Hybrid search merges both rankings; then a reranker picks the best 5 from a wider set, the biggest quality upgrade.","core":true},{"t":"Citations and grounding","gist":"Number the chunks, make the model cite [n] after each claim and say when the documents don't support an answer, then check the citations in code. Grounding turns a confident guess into an answer you can audit.","core":true},{"t":"Failure modes","gist":"The classic ways RAG goes wrong: facts lost in the middle, stale indexes, users seeing chunks they shouldn't, poisoned documents, short queries that don't match long passages, and too many chunks stuffed in. Each has a known fix.","core":true},{"t":"A worked pipeline over your own notes","gist":"A short Chroma pipeline that chunks your notes, retrieves 20 hits and reranks to 5. Test it with 30 known questions, measuring retrieval and answer correctness separately, because they fail differently.","core":false},{"t":"How this maps to the exams","gist":"How chunking, vector search indexes and reranking show up on the Databricks exam, where RAG is the center of gravity, and on AWS AIF-C01 and NCA-GENL.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Cosine similarity is a·b ÷ (|a||b|): 1 means same direction, 0 unrelated. (1, 2, 2) and (2, 1, 2) give 8/9 = 0.889.","part":0},{"p":"Baseline chunking is 300–800 tokens with 10–15% overlap; chunk size × top-k is the prompt you pay for on every query.","part":1},{"p":"Reciprocal rank fusion sums 1/(60 + rank) across engines, so a chunk both BM25 and vector search rank highly wins.","part":3},{"p":"Retrieve wide, rerank narrow: take a generous candidate set, say 50, and let a cross-encoder reranker keep the top 5.","part":3},{"p":"Filter by access metadata before similarity search, never after, or users can retrieve chunks they may not read.","part":5}]},"p34m4":{"big":"An agent is a model in a loop your code runs: you choose the tools, validate every call and fence it so a compromised model's harm stays bounded.","why":"Agents are where LLM products create real value and real damage. Knowing how to bound one, and when not to build one at all, is what makes an agent setup professional.","parts":[{"t":"The agent loop, as code","gist":"An agent loop is a while-loop: send messages and tool definitions, run any tool calls the model asks for, append the results, repeat. Your code adds the limits: a turn cap, a budget, a timeout and a log.","core":true},{"t":"Tool schemas","gist":"A tool is a name, a description and an argument schema, and the description is a prompt the model reads. Keep tools few, small and read-only where possible, with error messages the model can recover from.","core":true},{"t":"Planning patterns","gist":"ReAct mixes a short reasoning step with each action; plan-and-execute writes the plan first. But most problems are better solved by workflows: fixed code paths with model calls at set points.","core":true},{"t":"Multi-agent orchestration","gist":"A supervisor agent handing work to specialist agents is attractive but expensive: tokens multiply and errors compound. Use it for parallel work, sub-tasks needing different permissions, or a cheap model supervised by a strong one.","core":false},{"t":"The Model Context Protocol","gist":"MCP is the open standard that lets any AI app plug into tools and data: hosts run a client per server, and servers offer tools, resources and prompts. The 2026-07-28 revision made it stateless.","core":true},{"t":"Memory designs","gist":"Agents remember through the message list, a scratchpad, a store of past episodes or curated notes. Each is attack surface: an injected instruction written to memory persists across sessions.","core":false},{"t":"Guardrails and sandboxing","gist":"Guardrails are controls outside the model: tool allowlists, argument checks, sandboxes, human approval for irreversible actions, egress controls, budgets and tracing. Design so the worst a compromised model can do is bounded and visible.","core":true},{"t":"When not to build an agent","gist":"If the steps are known, write a workflow; it's cheaper, faster and testable. Build an agent only when the path depends on things you can't foresee and the actions are real but bounded.","core":true},{"t":"Worked example: an agent that runs your home-lab checks","gist":"A home-lab health-check agent with four read-only, allowlisted tools and a hard turn cap. It shows a useful agent you can trust because the fence, not the model, decides what runs.","core":false},{"t":"How this maps to the exams","gist":"Where tool design, agent patterns, MCP servers and agent memory appear on the Databricks, NCA-GENL and AWS AIF-C01 exams.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"The model never runs tools; it emits a structured request and your harness runs it, so validation and allowlists belong in your code.","part":0},{"p":"Few tools with excellent descriptions and clear, model-readable error messages beat a big toolbox; each tool widens the attack surface.","part":1},{"p":"An MCP server exposes three primitives, tools, resources and prompts, over JSON-RPC 2.0 via stdio or Streamable HTTP.","part":4},{"p":"The standard agent breach is OWASP LLM03 Excessive Agency plus LLM01 prompt injection: too much permission meeting an untrusted page.","part":6},{"p":"If the steps are known, write a workflow, not an agent: it is cheaper, faster, testable and deterministic.","part":7}]},"p34m5":{"big":"Fine-tune to change a model's defaults, such as style, format and schema adherence, not to teach it facts, and prove it beat the prompted and RAG baselines.","why":"Fine-tuning is the most expensive lever and the one most often reached for too early. Done on your own GPU and proven with a before/after table, it becomes a rare, hireable skill.","parts":[{"t":"When fine-tuning beats prompting and RAG","gist":"Prompting changes behavior, RAG supplies knowledge, and fine-tuning changes defaults like style, format and tone. It doesn't reliably teach facts, so try the cheaper levers first and keep the eval set showing they weren't enough.","core":true},{"t":"Supervised fine-tuning and data formats","gist":"Supervised fine-tuning trains the model on example conversations showing the replies you want. A few hundred clean, correct examples beat ten thousand noisy ones, and the eval set must be held out from training.","core":true},{"t":"LoRA, QLoRA and parameter-efficient methods","gist":"Full fine-tuning a 7B model needs roughly 112 GB, a multi-GPU job. LoRA freezes the weights and trains a small add-on update; QLoRA also stores the base in 4-bit, so a 7B run fits a 16 GB T4.","core":true},{"t":"Quantization for deployment","gist":"After training you shrink the model for serving: GGUF for llama.cpp on CPUs and consumer GPUs, AWQ or GPTQ for vLLM on NVIDIA GPUs. Always re-run your eval set, because 4-bit can cost several points.","core":true},{"t":"Preference tuning: RLHF and DPO","gist":"SFT shows what good answers look like; preference tuning teaches which of two answers is better. RLHF needs a reward model and is unstable and expensive; DPO learns from chosen-versus-rejected pairs without one.","core":true},{"t":"Evaluating a fine-tune","gist":"Before training, freeze an eval set and score the base model, the prompted baseline and the RAG baseline. Afterwards compare on the same set and check for regressions; beating the base but losing to the prompted baseline is a failure.","core":true},{"t":"The hardware arithmetic: training versus inference","gist":"Training is limited by memory and measured in GPU-hours; inference is limited by memory bandwidth and measured in tokens per second. The table shows which jobs fit a T4, a 24 GB card or data-center GPUs.","core":true},{"t":"How this maps to the exams","gist":"How fine-tuning, LoRA and quantization show up on the Databricks, NCA-GENL and AWS AIF-C01 exams, including when to fine-tune versus use RAG or prompting.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Fine-tune for behavior, format and style; facts belong in retrieval, where they can be updated and cited.","part":0},{"p":"Full fine-tuning needs about 16 bytes per parameter, so a 7B model needs roughly 112 GB before activations.","part":2},{"p":"QLoRA stores the frozen base in 4-bit NF4, so a 7B fine-tune fits a 16 GB T4 on free Colab.","part":2},{"p":"Re-run the eval set on the exact quantized file you serve; a 4-bit export can lose several points on your task.","part":3},{"p":"DPO tunes preferences from (prompt, chosen, rejected) triples with no reward model or RL loop.","part":4}]},"p34m6":{"big":"You only know an LLM system works by measuring it: build a versioned eval set before the prompt, trace every request, and red-team your own app before others do.","why":"Most LLM features die as demos that worked on five examples, then went quietly wrong on a thousand nobody checked. Measuring first, and keeping on measuring in production, is the cure.","parts":[{"t":"Why evals come first","gist":"You can't tell whether a change helped by looking at three outputs. An eval set is a fixed set of test inputs with expected answers or rubrics, built before the prompt and run on every change.","core":true},{"t":"Metrics and their limits","gist":"Each metric sees some things and misses others. Exact match and F1 suit short answers, BLEU and ROUGE measure word overlap rather than truth, and judges or humans handle open-ended quality. Use free, deterministic metrics wherever they apply.","core":true},{"t":"LLM-as-judge and its biases","gist":"An LLM judge is a strong model grading outputs against a rubric. It favors the first answer shown, longer answers and its own style, so swap positions, use rubrics with references, a different model family, and check against humans.","core":true},{"t":"Tracing and logging","gist":"One request can fan out into many model calls, retrievals and tool runs; tracing records that tree as nested spans. Log versions, tokens, cost and latency, but capture prompt content only when you opt in, with personal data masked.","core":true},{"t":"Red-teaming your own application","gist":"Red-teaming means attacking your own system, with authorization, to find failures before users do: injection, jailbreaks, PII leaks, runaway tool use. Turn every finding into an eval case and use the OWASP LLM Top 10 as your checklist.","core":true},{"t":"Defenses: injection, jailbreaks, output handling, PII","gist":"No single defense is enough, so stack imperfect layers: flag suspicious input, delimit untrusted content, limit tools, validate output before it reaches a shell, SQL or HTML, and mask personal data before it reaches the model or logs.","core":false},{"t":"Responsible-AI frameworks","gist":"NIST's AI Risk Management Framework sorts the work into Govern, Map, Measure and Manage. For an engineer that means: write down intended use, map the harms, measure them with evals, and manage them with controls and an owner.","core":true},{"t":"How this maps to the exams","gist":"Where evaluation, monitoring, judges, masking and governance show up on the Databricks, NCA-GENL and AWS AIF-C01 exams.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Thirty eval cases find gross problems and 300 find trends; keep the set versioned, hold part of it out, and grow it from every failure.","part":0},{"p":"BLEU and ROUGE measure n-gram overlap, not truth; exact match is blind to any paraphrase.","part":1},{"p":"LLM-judge biases are position, verbosity and self-preference; swap positions and count a verdict only when both orders agree.","part":2},{"p":"Capture prompt and completion content in traces only by opt-in, because transcripts are sensitive; mask PII before logging.","part":3},{"p":"NIST AI RMF organizes the work into four functions: Govern, Map, Measure and Manage.","part":6}]},"p34m7":{"big":"A production LLM app is a system: a gateway that controls keys, budgets and routing, a cache, served models and tracing, with numbers you can put on each part.","why":"This is the whiteboard interview for LLM-engineering roles and the shape of the Databricks exam; drawing the system and defending each part with numbers is what gets you hired.","parts":[{"t":"Architecture of a production LLM app","gist":"An AI gateway sits between users and models: it holds the keys, checks who is asking, enforces limits and budgets, routes, falls back when a provider fails, logs and masks PII. A semantic cache answers near-repeat questions for free.","core":true},{"t":"Serving local models: vLLM, llama.cpp, SGLang","gist":"vLLM is the production standard for serving open models on NVIDIA GPUs; SGLang shares cache across requests with common prefixes; llama.cpp's llama-server runs GGUF models on consumer hardware. TGI is now in maintenance mode.","core":true},{"t":"Throughput math","gist":"Generating each token reads the whole set of weights, so speed is limited by memory bandwidth. Batching serves many conversations per read, until the KV cache, the memory each conversation's context needs, fills the GPU.","core":true},{"t":"Cost controls","gist":"Cost control lives at the gateway: budgets and alerts with a kill switch, a cheap model by default with escalation, caching, shorter prompts, the Batch API, local serving when it pays, and cost recorded in every trace.","core":true},{"t":"A/B testing prompts","gist":"Offline evals pick the candidates; an A/B test in production decides. Randomize by user, fix the success metric first, compute the sample size, and watch guardrail metrics like latency and cost so nothing else gets worse.","core":true},{"t":"The Databricks exam, mapped","gist":"How the Databricks exam's six weighted sections map to this domain's modules, plus the format: forty-five questions in 90 minutes for $200. Practice on a trial workspace so the product names are things you've clicked.","core":false},{"t":"Portfolio projects that get interviews","gist":"Interviewers ask what you shipped, how you evaluated it and what it cost. Build a RAG assistant, a bounded agent with your own MCP server, a fine-tune with its before/after table, and a one-page architecture doc.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"The gateway holds the provider keys so clients never do, and enforces rate limits and budgets per user and per feature.","part":0},{"p":"vLLM serves an OpenAI-compatible API on port 8000, with PagedAttention and continuous batching.","part":1},{"p":"Single-stream decode is about bandwidth ÷ weight bytes: about 1,000 GB/s over 4.5 GB gives about 220 tokens per second in theory.","part":2},{"p":"KV cache per token is 2 × layers × KV heads × head dimension × bytes: 128 KB for Llama-3-8B, so 8K tokens hold 1 GB.","part":2},{"p":"Detecting a lift from 60% to 65% success needs about 1,470 users per arm; randomize by user, not by request.","part":4}]},"p35m1":{"big":"Production ML fails mostly around the model, in problem framing, data and infrastructure, so MLOps is the practice of running the whole system, not just training a model.","why":"Employers pay ML engineers to make models work in production, not to train them. Framing the problem with a business metric, cost matrix and baseline first keeps a strong notebook score from losing money.","parts":[{"t":"The evidence: most ML projects fail","gist":"A 2024 RAND study concluded that more than 80 percent of AI projects fail. Four of its five root causes are about the problem, the data and the infrastructure; only one is about the model itself.","core":true},{"t":"The lifecycle is a loop","gist":"ML work is a loop: scope, collect data, build features, train, deploy, monitor, retrain, and back to scoping when the world changes. Each arrow is a place the system can rot, and each later module covers one stretch.","core":true},{"t":"Hidden technical debt in ML systems","gist":"Google's 'hidden technical debt' paper names how ML systems rot: features that affect each other, unknown systems reading your predictions, unstable inputs, tangled pipelines and missing monitoring. The rest of this domain is the cure.","core":true},{"t":"Problem framing and the baseline","gist":"Before any model, map the business metric to the model metric, price false positives and false negatives, and set a baseline to beat. With 0.2% fraud, 'never fraud' scores 99.8% accuracy, so accuracy can mislead.","core":true},{"t":"MLOps maturity levels","gist":"Teams mature from level 0, manual notebooks, to level 1, automated training pipelines, to level 2, where CI/CD automation builds, tests and deploys the pipeline itself. Moving a team from 0 to 1 is the most valuable step.","core":true},{"t":"Roles and the market","gist":"Data engineers own pipelines, data scientists frame problems, ML engineers turn models into reliable services, and platform engineers build shared tooling. The 2026 market is strong but tests systems skills, not model trivia.","core":false},{"t":"How this maps to the exam","gist":"How the AWS ML Engineer Associate exam's four domains follow this lifecycle, and why its favorite phrases are 'least operational overhead' and 'most cost-effective'.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"RAND (2024): more than 80% of AI projects fail, roughly twice the rate of ordinary IT projects; only one of five root causes is modeling.","part":0},{"p":"CACE means Changing Anything Changes Everything: adding one feature can shift the weights of all the others.","part":2},{"p":"A model that does not beat the baseline by more than its operating cost should not ship.","part":3},{"p":"With 0.2% fraud, 'never fraud' scores 99.8% accuracy, so imbalanced problems need precision and recall instead.","part":3},{"p":"Google's MLOps levels: 0 is manual, 1 automates the training pipeline (continuous training), 2 automates the pipeline itself with CI/CD.","part":4}]},"p35m2":{"big":"Data is the part of an ML system that breaks most often, so version it, check it, compute features one way for training and serving, and guard against leakage.","why":"In real teams the first thing that breaks a model is the data feeding it, and leakage makes a model look brilliant until the day it ships. Data Preparation is also the biggest exam domain, at 28%.","parts":[{"t":"Pipelines and versioning","gist":"Data needs version control like code, but files are too big for Git. DVC commits a small pointer holding the file's hash, and lakeFS adds Git-style branches over a data lake. Every training run must record its data version.","core":true},{"t":"Feature engineering at scale","gist":"Features turn raw data into model inputs. At scale they must be point-in-time correct, using only data known when the prediction was made, and come from one implementation rather than two that drift apart.","core":true},{"t":"Feature stores","gist":"A feature store keeps one definition per feature, with an offline store for building training sets and an online store for fast lookups at prediction time. A two-person team scoring nightly batches doesn't need one.","core":true},{"t":"Labels and data quality","gist":"A model is only as good as its labels. Measure how well annotators agree with Cohen's kappa, and gate training data with automated checks on schema, statistics and freshness that fail the pipeline loudly.","core":true},{"t":"Train/serve skew","gist":"Train/serve skew is any difference between the features seen in training and in production, such as different code, time windows or null handling. Catch it by comparing logged served features to the training data.","core":true},{"t":"Leakage: five kinds, worked","gist":"Leakage lets a model use information it won't have at prediction time, so it looks brilliant offline and fails live. The five kinds are target, temporal, preprocessing, duplicate or group, and feature-availability leakage.","core":true},{"t":"Data contracts","gist":"A data contract is a code-enforced agreement between a data producer and its users covering schema, meaning, quality, freshness and ownership. It is checked in CI and on arrival, so upstream changes fail loudly.","core":true},{"t":"How this maps to the exam","gist":"MLA-C02's Data Preparation domain, the heaviest at 28%, and the AWS services it expects for ingesting, transforming, validating and storing features.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"DVC commits a small pointer file with the data's hash to Git, while the data itself lives in a cache or S3.","part":0},{"p":"A feature store's offline store builds training sets and its online store serves fresh values, both from one definition, which kills train/serve skew.","part":2},{"p":"Cohen's kappa of 0.8 or better is good annotator agreement; 0.4 means your task definition is broken.","part":3},{"p":"Split time-ordered data by time, not randomly: train on months 1–10, validate on 11, test on 12.","part":5},{"p":"If one feature has suspiciously high importance, or offline metrics look too good, assume leakage until proven otherwise.","part":5}]},"p35m3":{"big":"Training becomes engineering when every run is tracked, reproducible from pinned code, data, environment, configuration and seeds, and budgeted in GPU dollars.","why":"Tracked, reproducible, affordable training is what separates an engineer from a notebook user. It lets you say exactly which data, code and settings produced the model you are about to ship.","parts":[{"t":"Experiment tracking","gist":"Experiment tracking records every run's settings, metrics, files, code version and data version, so you can compare fifty runs and know what produced each. MLflow is the default choice; the habit matters more than the tool.","core":true},{"t":"Reproducibility","gist":"A run is reproducible only when code, data, environment and configuration are pinned and randomness is controlled. Seeds alone aren't enough, and some GPU operations are nondeterministic by design.","core":true},{"t":"Hyperparameter search","gist":"Hyperparameters are knobs set before training. Grid search tries every combination and grows explosively; random or Bayesian search with early stopping finds equally good settings in far fewer runs.","core":true},{"t":"Distributed training: the three parallelisms","gist":"Data parallelism copies the whole model to each GPU and averages gradients. Tensor and pipeline parallelism split the model itself, and sharded optimizers spread memory across GPUs when the model won't fit on one.","core":true},{"t":"GPUs vs CPUs and cloud economics","gist":"GPUs win on dense matrix math; CPUs win on tabular boosting and I/O-heavy work. Pick the smallest instance that fits, use discounted but interruptible spot capacity, and checkpoint so an interruption costs minutes, not the run.","core":true},{"t":"Checkpointing","gist":"A checkpoint saves everything needed to resume: weights, optimizer and scheduler state, random-number states, epoch and step. Save regularly, write atomically so a crash can't corrupt it, and resume rather than restart.","core":false},{"t":"AutoML, honestly assessed","gist":"AutoML quickly produces a strong tabular baseline and tunes well, but it can't frame the problem, invent domain features or notice leakage. Use it to set the bar your own model must clear, not to skip the thinking.","core":true},{"t":"How this maps to the exam","gist":"What the MLA-C02 exam's Domains 2 and 3 test on training, tuning, spot instances with checkpoints and reproducibility.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Reproducible means code, data, environment and configuration pinned plus controlled randomness; a seed alone controls one of the five.","part":1},{"p":"A grid of 5 × 5 × 4 is 100 runs, 33 hours at 20 minutes each; random or Bayesian search with ASHA needs far fewer.","part":2},{"p":"Mixed-precision Adam needs about 16 bytes per parameter, so a 7B model needs about 112 GB, more than one 80 GB GPU.","part":3},{"p":"Use data parallelism when the model fits on one GPU; shard with FSDP or ZeRO only when it does not.","part":3},{"p":"A g5.xlarge costs about $1.006 an hour on demand and about $0.44 on spot; checkpoint so interruptions cost minutes.","part":4}]},"p35m4":{"big":"Serving turns a model into a safe, fast, replaceable service: load only safe formats, pick a pattern by latency, size with Little's law and release gradually through a registry.","why":"Serving is where a model meets users and attackers. Doing it well means answering fast at peak load, surviving a bad release, and never running code hidden inside a downloaded file.","parts":[{"t":"Model formats: what you are really loading","gist":"Some model files are programs in disguise: loading a pickle can run whatever code the file contains. Use safetensors or ONNX for anything from outside, keep weights_only=True, and use pickle only for your own artifacts.","core":true},{"t":"Serving patterns","gist":"Choose how to serve from two questions: how fast does someone need the answer, and how fresh must the features be? If nobody waits, batch scoring is simplest and cheapest; otherwise online, streaming, asynchronous or on-device.","core":true},{"t":"FastAPI + Docker walkthrough","gist":"A small FastAPI app in a pinned, non-root Docker container is the basic unit of serving. Load the model once at startup, validate input, return the model version with every answer, and load-test it.","core":true},{"t":"Serving frameworks and managed endpoints","gist":"Triton, BentoML, KServe and SageMaker endpoints run that container at scale; TorchServe is no longer actively maintained. Underneath them all, the endpoint is just a pointer to a versioned artifact.","core":false},{"t":"Latency and throughput arithmetic","gist":"Little's law says concurrency equals throughput times latency, which tells you how many workers you need. Plan for 60% utilization, and judge latency by percentiles like p99, because the slow tail is what users feel.","core":true},{"t":"Autoscaling, canary, shadow and the registry","gist":"Release new models gradually. A shadow copies live traffic to the new model but throws its answers away; a canary serves real users a growing share with automatic rollback. The registry decides which version is approved.","core":true},{"t":"How this maps to the exam","gist":"MLA-C02 Domain 3: choosing real-time, serverless, asynchronous or batch endpoints, autoscaling, pushing containers to ECR, and deployment strategies such as canary and shadow.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Loading an untrusted pickle is remote code execution; use safetensors or ONNX across trust boundaries and keep weights_only=True.","part":0},{"p":"If nobody waits for the answer, serve in batch; it removes most of the operational burden.","part":1},{"p":"At 20 ms per request one worker handles 50 per second, so 400 per second needs 8 workers, or 14 at 60% utilization.","part":4},{"p":"Plan on p99, not the mean: a p50 of 20 ms with a p99 of 400 ms means one user in a hundred waits twenty times longer.","part":4},{"p":"A shadow mirrors traffic and discards responses, with no user risk; a canary serves real users 5%, then 25%, then 100%.","part":5}]},"p35m5":{"big":"A model can be up and fast yet wrong for months, so measure drift, join late labels to predictions, set SLOs, and retrain or roll back based on diagnosis.","why":"Unlike ordinary software, an ML service can be up and fast yet wrong for months. Monitoring measures how fast the model goes stale and what to do when it does, and it is 24% of the exam.","parts":[{"t":"Three kinds of change","gist":"Inputs can shift (data drift), or what the inputs mean can change (concept drift). But the most common cause is plain upstream breakage, like a renamed column or a unit change, so monitoring starts with the data contract.","core":true},{"t":"Detecting drift: PSI","gist":"PSI compares how data falls into bins now versus in training. Below 0.1 means no meaningful shift, 0.1 to 0.25 means investigate, and above 0.25 means act.","core":true},{"t":"Detecting drift: the KS test and friends","gist":"The KS test measures the biggest gap between two distributions, but with huge samples even trivial gaps look 'significant', so pair it with an effect-size threshold. Weight drift alerts by how much the model relies on that feature.","core":true},{"t":"Performance monitoring with delayed labels","gist":"True labels often arrive days, months or years later. Log every prediction with its features, model version and an ID so labels can be joined back later, and watch quick proxies like the spread of predicted scores meanwhile.","core":true},{"t":"SLIs, SLOs and alerting for ML services","gist":"Set measurable targets (SLOs) for availability, p99 latency, freshness and quality. Alert a human only on symptoms that need action now, send the rest to a daily report, and link every alert to a runbook.","core":true},{"t":"Retraining triggers and pipelines","gist":"Retraining should be an automated pipeline fired by a schedule, drift, a performance drop or new data. It registers a new model only if it beats the champion. An upstream unit change needs a data fix instead.","core":true},{"t":"Rollbacks and incident response for models","gist":"Because registry versions are immutable, rolling back is just repointing the endpoint to the previous approved version. In an incident, check upstream data first, then features, model and infrastructure, and fall back to the simple baseline if needed.","core":true},{"t":"How this maps to the exam","gist":"MLA-C02 Domain 4: SageMaker Model Monitor, Clarify, CloudWatch alarms and EventBridge rules that start a retraining pipeline when drift is detected.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Data drift is a change in the inputs, P(X); concept drift is a change in the relationship P(Y|X).","part":0},{"p":"PSI thresholds: below 0.1 no meaningful shift, 0.1–0.25 moderate (investigate), above 0.25 significant (act).","part":1},{"p":"With a million rows, a trivial KS difference is 'significant'; require an effect-size threshold, for example alert when D exceeds 0.1.","part":2},{"p":"A prediction store logs each prediction's features, timestamp, model version and ID so late labels can be joined back.","part":3},{"p":"Drift does not automatically mean retrain: diagnose first, because an upstream unit or schema change needs a data fix.","part":5}]},"p35m6":{"big":"Responsible ML means measuring fairness by group outcomes, explaining predictions, protecting training data, defending against attacks and untrusted model files, and documenting it for governance.","why":"An ML engineer is accountable both to the people a model can harm and to those who want to steal or subvert it. This is where a security background becomes an advantage few ML engineers have.","parts":[{"t":"Fairness metrics and bias audits","gist":"Fairness is measured by comparing outcomes across groups, for example the disparate impact ratio, where below 0.8 flags adverse impact. When base rates differ, the metrics can't all hold at once, so an audit documents a choice.","core":true},{"t":"Explainability: SHAP and LIME","gist":"SHAP splits one prediction into exact contributions from each feature; LIME fits a small simple model around one example. Both explain what the model did, not what causes outcomes in the world.","core":true},{"t":"Model cards and datasheets","gist":"A model card is a short document shipped with a model: intended use, out-of-scope uses, data, metrics broken down by subgroup, caveats and owner. A datasheet answers the same kind of questions for a dataset.","core":true},{"t":"Privacy: differential privacy and federated learning","gist":"Models memorize, so attackers can sometimes tell who was in the training data. Differential privacy adds calibrated noise so one record barely changes the output; federated learning keeps raw data local and shares only model updates.","core":true},{"t":"Adversarial attacks and model theft, defensively","gist":"Attackers may craft inputs to fool a model, poison its training data, clone it through its API, or infer who was in the training set. Each has known defenses, and practice belongs only on systems you're authorized to test.","core":true},{"t":"Supply chain: a downloaded model is code you did not write","gist":"A downloaded model is code you didn't write, and malicious ones have been found on public hubs. Accept only safetensors or ONNX, pin by hash, scan, verify signatures and load untrusted models in a sandbox.","core":true},{"t":"Governance: NIST AI RMF and the EU AI Act","gist":"NIST's voluntary AI RMF organizes the work as Govern, Map, Measure and Manage. The EU AI Act is law with risk tiers, and its Annex III high-risk obligations now start on 2 December 2027.","core":true},{"t":"How this maps to the exam","gist":"How MLA-C02 threads bias detection, Clarify, model cards and platform security such as IAM, KMS and VPC endpoints through its domains.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Four-fifths rule: a disparate impact ratio below 0.8 is evidence of adverse impact; 60% versus 45% approval gives 0.75.","part":0},{"p":"Removing the protected attribute doesn't make a model fair; proxies like zip code carry it back in, so measure outcomes by group.","part":0},{"p":"SHAP shows what moved the model's prediction, not what causes the outcome, and it can perfectly explain an unfair model.","part":1},{"p":"Differential privacy gives a provable bound on what one record can change; smaller ε is more private and less accurate.","part":3},{"p":"Treat downloaded models like code: safetensors or ONNX only, pin by content hash, scan, and load untrusted ones in a sandbox.","part":5}]},"p35m7":{"big":"An MLOps platform is a set of jobs, from storage and features through training, registry, serving and monitoring to retraining, and each job has an AWS and an open-source tool.","why":"This is the architecture you draw in interviews and the pipeline you build for the capstone. Knowing each job lets you pick up either stack's products quickly and keep the monthly bill under control.","parts":[{"t":"A reference architecture on AWS","gist":"On AWS, data lands in S3, features go to SageMaker Feature Store, training jobs are tracked and approved in the Model Registry, endpoints serve, and Model Monitor with EventBridge triggers retraining pipelines. IAM, KMS and a VPC secure it.","core":true},{"t":"The open-source twin","gist":"Every AWS piece has an open-source counterpart on Kubernetes: MinIO with DVC or lakeFS, Feast, MLflow, Kubeflow Pipelines, KServe, Prometheus and Evidently. Learn the job each row does and either product follows quickly.","core":true},{"t":"Cost management","gist":"ML money leaks through idle endpoints and notebooks, on-demand GPUs, oversized instances and forgotten checkpoints. Scale to zero where traffic is intermittent, use spot for training, stop idle notebooks, and set a budget alarm first.","core":true},{"t":"CI/CD/CT for ML","gist":"CI for ML also tests data and model behavior, CD deploys through a canary, and CT, continuous training, retrains on a trigger and registers a challenger. Secrets stay out of the repo by using OIDC federation into an IAM role.","core":true},{"t":"The exam: AWS Certified Machine Learning Engineer – Associate","gist":"MLA-C02 replaced MLA-C01 and adds Bedrock, RAG, agents and LLMOps: 65 questions in 130 minutes, $150, 720 to pass. Google's PMLE and the Databricks ML Associate are alternatives if your target employer uses them.","core":false},{"t":"The 12-week plan","gist":"A week-by-week plan pairing each module with reading, a lab and the exam domain it serves, ending with timed practice exams, a published repo and a booked exam.","core":false},{"t":"Exam strategy and how this maps","gist":"Read for constraint words: 'least operational overhead' points to the managed service, 'most cost-effective' to spot, serverless or batch, 'no customer impact' to shadow. Eliminate answers with public data or wide IAM policies.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Each row of the AWS-to-open-source mapping is one job; learn the job and both columns become products you can pick up quickly.","part":1},{"p":"Two always-on ml.m5.large endpoints cost 2 × $0.115 × 730 h = $167.90 a month, because idle instances cost the same at 3 a.m.","part":2},{"p":"CT, continuous training, is the part only ML has: the pipeline retrains on a trigger and registers a challenger.","part":3},{"p":"ML CI adds behavioral tests: invariance (changing a name doesn't change the score) and directional (more splice loss doesn't lower predicted failure).","part":3},{"p":"Exam cues: 'intermittent traffic' means serverless, 'no customer impact' means shadow, 'large payloads or long processing' means asynchronous.","part":6}]},"p36m1":{"big":"Analytical data is copied out of transactional databases into columnar, partitioned files or warehouses, because questions that scan billions of rows need a different shape than apps do.","why":"Every dashboard, every model and every review of where sensitive data sits depends on this plumbing, and choosing the right format and layout can make the same query fifty times cheaper.","parts":[{"t":"OLTP versus OLAP, with numbers","gist":"OLTP databases handle many tiny transactions, stored row by row. OLAP queries scan billions of rows but only a few columns, so columnar stores, which read just those columns and compress them well, finish in seconds.","core":true},{"t":"The modern data stack","gist":"Data flows from sources through ingestion, storage, transformation and orchestration to serving. Batch runs on a schedule over a fixed chunk; streaming handles each event as it happens and earns its complexity only when value decays in minutes.","core":true},{"t":"ETL versus ELT","gist":"ETL transforms data before loading it; ELT loads raw data first and transforms it with SQL inside the warehouse. ELT is the modern default, partly because keeping the raw copy lets you fix a bug by re-running.","core":true},{"t":"Lake, warehouse, lakehouse","gist":"A warehouse is a managed analytical database; a lake is cheap files in object storage that can turn into a 'swamp'. A lakehouse adds a table format like Delta or Iceberg, giving the lake transactions, schema enforcement and time travel.","core":true},{"t":"File formats, with the arithmetic","gist":"CSV and JSON are row-based text: big and slow to read. Parquet and ORC store data by column with statistics, so readers skip what they don't need; the same query costs about $0.50 on CSV and a cent on Parquet.","core":true},{"t":"Partitioning","gist":"Partitioning stores files in folders by a column, usually date, so a query for one day reads one folder. Don't over-partition: thousands of tiny files cost more to list than the data costs to read.","core":true},{"t":"On the exams","gist":"Where the AWS DEA-C01 and Databricks associate exams test formats, CSV-to-Parquet conversion, batch versus streaming, data stores and the lakehouse.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"OLTP means many tiny transactions on a few rows; OLAP means few queries scanning many rows but only a few columns.","part":0},{"p":"ELT loads raw data first and transforms it in the warehouse; keeping the raw copy lets you fix a bug by re-running the transformation.","part":2},{"p":"A lakehouse is lake files plus a table format, Delta Lake, Iceberg or Hudi, adding ACID commits, schema enforcement and time travel.","part":3},{"p":"Athena bills $5 per terabyte scanned: a two-column query costs about $0.50 over 100 GB of CSV and about a cent on Parquet.","part":4},{"p":"Partition by the coarsest column your queries always filter on, usually date, and keep files in the 128 MB to 1 GB range.","part":5}]},"p36m2":{"big":"An orchestrator runs a graph of dependent tasks on schedule, and making every task idempotent is what lets retries and backfills run safely without doubling data.","why":"Orchestration and idempotency let a pipeline run every night for years and be fixed in minutes when a crew uploads late, without anything being loaded twice.","parts":[{"t":"Apache Airflow","gist":"Airflow runs DAGs, graphs of tasks with no loops, written in Python. The tricky part is time: the run for the 25th's data starts after that day ends, on the 26th, and its logical date is the 25th.","core":true},{"t":"Idempotency, retries and alerts","gist":"A task is idempotent if running it twice leaves the same result as once, like replacing a day's partition or a MERGE on a key. Only then are retries safe. Alert on failures and on stale tables.","core":true},{"t":"Incremental loads and change data capture","gist":"Incremental loads pull only rows changed since the last run, using a timestamp watermark, but they miss deletes. Change data capture, such as Debezium reading the database's own transaction log, sees every insert, update and delete.","core":true},{"t":"Data contracts and schema evolution","gist":"A data contract is a versioned agreement on schema, meaning, freshness and an owner. Adding an optional field can stay compatible; renaming, retyping or removing a required column is breaking and needs a new version.","core":true},{"t":"The as-built pipeline, end to end","gist":"The daily as-built DAG waits for the files, validates and quarantines bad rows, loads staging, merges into the fact table, runs tests and publishes. Because the merge is idempotent, a late upload just triggers a one-day backfill.","core":true},{"t":"On the exams","gist":"Where AWS DEA-C01 tests orchestration (MWAA, Step Functions, Glue workflows) and replayability, and the Databricks exam's Lakeflow Jobs section.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"The run for data interval 2026-10-25 starts after the interval ends, at 06:00 on the 26th; its logical_date is the 25th.","part":0},{"p":"Idempotent means running twice leaves the same state as once: delete and re-insert the partition or MERGE on the key, never plain INSERT.","part":1},{"p":"Retry transient faults like a network blip with backoff; don't retry deterministic faults like a schema violation.","part":1},{"p":"Polling updated_at misses deletes; log-based CDC such as Debezium reads the write-ahead log and sees every change.","part":2},{"p":"Backward compatible: new readers read old data. Forward compatible: old readers read new data. Full: both.","part":3}]},"p36m3":{"big":"Spark runs one task per data partition in parallel; the expensive part is the shuffle, so good jobs move as little data across the network as possible.","why":"Reading a Spark plan is the professional's reflex for finding the shuffles that slow a job, and knowing when one machine wins on speed, cost and simplicity can save you a cluster.","parts":[{"t":"The machine model","gist":"A driver plans the job and executors do the work: each DataFrame is split into partitions, and each core runs one task per partition. DataFrames beat raw RDDs because the optimizer knows the columns and can skip work.","core":true},{"t":"Lazy evaluation and the DAG scheduler","gist":"Transformations like filter and join only build a plan; nothing runs until an action like count or write. That laziness lets Spark optimize the whole query, then cut it into stages wherever data must move.","core":true},{"t":"Shuffles, and why they hurt","gist":"A shuffle moves rows across the network so matching keys meet, as in groupBy or join, and the next stage waits for it. Adaptive query execution fixes most sizing problems at runtime; a skewed hot key remains the killer.","core":true},{"t":"Joins","gist":"A broadcast join copies a small table to every executor, so the big table never moves. Spark does this automatically under 10 MB; otherwise it shuffles and sorts both sides, which for a 1 TB table is far costlier.","core":true},{"t":"A worked PySpark job","gist":"A short PySpark job that prunes partitions, broadcasts a small table and groups once, so its plan should show exactly one exchange. Read the plan with explain() before you run it.","core":false},{"t":"Sizing arithmetic","gist":"Aim for 128 MB to 1 GB per partition and about five cores per executor. Of each executor's memory, 300 MB is reserved and 0.6 of the rest forms the shared pool for shuffles, sorts and cached data.","core":true},{"t":"When not to use Spark","gist":"Spark carries the overhead of a JVM, a scheduler and a cluster. Under a few hundred gigabytes, DuckDB or Polars on one machine is faster, cheaper and simpler; Spark wins when data truly outgrows one machine.","core":true},{"t":"On the exams","gist":"Where the Databricks associate exam tests DataFrames, Spark SQL and reading the Spark UI, and where DEA-C01 asks you to choose EMR or Glue.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Each executor core runs one task per partition at a time: 5 GB at 128 MB per partition is about 40 partitions, three waves on 16 cores.","part":0},{"p":"Transformations are lazy; only an action such as count, show or write makes Spark do any work.","part":1},{"p":"groupBy, join, distinct, repartition and window functions cause a shuffle, which ends a stage.","part":2},{"p":"Spark broadcasts tables under 10 MB by default; broadcasting a 5 MB table to 200 executors moves 1 GB instead of a terabyte.","part":3},{"p":"Below a few hundred GB, DuckDB or Polars on one machine beats Spark on speed, cost and simplicity.","part":6}]},"p36m4":{"big":"Kafka is a replicated, append-only log kept for days: same-key records stay in order on one partition, and each consumer reads at its own pace from its saved offset.","why":"Streaming is how alerts, IoT data and change data capture actually flow. Knowing partitions, consumer groups, lag and delivery guarantees lets you size a stream and diagnose it when it stalls.","parts":[{"t":"The log","gist":"A topic is split into partitions, each an ordered list of records numbered by offset. The record's key picks its partition, so order holds per key only. With three replicas, acks=all and min.insync.replicas=2, one broker can die without data loss.","core":true},{"t":"Consumers, groups and lag","gist":"Consumers in a group split the partitions, one member per partition, so you can't have more active consumers than partitions. Lag, how far a group is behind the newest record, is the one metric to alert on.","core":true},{"t":"Delivery guarantees","gist":"Commit before processing and a crash loses records (at-most-once); commit after and a crash makes duplicates (at-least-once). Exactly-once works inside Kafka, but a database or file sink must be idempotent or transactional too.","core":true},{"t":"Connect, Schema Registry and stream processors","gist":"Kafka Connect runs ready-made connectors that pull from databases or push to stores like S3. Schema Registry blocks breaking schema changes. Kafka Streams, Flink and Spark Structured Streaming do the processing, each with its own model.","core":false},{"t":"Windows and watermarks","gist":"Stream totals need windows: tumbling, sliding or session. Use event time, when it happened, not arrival time. A watermark says how late data may arrive before a window is finalized, trading memory and speed against completeness.","core":true},{"t":"The sensor stream, worked","gist":"Five hundred sensors at one reading per second is 100 KB/s, 8.6 GB a day, and about 181 GB for a week at three replicas. A windowed job, an alerting service and an archive all read the same topic.","core":true},{"t":"On the exams","gist":"DEA-C01's Kinesis and MSK questions use the same ideas with shards instead of partitions, and the Databricks exam covers Structured Streaming and Auto Loader.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Kafka orders records only within a partition; hash(key) mod partitions keeps each key's records on one partition, in order.","part":0},{"p":"A consumer group has at most one active consumer per partition: twelve partitions feed at most twelve, and a thirteenth idles.","part":1},{"p":"Lag is the log-end offset minus the committed offset, and it is the streaming metric you alert on.","part":1},{"p":"'Effectively once' means at-least-once delivery plus an idempotent sink, and it is what you will build most often.","part":2},{"p":"With a 30-second watermark, once the latest event time seen is T, windows ending before T − 30 s are finalized.","part":4}]},"p36m5":{"big":"Modern warehouses and lakehouses separate cheap columnar storage from rented compute, so cost depends on how much data a query scans and how long compute runs.","why":"This is where most data-engineering money is spent: the same SQL can cost two cents or forty dollars depending on layout and settings, and the cost arithmetic is what employers notice first.","parts":[{"t":"Three warehouses","gist":"Snowflake, BigQuery and Redshift all keep columnar data separate from compute, and all skip data their metadata proves a query doesn't need. They differ mainly in billing: compute time, bytes scanned, or node or RPU hours.","core":true},{"t":"Open table formats","gist":"Delta Lake and Iceberg add a metadata log over Parquet files that records exactly which files make up the table right now. That gives transactions, time travel and schema evolution on plain object storage; compaction fixes small files.","core":true},{"t":"Medallion architecture","gist":"The medallion layout has three layers: bronze is raw and never edited, silver is cleaned and conformed, gold is shaped for dashboards and models. Each layer is built only from the one before, by versioned code.","core":true},{"t":"dbt","gist":"dbt writes the transform step as SELECT statements, and ref() links them into a DAG. Materializations decide whether each becomes a view, table or incremental table, YAML tests check the data, and dbt build runs it all in order.","core":true},{"t":"Cost control, with arithmetic","gist":"BigQuery charges by bytes scanned, so partition filters and column lists cut the bill. Snowflake charges for warehouse time, so auto-suspend matters: a Medium warehouse left running all month costs about $8,640.","core":true},{"t":"On the exams","gist":"Why the Databricks associate exam is mostly Delta Lake and medallion work, and what DEA-C01 asks about Redshift keys, S3 storage classes and formats.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Delta's _delta_log transaction log gives ACID and time travel on S3; VACUUM removes unreferenced files after a default seven days.","part":1},{"p":"Bronze is raw, append-only and never edited, so you can always reprocess silver from it when silver has a bug.","part":2},{"p":"dbt build runs models, tests, snapshots and seeds in DAG order and stops downstream work when a test fails.","part":3},{"p":"BigQuery on-demand bills $6.25 per TiB scanned, so SELECT * on a 10 TB table costs $62.50 per query.","part":4},{"p":"A Medium Snowflake warehouse (4 credits/hour) left on all month uses 2,880 credits, about $8,640; set auto-suspend.","part":4}]},"p36m6":{"big":"Data is trustworthy only when it is tested before publishing, traceable through lineage, guarded by least privilege, masking and encryption, and watched so failures are caught before users notice.","why":"A pipeline can run green for a month while quietly loading nulls. These habits catch bad data before it reaches a dashboard, and they are where data engineering meets your security career.","parts":[{"t":"Data quality and where to test","gist":"Test at three points: at ingestion against the contract, after transformation for keys, totals and possible ranges, and before publishing for freshness and volume. Write-audit-publish swaps data into the consumer-facing table only after the checks pass.","core":true},{"t":"Lineage and catalogs","gist":"Lineage is the map of where each table came from and what uses it, so during an incident you know the cause, what was affected and who to tell. Generate catalogs automatically rather than hand-typing them.","core":true},{"t":"Access control, masking and PII","gist":"Grant access by role with least privilege, then protect sensitive columns with row filters and column masks. Classify personal data at ingestion, keep only what you need, and delete it physically with DELETE plus VACUUM.","core":true},{"t":"Encryption, keys and secrets","gist":"Data at rest is encrypted by default, so the real question is who controls the keys; customer-managed KMS keys add audit and revocation. Use TLS in transit, keep secrets in a secrets manager, and enforce MFA.","core":true},{"t":"Observability and SLAs","gist":"Watch five signals: freshness, volume, schema, distribution and lineage, and alert on deviation, not just task failure. An SLI is the measurement, an SLO the target, an SLA the target written into an agreement.","core":true},{"t":"The data post-mortem","gist":"When trust breaks, run a blameless post-mortem: timeline, why monitoring missed it, impact from lineage, a root cause that is a process rather than a person, the fix, and owned action items, usually a new test and monitor.","core":true},{"t":"On the exams","gist":"DEA-C01's security, governance and operations domains and the Databricks exam's Governance and Security section: Lake Formation, KMS, Macie, Glue Data Quality and Unity Catalog.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Write-audit-publish: write to a staging table or branch, run the audits, then swap it into the consumer-facing name.","part":0},{"p":"Masking is irreversible redaction, tokenization is reversible through a vault, and a keyed hash (HMAC) gives pseudonyms that still join.","part":2},{"p":"Deleting a lakehouse row only marks it removed in the log; the bytes stay in Parquet until VACUUM rewrites or deletes the file.","part":2},{"p":"Encryption at rest doesn't stop a leaked credential; MFA, least privilege and short-lived credentials do.","part":3},{"p":"SLI is the measurement, SLO the target, SLA the SLO in an agreement with consequences; at 99%, three misses a year are allowed.","part":4}]},"p36m7":{"big":"A data platform is declared as code, runs on shared or managed infrastructure, is measured in dollars per run, and fits on one page an interviewer can follow.","why":"This is the difference between running a notebook and owning a platform: you can stand it up again in a new account tomorrow, prove what it costs, and show a repository that gets you hired.","parts":[{"t":"Infrastructure as code for data","gist":"Click-built platforms can't be reviewed, reproduced or rolled back. Declare every bucket, role, job and grant in version-controlled code, with Terraform or CloudFormation on AWS and Asset Bundles on Databricks, deployed through CI/CD.","core":true},{"t":"Kubernetes for data workloads","gist":"Spark, Airflow and Kafka all run on Kubernetes, but size Spark executor pods with memory overhead or they get killed mid-shuffle. Often a managed service is the better call; a two-person team usually should buy, not build.","core":true},{"t":"Cost management","gist":"Tag everything, set budgets, and track cost per run and per terabyte. Pull the biggest levers first: Parquet and partitioning, stopping idle compute, right-sizing, spot instances, compaction and cheaper storage, and only then tune code.","core":true},{"t":"Performance tuning checklist","gist":"A quick checklist of habits from earlier modules: right-sized Parquet files, coarse partitions, early filters, broadcast joins, skew fixes, built-in functions over Python UDFs, incremental idempotent loads and right-sized warehouses.","core":false},{"t":"Reference architecture: AWS","gist":"On AWS, data lands in S3, is cataloged by Glue, transformed by Glue or EMR, and queried with Athena and Redshift, with Lake Formation, KMS, CloudTrail and CloudWatch around it. That covers most of DEA-C01's service list.","core":true},{"t":"Reference architecture: Databricks","gist":"On Databricks, Lakeflow Connect and Auto Loader land bronze Delta tables, Declarative Pipelines build silver and gold, SQL warehouses serve BI, and Unity Catalog governs it all. The storage underneath is still your own object storage.","core":true},{"t":"The two exams","gist":"The Databricks Data Engineer Associate is 45 questions in 90 minutes for $200; AWS DEA-C01 is 65 questions in 130 minutes for $150, pass at 720. Take Databricks first, then AWS.","core":false},{"t":"The portfolio","gist":"One public repository per lab plus the capstone, each README showing the architecture, cost per run, lineage and tests. Make the as-built platform your flagship, because a telecom field dataset sets you apart.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"A platform that cannot be redeployed from code cannot be trusted or audited; buckets, catalogs, grants and jobs are infrastructure too.","part":0},{"p":"Size Spark executor pods as executor memory plus 10% overhead, minimum 384 MB, or Kubernetes will kill them mid-shuffle.","part":1},{"p":"The top cost lever is converting to Parquet and partitioning, which cuts bytes scanned 10–50×; next comes stopping idle compute.","part":2},{"p":"A 10-DPU Glue job for 20 minutes is 3.33 DPU-hours at $0.44, about $1.47 a run; the Flex class cuts it to $0.97.","part":2},{"p":"AWS reference flow: S3, then Glue Data Catalog, then Glue or EMR, then Athena and Redshift, governed by Lake Formation, KMS and CloudTrail.","part":4}]},"p37m1":{"big":"Across a network, a missing reply could mean three things you can't tell apart, so every operation must be safe to retry and every timeout is a decision under uncertainty.","why":"Every cloud, cluster and network you build or test is distributed. Knowing what breaks and why lets you design retries, timeouts and failovers that help instead of making outages worse.","parts":[{"t":"The eight fallacies","gist":"Eight comfortable assumptions about networks are false: that they are reliable, instant, unlimited, secure, unchanging, run by one administrator, free and uniform. Each is a production incident waiting to happen, with a known engineering response.","core":true},{"t":"Partial failure","gist":"When no reply arrives, the request was lost, the server crashed or is slow, or the reply was lost, and you can't tell which. So every operation must be safe to repeat, often using an idempotency key.","core":true},{"t":"Latency numbers every engineer should know","gist":"Memorize the orders of magnitude, from a 0.5 ns cache hit to a 150 ms ocean round trip. Light in fiber costs roughly 5 µs per kilometer, and no cache, language or budget beats that.","core":true},{"t":"Failure detectors and timeouts","gist":"You can't know a node is dead, only that it stopped talking. A short timeout detects crashes fast but can misfire and elect a second leader, a split brain; a long one is safe but slow.","core":true},{"t":"The tail at scale","gist":"When one request fans out to many servers and waits for all, rare slowness becomes common: 100 servers each slow 1% of the time make about 63% of requests slow. Design for tail latency, not averages.","core":true},{"t":"The end-to-end argument","gist":"A guarantee like 'the file arrived intact' can only be fully provided at the endpoints, not by lower layers like TCP. So build reliability at the edges: application-level hashes, acknowledgements from the real owner, and your own retries.","core":true},{"t":"Toward the exam","gist":"How CCDAK producer settings like acks and retries, and the AWS reliability pillar's timeouts, backoff and health checks, are responses to this module's fallacies.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"A missing reply has three indistinguishable causes: the request was lost, the server crashed or is slow, or the reply was lost.","part":1},{"p":"Fiber costs about 5 µs per kilometer one way; a datacenter round trip is about 0.5 ms; California to the Netherlands and back is about 150 ms.","part":2},{"p":"etcd ships a 100 ms heartbeat with a 1,000 ms election timeout, which is 10 missed heartbeats.","part":3},{"p":"Fan out to 100 servers each slow 1% of the time and 1 − 0.99^100 ≈ 63% of requests hit a slow one.","part":4},{"p":"TCP retransmits and checksums, yet a file still needs an application-level hash, because reliability is end to end.","part":5}]},"p37m2":{"big":"Machines share no clock, so order across them is built from logical clocks, and each system sits on a consistency ladder where stronger guarantees cost coordination and latency.","why":"Every database, cache and replicated service sits somewhere on this ladder. Writing down the consistency each operation needs, and buying no more than that, is a core skill and a standard interview question.","parts":[{"t":"Physical clocks: what a quartz crystal and NTP actually give you","gist":"Server clocks drift, and NTP can jump them backwards, so timestamps from two machines can't reliably order events. Measure durations with a monotonic clock; Spanner's TrueTime instead returns a time interval and waits out the uncertainty.","core":true},{"t":"Happens-before and Lamport clocks","gist":"Event a happens before b if it came first in one process, or a sent a message b received, or a chain links them. Lamport clocks respect that order, but they can't tell you when two events were concurrent.","core":true},{"t":"Vector clocks","gist":"Vector clocks keep one counter per process. If neither vector is less than or equal to the other in every slot, the writes were concurrent, a conflict that must be merged rather than settled by timestamp.","core":true},{"t":"The consistency spectrum","gist":"Consistency models form a ladder. Linearizable behaves like a single copy but costs a coordination round trip; sequential, causal and eventual are cheaper and weaker. Session guarantees like read-your-writes are what users actually notice.","core":true},{"t":"CAP, stated precisely, and PACELC","gist":"CAP says that during a network partition you can't guarantee both linearizability and availability; 'pick two' is a misreading, because partitions simply happen. PACELC adds that on normal days you trade latency against consistency.","core":true},{"t":"Read-your-writes with two replicas","gist":"If you read from a follower that lags the leader, your own new comment can vanish. Fixes: read from the leader for a minute after writing, wait until the follower reaches your write's log position, or stick to one replica.","core":true},{"t":"Toward the exam","gist":"How Kafka's per-partition ordering, acks=all and read_committed apply this module on the CCDAK, and how DynamoDB, Aurora and S3 consistency choices appear on the AWS Professional exam.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Never measure a duration with the wall clock; use a monotonic clock such as time.monotonic() or System.nanoTime().","part":0},{"p":"Lamport rule: on receipt, set the clock to max(own, message) + 1. If a → b then C(a) < C(b), but not the reverse.","part":1},{"p":"Vector clocks [1,0] and [0,1]: neither dominates, so the writes were concurrent and must be merged.","part":2},{"p":"CAP: during a partition you choose to refuse requests (C) or to answer with possibly stale data (A).","part":4},{"p":"PACELC: if there is a Partition, trade Availability against Consistency; Else, trade Latency against Consistency.","part":4}]},"p37m3":{"big":"Replication keeps several copies so one machine can die safely; partitioning splits data across machines so none is overloaded; both need rules for who accepts writes and how conflicts resolve.","why":"These are the mechanics inside every scalable datastore. You will size quorums, choose partition keys and debug hot spots on real systems and in interviews.","parts":[{"t":"Leader-based replication","gist":"One leader takes all writes and ships a log of changes to followers. Failover is the danger: the new leader may lack the last asynchronous writes, and an old leader that wakes up can cause a split brain: two leaders.","core":true},{"t":"Multi-leader replication","gist":"With leaders in several places, two sites can change the same thing at once. Resolve conflicts by last-write-wins, which loses data, by keeping both versions for the app to merge, or with a CRDT that merges automatically.","core":true},{"t":"Leaderless replication and quorums","gist":"Leaderless systems write to W of N replicas and read from R. If W + R > N, every read overlaps at least one replica holding the latest acknowledged write. That gives durability and 'probably fresh', not a single copy.","core":true},{"t":"Conflict-free replicated data types","gist":"A CRDT is a data type whose merge works in any order, any number of times, so replicas always converge without coordination. It guarantees convergence, not business correctness, and its metadata must be garbage-collected.","core":true},{"t":"Partitioning strategies","gist":"Range partitioning keeps range scans cheap but piles sequential keys onto one partition; hash partitioning spreads load but kills range queries. Never use hash(key) mod N: going from 4 to 5 nodes moves 80% of the data.","core":true},{"t":"Consistent hashing: the ring","gist":"Consistent hashing puts nodes and keys on one ring, and each key belongs to the next node clockwise. Adding a node moves only about 1/N of the keys, and virtual nodes even out the load.","core":true},{"t":"Rebalancing, routing and secondary indexes","gist":"Rebalance with a fixed partition count, dynamic splitting or tokens per node, but rate-limit automatic moves. Secondary indexes are either local, forcing scatter-gather reads, or global, making writes update another partition.","core":false},{"t":"Hot spots","gist":"Even a fair scheme fails when one key gets most of the traffic. Fix hot spots by redesigning the key, salting it with a random suffix, giving a heavy tenant dedicated capacity, or caching the hot item.","core":true},{"t":"Toward the exam","gist":"How the CCDAK tests partitions, replication factor and the default partitioner, and how the AWS Professional exam tests DynamoDB partitions, Global Tables and quorums.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"DynamoDB Global Tables use last-writer-wins: fine for a user profile, wrong for a bank balance.","part":1},{"p":"W + R > N makes read and write sets overlap (N = 3: W = 2, R = 2), but a quorum is still not linearizable.","part":2},{"p":"With hash mod N, going from 4 to 5 nodes keeps only 4 of every 20 keys in place, so 80% of the data moves.","part":4},{"p":"On a consistent-hashing ring, adding a node moves only about 1/N of the keys; nothing else is touched.","part":5},{"p":"Salting a hot key with a random suffix 0–9 spreads it across ten partitions, at the cost of ten reads to reassemble.","part":7}]},"p37m4":{"big":"Consensus lets nodes agree on one ordered log by majority, so 2f + 1 nodes survive f crashes and never decide two different values.","why":"Kubernetes, Kafka and every cloud control plane rest on consensus. Understanding majorities, terms and fencing lets you size clusters, survive failures and spot unsafe designs.","parts":[{"t":"FLP: why it is impossible, and why we do it anyway","gist":"When message delays have no limit, no fixed-rule protocol can guarantee agreement if one node may crash, since slow looks like dead. Real systems use timeouts: they may stall, but never decide two different values.","core":true},{"t":"Paxos: the intuition","gist":"Paxos agrees on one value in two phases: a proposer first gets a majority to promise, then asks them to accept a value. Any two majorities share a member, so a later proposer finds and keeps an already-chosen value.","core":true},{"t":"Raft in detail","gist":"Raft keeps one leader per numbered term. A follower that misses heartbeats for a random timeout runs for leader, and an entry is committed once a majority has stored it.","core":true},{"t":"ZooKeeper and etcd: consensus as a service","gist":"You'll rarely build consensus yourself; you'll use ZooKeeper or etcd, which already do it. Run 3 or 5 nodes, and keep only small, slow-changing data there, like locks, configuration and membership.","core":true},{"t":"Leases, fencing tokens, and why distributed locks are dangerous","gist":"A lease is a lock that expires, but a paused holder can wake up still thinking it owns it. A fencing token, a number that only goes up, lets the resource reject writes from the stale holder.","core":true},{"t":"Distributed transactions: two-phase commit and sagas","gist":"Two-phase commit makes several participants commit or abort together, but if the coordinator crashes they sit holding locks. Across microservices, use a saga: local steps, each undone by a compensating step if a later one fails.","core":true},{"t":"Toward the exam","gist":"How this module shows up on the CCDAK, CKA and AWS Professional exams, plus interview habits: say majority, fencing token and saga.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Surviving f crashed nodes takes 2f + 1 nodes: 3 nodes tolerate 1 failure, 5 tolerate 2.","part":0},{"p":"Paxos is safe because any two majorities share at least one acceptor, so a later proposer discovers an already-chosen value and re-proposes it.","part":1},{"p":"Raft followers start an election after a randomized timeout with no heartbeat (150–300 ms in the paper); an entry is committed once a majority stores it.","part":2},{"p":"Every lock or lease needs a fencing token: an increasing number the resource checks, rejecting any write with a lower token than it has seen.","part":4},{"p":"Across services, prefer a saga over two-phase commit: each step commits locally, and if a later step fails, compensating steps undo earlier ones in reverse order.","part":5}]},"p37m5":{"big":"Microservices turn function calls into network calls that can partly fail; they pay off when several teams must ship independently, and survive only with timeouts, safe retries and breakers.","why":"This is how modern backends are built and how they break. The same patterns show up on the AWS Professional and Kubernetes exams and in every on-call incident you will work.","parts":[{"t":"Service boundaries: domain-driven design","gist":"Cut services along bounded contexts: parts of the business with their own vocabulary. Give each its own database, and migrate with the strangler fig: a routing front that moves one capability at a time off the monolith.","core":true},{"t":"REST, gRPC or messaging","gist":"Use REST for public APIs and browsers, gRPC for compact, typed internal calls, and messaging for events. Call and wait when the caller needs the answer to continue; otherwise send a message.","core":true},{"t":"Gateways and discovery","gist":"An API gateway is the single front door that handles TLS, authentication, rate limits and routing. Service discovery finds where a service is running right now, and health checks decide which instances get traffic.","core":false},{"t":"Resilience patterns and how they interact","gist":"A few patterns stop one slow service from sinking the rest: deadlines that add up along the path, retries with backoff only for safe-to-repeat calls, circuit breakers that fail fast, and a separate pool per dependency.","core":true},{"t":"Service meshes","gist":"A service mesh moves mutual TLS encryption, retries, timeouts and telemetry out of your code into proxies. It adds roughly a millisecond per hop and a control plane to run, and can't make operations idempotent.","core":true},{"t":"Observability: tracing a request","gist":"A trace follows one request through every service as a tree of timed steps called spans, linked by the traceparent header. OpenTelemetry collects them and Jaeger shows them, so you can see which call made it slow.","core":true},{"t":"Toward the exam","gist":"How these patterns appear on the CCDAK, AWS Professional and CKA exams, and the interview habit of stating each call's timeout, whether it is retried, and what makes the retry safe.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Each service owns its own database; shared tables are a hidden API. Migrate with the strangler fig: move one capability at a time behind a routing facade.","part":0},{"p":"REST for public APIs and browsers, gRPC for internal service-to-service calls, messaging for events and work that need not answer now.","part":1},{"p":"Retry only idempotent calls, at one layer, with exponential backoff and full jitter; three layers of 3 retries each make 27 attempts.","part":3},{"p":"A circuit breaker is closed (passes traffic), open (fails fast without calling) or half-open (lets one trial call through to decide).","part":3},{"p":"A trace is a tree of spans; the W3C traceparent header carries the trace id from service to service.","part":5}]},"p37m6":{"big":"An append-only log is the shared structure behind databases, replication and messaging, and Kafka turns it into a replicated, durable backbone for events between services.","why":"It lets many services share facts like 'order paid' without calling each other, losing them or applying them twice. Kafka and event-driven design are the data backbone of most large companies, and what the CCDAK tests.","parts":[{"t":"Log or queue?","gist":"A log keeps records in order and lets each reader track its own position and replay history; a queue deletes each message once it's acknowledged. Pick a log for event streams, a queue for task distribution.","core":true},{"t":"Inside a partition: segments, the page cache and zero-copy","gist":"A partition is segment files written by sequential appends, and Kafka acknowledges before flushing to disk, so durability comes from copies in different zones. Size partitions from target throughput with headroom, because adding them later breaks per-key order.","core":true},{"t":"Replication: ISR, the high watermark and leader epochs","gist":"A record is committed once every in-sync replica, the copies that are caught up, has it, marked by the high watermark. Use replication factor 3 with min.insync.replicas=2 and acks=all, so one broker can fail with no loss.","core":true},{"t":"Exactly-once, precisely","gist":"Kafka numbers each producer's batches so retried sends are written once, and transactions commit outputs and consumed offsets together. Exactly-once only covers consume, transform, produce inside Kafka; an email or database write outside needs a consumer that ignores duplicates.","core":true},{"t":"Event sourcing and CQRS","gist":"Event sourcing stores what happened as the record of truth and rebuilds current state from those events. CQRS splits the write side from read views built for each query, and those views must ignore events they've already applied.","core":true},{"t":"The outbox pattern","gist":"Writing to the database, then publishing to Kafka, can lose an event if you crash in between. The outbox puts the event in the same database transaction; a relay publishes it, and consumers drop duplicates using an inbox table.","core":true},{"t":"Stream processing: time, windows, joins and state","gist":"Event time is when something happened; processing time is when the engine saw it. A watermark says no older events are expected, so a window can close; checkpoints save state so recovery keeps state updates exactly-once.","core":true},{"t":"Backpressure","gist":"Backpressure lets a slow stage tell a fast one to slow down instead of buffering until memory runs out. In Kafka the log is the buffer, so a slow consumer builds lag. Give every queue a limit and a policy.","core":true},{"t":"Toward the exam","gist":"The CCDAK settings and topics this module covers, from producer and consumer configs to Kafka Streams, Connect and Schema Registry, and the key phrases scenario questions hinge on.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"A log keeps records until retention expires, so many consumer groups can each replay at their own position; a queue deletes acknowledged messages and has no replay.","part":0},{"p":"Use replication factor 3, min.insync.replicas=2 and acks=all. With the default min.insync.replicas=1, acks=all can silently behave like acks=1.","part":2},{"p":"Kafka's exactly-once covers consume–transform–produce inside Kafka with read_committed readers; a payment, email or database row outside needs an idempotent sink.","part":3},{"p":"The outbox pattern fixes the dual write by inserting the event row in the same database transaction as the data; a relay such as Debezium then publishes it.","part":5},{"p":"A watermark is the engine's claim that no events older than T are still expected; a window closes when the watermark passes its end plus any allowed lateness.","part":6}]},"p37m7":{"big":"Running at scale means planning with numbers: size for peak load at 50–70% utilization plus a lost zone, and know what happens to users when any piece, or a region, disappears.","why":"These are the decisions staff engineers and solutions architects are hired to make, and the backbone of the AWS Solutions Architect – Professional exam and of system-design interviews.","parts":[{"t":"Capacity planning arithmetic","gist":"Turn users into requests per second, then use Little's law, requests in flight = arrival rate × time each takes, to size concurrency. Run servers around 50–70% busy, then add 1.5× so losing one of three zones is survivable.","core":true},{"t":"Caching layers and invalidation","gist":"The hit ratio drives everything: going from 0.90 to 0.99 cuts database load tenfold. Default to cache-aside, delete the key on writes rather than set it, and stop stampedes, when an expired hot key sends everyone to the database.","core":true},{"t":"Load balancing algorithms","gist":"Round robin works when everything is uniform, but the best general default is the power of two random choices: pick two servers at random and send to the less loaded. Use consistent hashing when requests must stick to one server.","core":true},{"t":"Rate limiting and load shedding","gist":"A token bucket allows bursts up to its capacity and a steady long-run rate, keeping tenants fair. Load shedding protects the server itself by rejecting the lowest-priority work first when it's overloaded.","core":true},{"t":"Multi-region and disaster recovery","gist":"Design recovery to two numbers: RPO, how much data you may lose, and RTO, how long you may be down. Cross-region sync costs a round trip per commit, so most systems replicate asynchronously and accept an RPO of seconds.","core":true},{"t":"Chaos engineering","gist":"Chaos engineering breaks things on purpose: predict a measured steady state holds, inject a real fault, and limit how much can be hurt. Doing it to systems you don't own or aren't authorized to test is an attack.","core":true},{"t":"SLOs and error budgets as an engineering control","gist":"The error budget is 1 minus the SLO: 99.9% over 30 days allows 43.2 minutes of failure. Budget left, ship; budget spent, freeze risky launches and work on reliability.","core":true},{"t":"One request, end to end","gist":"Following one checkout request through DNS, the edge, the load balancer, checkout, payment and the outbox shows every earlier pattern at work. Every arrow needs a timeout, a retry rule and a reason the retry is safe.","core":true},{"t":"The staff-level system-design interview, done properly","gist":"A strong design interview is about judgment: clarify requirements, estimate out loud, sketch the API and data, walk one request, deep-dive the hardest parts, then run the failure analysis. Put a number on every trade-off.","core":true},{"t":"Toward the exam","gist":"How this module maps onto the AWS Solutions Architect – Professional exam, including the switch from SAP-C02 to SAP-C03 in November 2026, plus links to the CKA and CCDAK.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Little's law: requests in flight = arrival rate × time each takes. Run servers at 50–70% busy, then add 1.5× to survive losing one of three zones.","part":0},{"p":"Cache-aside: on a miss, read the database and fill the cache; on a write, update the database and then delete the key rather than set it.","part":1},{"p":"Power of two random choices: pick two backends at random and send to the less loaded one. It is the best general default.","part":2},{"p":"RPO is how much recent data you may lose; RTO is how long you may be down. Together they define a recovery design.","part":4},{"p":"Error budget = 1 − SLO: a 99.9% SLO over 30 days allows 43.2 minutes of total failure.","part":6}]},"p38m1":{"big":"Numbers are bit patterns, and a processor is a clocked loop that fetches each instruction and runs it through registers, the ALU and memory.","why":"Everything you will ever debug, exploit or defend sits on top of this machine; knowing what one clock tick does makes assembly, compilers and side channels make sense.","parts":[{"t":"Numbers the machine can hold","gist":"Two's complement stores negatives by flipping every bit and adding 1, so one adder handles both signs. IEEE 754 stores real numbers as sign, exponent and fraction, and values like 0.1 can't be stored exactly.","core":true},{"t":"Combinational and sequential logic","gist":"Combinational logic, like gates and adders, computes outputs from current inputs with no memory. Sequential logic remembers: a flip-flop copies its input only on the clock's rising edge, and the clock period must cover the longest path between flip-flops.","core":true},{"t":"The ALU and the three resources","gist":"The ALU is the calculator: it adds, subtracts, applies AND, OR and XOR, shifts and compares. Every instruction uses at most three resources, the register file, the ALU and memory, and that makes diagrams readable.","core":true},{"t":"The single-cycle datapath","gist":"The datapath wires the program counter, instruction memory, registers, ALU and data memory together. In a single-cycle design every instruction takes one clock, so the clock must fit the slowest one: a load, 800 ps with the textbook delays.","core":true},{"t":"Fetch, decode, execute","gist":"The processor repeats one loop forever: fetch the instruction at PC, decode it into control signals, execute it, update PC. Instructions are just bytes in the same memory as data, which enables compilers and also code injection.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"To negate in two's complement, invert every bit and add 1: −5 in 8 bits is 11111011, or 0xFB.","part":0},{"p":"IEEE 754 single precision has 1 sign bit, an 8-bit exponent with bias 127 and a 23-bit fraction; 0.1 has no exact binary form.","part":0},{"p":"A D flip-flop copies its input to its output only at the rising clock edge and holds it otherwise.","part":1},{"p":"Every instruction touches at most three resources: the register file, the ALU and memory.","part":2},{"p":"In a single-cycle datapath the load sets the clock: 200 + 100 + 200 + 200 + 100 = 800 ps, or 1.25 GHz, and an add still waits the full 800.","part":3}]},"p38m2":{"big":"An ISA is the contract between software and hardware, and RISC-V's open one is small and regular: 32 registers, 40 base instructions and six fixed-layout 32-bit formats.","why":"This is 60% of the RVFA exam and the assembly you will read in Ghidra when RISC-V binaries show up, and following the ABI stops linked compiled code from corrupting your values.","parts":[{"t":"Registers and the calling convention","gist":"RISC-V has 32 registers, x0 to x31; x0 always reads 0. By convention a0–a7 carry arguments, ra the return address and sp the stack; a called function may destroy t registers but must preserve s registers.","core":true},{"t":"The 40 base instructions","gist":"Only loads and stores touch memory; ALU instructions use registers or a 12-bit immediate, a constant stored in the instruction. With no flags register, branches compare two registers directly, and names like li and ret are assembler shortcuts.","core":true},{"t":"Six encoding formats","gist":"Every instruction is 32 bits in one of six formats, and the opcode and register fields always sit in the same bits, so decoding is cheap. You can encode by hand: addi x5, x0, 10 is 0x00A00293.","core":true},{"t":"Extensions and profiles","gist":"The small base grows through lettered extensions: M multiply, A atomics, F and D floating point, C compressed, V vectors. Profiles like RVA23 fix which ones an application processor must have, so the ecosystem doesn't fragment.","core":true},{"t":"Against x86-64 and ARM","gist":"x86-64 has variable-length instructions, 16 registers and flags; ARM and RISC-V use fixed 32-bit, load/store designs. Only RISC-V is open and royalty-free, and only it has no condition flags. This module is 60% of the RVFA exam.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"x0 is hardwired to zero; ra holds the return address, sp the stack pointer, and a0–a7 the arguments, with a0 and a1 also carrying return values.","part":0},{"p":"t registers are caller-saved, so a call may destroy them; s registers are callee-saved, so a function that uses them must restore them.","part":0},{"p":"ALU immediates are 12-bit signed, −2048 to +2047; branches reach ±4 KiB and jal reaches ±1 MiB.","part":1},{"p":"The opcode is always bits 6:0, rd 11:7, funct3 14:12, rs1 19:15 and rs2 24:20, wherever a format has them.","part":2},{"p":"RVA23, ratified in October 2024, makes the V (vector) and H (hypervisor) extensions mandatory for application processors.","part":3}]},"p38m3":{"big":"Pipelining overlaps instructions so one can finish every clock: throughput rises nearly fivefold while each instruction takes about as long as before, and hazards eat into the gain.","why":"Hazards and forwarding are the classic interview question, Amdahl's law governs every performance decision you will make, and Spectre is required knowledge for anyone in security.","parts":[{"t":"The five stages","gist":"The datapath is cut into five stages, fetch (IF), decode (ID), execute (EX), memory (MEM) and write-back (WB), with pipeline registers between them. Each instruction passes through all five, but a new one can start every clock.","core":true},{"t":"Hazards and their fixes","gist":"A hazard stops the next instruction starting on time. Forwarding passes a result straight back to the ALU, except when the next instruction needs a just-loaded value, which costs one stall; branch prediction guesses which way branches go.","core":true},{"t":"The performance equations","gist":"CPU time = instruction count × cycles per instruction × clock period. Amdahl's law caps any speedup by the part you didn't improve: making 90% of a program 10× faster gives only 5.26×.","core":true},{"t":"Beyond one instruction per cycle","gist":"Superscalar cores issue several instructions per cycle, and out-of-order execution lets independent instructions pass a stalled one. Register renaming removes false conflicts, and a reorder buffer retires results in program order so the program can't tell.","core":true},{"t":"Speculative execution and the Spectre lesson","gist":"CPUs run instructions on a guess and discard them if the guess was wrong. Registers are rolled back, but cache traces remain, and Spectre and Meltdown read secrets from them by timing memory accesses.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Pipelining improves throughput, not latency: with five stages of about 200 ps, one instruction finishes every 200 ps, yet each still takes about 1,000 ps.","part":0},{"p":"Forwarding fixes most data hazards, but a load-use hazard costs one stall unless the compiler schedules an independent instruction in between.","part":1},{"p":"Branch predictors reach 95–99% accuracy, and a misprediction on a deep modern pipeline costs 15–20 cycles.","part":1},{"p":"Amdahl's law: speedup = 1 / ((1 − p) + p/s). Making half a program infinitely fast gives at most 2×.","part":2},{"p":"Spectre and Meltdown leak secrets through cache timing left by speculative execution; defenses include KPTI, retpolines, speculation fences and coarser browser timers.","part":4}]},"p38m4":{"big":"Memory, not arithmetic, decides how fast real programs run: caches hide DRAM's 300-plus-cycle delay by betting your code reuses data and touches nearby addresses next.","why":"Knowing the hierarchy lets you make the same algorithm run 5–10× faster, and explains why Big-O analysis and tokens-per-second figures behave the way they do.","parts":[{"t":"SRAM, DRAM and the numbers","gist":"SRAM is fast but costly in chip area; DRAM is dense and cheap but must be refreshed. L1 answers in 4–5 cycles, about 1 ns, L2 in about 14 cycles, L3 in 40–50, while DRAM takes 70–100 ns.","core":true},{"t":"How a cache is organized","gist":"A cache stores 64-byte lines. An address splits into offset (byte in the line), index (which set) and tag (which memory block). Direct-mapped gives one slot, fully associative any slot, set-associative one of n ways.","core":true},{"t":"Hit and miss arithmetic","gist":"Average memory access time is hit time plus miss rate times miss penalty, applied level by level, so a small rise in misses hurts a lot. Write-back caches mark changed lines dirty and write them out only on eviction.","core":true},{"t":"Coherence for multicore: MESI","gist":"With a cache per core, MESI tracks each line as Modified, Exclusive, Shared or Invalid so caches can't disagree. False sharing, two threads writing different variables on one 64-byte line, makes that line bounce between cores.","core":true},{"t":"Virtual memory and the TLB","gist":"Page tables map 4 KiB virtual pages to physical frames, in two levels on Sv32 and three on Sv39. The TLB caches translations so the hardware needn't walk the tables on every access.","core":true},{"t":"Why cache-friendly code is 10× faster","gist":"Code with the same arithmetic and Big-O often runs 5–10× apart depending on how it walks memory. Read arrays in order so each 64-byte line delivers several useful values, avoid linked-list pointer chasing, and measure misses first.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"For a 32 KB 8-way cache with 64-byte lines and 32-bit addresses: 6 offset bits, 6 index bits (64 sets) and 20 tag bits.","part":1},{"p":"AMAT = hit time + miss rate × miss penalty, per level: 4 + 0.05 × (12 + 0.2 × 200) = 6.6 cycles.","part":2},{"p":"False sharing: two threads writing different variables in one 64-byte line make it ping-pong; pad or align hot per-thread data to 64 bytes.","part":3},{"p":"The TLB caches page-table translations; Sv39 uses three levels of 9 bits because 512 eight-byte entries fill one 4 KiB page.","part":4},{"p":"Walk arrays in memory order: switching matrix multiply from i-j-k to i-k-j typically runs 5–10× faster with the same arithmetic.","part":5}]},"p38m5":{"big":"Since clock speeds stalled, performance comes from parallel hardware, cores, SIMD, GPUs and NPUs, but memory bandwidth is usually the real limit, and the roofline tells you which.","why":"The roofline tells you in one line of arithmetic whether optimizing a kernel is worth it, and the accelerator landscape is where your AI work actually runs.","parts":[{"t":"Flynn's taxonomy and the forms of parallelism","gist":"Flynn sorts machines by instruction and data streams: SISD, SIMD, MISD and MIMD, and GPUs add SIMT. SMT lets two threads share one core to fill stalls, and Amdahl's serial fraction still caps multicore gains.","core":true},{"t":"SIMD and the RISC-V vector extension","gist":"SIMD applies one instruction to many values at once, but fixed-width sets like AVX need new instructions for every width. RISC-V V is vector-length agnostic: vsetvli asks the hardware how many elements it handles, so one loop runs anywhere.","core":true},{"t":"GPUs as throughput machines","gist":"A CPU spends transistors hiding latency for one thread; a GPU spends them on arithmetic and hides latency with sheer thread count, run in warps of 32. It shines on millions of regular operations and struggles with branchy, dependent code.","core":true},{"t":"TPUs, NPUs and domain-specific hardware","gist":"Matrix multiplication dominates neural networks, so TPUs and NPUs build grids of multiply-accumulate units that skip most instruction fetching. With general-purpose scaling slowed, hardware shaped to the problem gives the big gains.","core":false},{"t":"The roofline model: bandwidth is the wall","gist":"A kernel is capped by the lower of peak compute and memory bandwidth × arithmetic intensity, the FLOPs per byte moved. Below the ridge point, about 58 FLOP/byte on the RTX 4070, only moving fewer bytes helps.","core":true},{"t":"Heterogeneous SoCs","gist":"Modern chips combine CPU cores, a GPU, an NPU and I/O blocks, spending transistors where the energy is. Apple's unified memory lets CPU and GPU share one pool with no copies; the RP2350 carries both Arm and RISC-V cores.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"SMT shares one core's pipelines between two threads, typically for a 20–30% throughput gain; a 16-core, 32-thread Ryzen has 16 real cores.","part":0},{"p":"RISC-V V is vector-length agnostic: vsetvli returns how many elements this hardware will process, so one loop runs unchanged on any vector width.","part":1},{"p":"GPU threads run in warps of 32 in lockstep, and each SM keeps dozens of warps resident so one can issue while another waits on memory.","part":2},{"p":"Roofline: attainable = min(peak, bandwidth × arithmetic intensity). SAXPY's intensity of about 0.17 caps it near 84 GFLOP/s on a 504 GB/s card.","part":4},{"p":"LLM token generation is memory-bound: a 7B model at 4 bits reads about 3.5 GB per token, so 504 GB/s caps it near 144 tokens per second.","part":4}]},"p38m6":{"big":"With an FPGA you describe hardware in Verilog, and the tools turn that description into a chip configuration where every part runs at once, as fast as timing allows.","why":"This is how you build the processor you have been studying, and FPGA, digital-design and verification engineering are large, well-paid fields that your telecom background feeds into.","parts":[{"t":"What an FPGA is","gist":"An FPGA is a grid of logic cells, each holding a LUT, a tiny truth table that can act as any gate of its inputs, and a flip-flop. Block RAM, multipliers and rewirable routing surround them, and a bitstream sets it all.","core":true},{"t":"Verilog: describing hardware, not programming it","gist":"Verilog describes hardware that all exists at once; nothing runs line by line. Use = in always @* for combinational logic and <= in always @(posedge clk) for registers, because that matches how real flip-flops update together.","core":true},{"t":"A worked UART","gist":"A UART, a simple serial port, transmits with a small state machine and a shift register: idle, start bit, 8 data bits, stop bit. From 27 MHz, dividing by 234 gives 115,200 baud with only 0.16% error.","core":true},{"t":"Simulation and testbenches","gist":"Never trust untested hardware: a testbench drives your design's inputs, checks its outputs and saves a waveform. Icarus Verilog suits small designs and catches uninitialized X values; Verilator compiles to C++ and runs 10–100× faster.","core":true},{"t":"Synthesis, place-and-route and timing","gist":"Yosys, nextpnr and a packer turn Verilog into a bitstream. Every path must settle a setup time before the next clock edge, which sets fmax, the fastest safe clock; signals from outside or another clock need a two-flip-flop synchronizer.","core":true},{"t":"Safety on the bench","gist":"FPGA pins run at 3.3 V, and 5 V from an Arduino or old sensor destroys the chip, so use level shifters. Handle boards with static (ESD) care and never hot-plug cables on a powered board.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"A LUT4 is a 16-bit truth table that can implement any 4-input Boolean function; a 6-input LUT holds 64 bits.","part":0},{"p":"Registers use always @(posedge clk) with non-blocking <=; combinational logic uses always @* with blocking =.","part":1},{"p":"From a 27 MHz clock, a divider of 234 gives 115,200 baud with 0.16% error; each 8N1 byte costs 10 bit-times.","part":2},{"p":"fmax = 1 / (t_cq + t_logic + t_setup): 0.5 + 3.2 + 0.3 = 4.0 ns, so 250 MHz.","part":4},{"p":"Pass any asynchronous input, such as a button or a UART RX line, through a two-flip-flop synchronizer, or the flip-flop may go metastable.","part":4}]},"p38m7":{"big":"Running your own code on your own RISC-V CPU takes a soft-core SoC, the GNU toolchain, a linker script and startup code, and real chips add a layered boot chain.","why":"A working soft-core SoC and a taped-out chip are the portfolio that opens verification, embedded, FPGA and hardware-security jobs, and the RVFA certifies the knowledge.","parts":[{"t":"Soft cores and the SoC around them","gist":"A soft core is a CPU written in a hardware language and built into FPGA fabric, like PicoRV32. To do anything it needs a bus, RAM, a UART, GPIO and a memory map your software agrees with: together, an SoC.","core":true},{"t":"The toolchain","gist":"One GNU compiler targets every RISC-V variant through -march and -mabi flags. QEMU is fast and models a whole machine, Spike is the slow but authoritative reference, and RARS and Venus are for teaching.","core":true},{"t":"Bare metal: what runs when there is no operating system","gist":"With no operating system, you supply three things: a linker script for the memory map, a start file that sets the stack pointer and zeroes .bss, and drivers that reach hardware through volatile pointers.","core":true},{"t":"How real silicon boots","gist":"RISC-V has machine, supervisor and user modes, and booting climbs down through them: mask ROM, then SPL to start DRAM, then OpenSBI firmware in M-mode, U-Boot in S-mode, and Linux with a device tree describing the hardware.","core":true},{"t":"From RTL to silicon: the open flow and Tiny Tapeout","gist":"Making a chip turns a hardware description into standard cells, places and routes them, checks the layout, and sends it to a foundry. Open tools and foundry kits cover it, and Tiny Tapeout shares one die among hundreds of designs.","core":true},{"t":"The exam and the careers","gist":"The RVFA is a 90-minute online exam costing $250, weighted toward the ISA (35%) and assembly (25%). The careers it opens include verification, RTL and FPGA design, embedded firmware and hardware security.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"PicoRV32 is RV32I with optional M and C in 750–2,000 LUTs; SERV, bit-serial in about 200 LUTs, is the smallest RISC-V.","part":0},{"p":"Build with -march=rv32imc -mabi=ilp32 for a 32-bit core; QEMU's virt machine puts the UART at 0x10000000 and RAM at 0x80000000.","part":1},{"p":"Bare-metal start.S sets sp to the top of RAM, zeroes .bss and calls main; hardware registers are reached through volatile pointers.","part":2},{"p":"RISC-V boot chain: mask ROM → SPL → OpenSBI (M-mode) → U-Boot (S-mode) → Linux, with a device tree instead of ACPI.","part":3},{"p":"Tiny Tapeout fits a design of about a thousand standard cells onto a shared die; a tile has historically cost about $300.","part":4}]},"p39m1":{"big":"Mobile apps run in a strict sandbox on an OS that can kill them without warning, and usually reach users through stores with their own rules and fees.","why":"Every later module builds on this: the runtime explains performance, the lifecycle explains architecture, and the store rules decide whether your work ever reaches a user.","parts":[{"t":"Two stacks, one idea","gist":"Android runs on a Linux kernel and turns Kotlin or Java into DEX bytecode, which ART interprets or compiles; iOS compiles Swift to ARM64 machine code before the app ships, with no virtual machine. Both isolate apps as separate processes.","core":true},{"t":"The lifecycle: assume you will be killed","gist":"Both systems may kill your app without warning, and Android also recreates the screen (an Activity) on rotation. So save state early, restore it gracefully, and never rely on shutdown callbacks running.","core":true},{"t":"Sandboxing and the two permission models","gist":"Each app is walled off in its own sandbox. Sensitive access like camera, location or microphone needs the user's permission at runtime, which they can revoke; iOS also needs a purpose string explaining why, or the app is terminated on access.","core":true},{"t":"Stores, review and money","gist":"Google Play costs $25 once; new personal accounts first need a 12-tester, 14-day closed test. Apple costs $99 a year with human review, and each store's commission depends on your earnings, the market and whether it's a subscription.","core":true},{"t":"Native, cross-platform or web","gist":"Native gives top performance and day-one APIs but two codebases. Flutter, React Native and Kotlin Multiplatform share code, and a PWA skips the store. Decide by device needs, team skills, lifespan and the cost of lagging new features.","core":true},{"t":"Setting up the tools","gist":"Android Studio is free but wants 16 GB of RAM and hardware virtualization; Xcode is free but runs only on an Apple-silicon Mac. Without a Mac, learn Swift on Linux or Windows or rent a cloud Mac.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Android executes Kotlin or Java as DEX bytecode on ART; iOS compiles Swift ahead of time to ARM64 machine code, with no virtual machine.","part":0},{"p":"Rotation destroys and recreates an Android Activity by default; keep state in a ViewModel for rotation and in saved state or a database for process death.","part":1},{"p":"Since Android 6.0, dangerous permissions are granted at runtime and can be revoked; on iOS, accessing protected data without a purpose string terminates the app.","part":2},{"p":"Google Play: $25 once, plus a 12-tester, 14-day closed test for new personal accounts. Apple: $99 a year, with human review.","part":3},{"p":"A $4.99 monthly subscription with 1,000 subscribers grosses $4,990: you keep $4,241.50 at a 15% commission and $3,493 at 30%.","part":3}]},"p39m2":{"big":"In modern Android, the database is the source of truth, a ViewModel turns it into screen state, and Compose redraws only the parts that read changed state.","why":"This is the stack Android employers use and the Meta Android certificate teaches; recomposition and the database-as-source-of-truth idea are what interviews probe.","parts":[{"t":"Kotlin essentials","gist":"Kotlin builds null safety into types: String can't be null, String? must be handled, and !! is a red flag. Data classes, sealed types and coroutines, which pause without blocking a thread, are the features you'll use constantly.","core":true},{"t":"Views versus Compose","gist":"Old Android UI meant changing View objects by hand. Compose is declarative: a function describes the screen for a given state, and when state changes, only the functions that read it re-run. That re-run is recomposition.","core":true},{"t":"State, lifecycle and navigation","gist":"A ViewModel holds a screen's state through rotation and exposes it as a StateFlow, but it does not survive process death; small values go in SavedStateHandle. Navigation 3 makes the back stack a plain list you own.","core":true},{"t":"Data: Room, Retrofit, Ktor, DataStore","gist":"Room stores data in SQLite with queries checked at build time, and returns Flows that emit when tables change. Retrofit or Ktor fetch JSON from servers. The database is the single source of truth the UI observes.","core":true},{"t":"Hilt and Gradle","gist":"Hilt writes the code that hands each class its dependencies, at compile time, so tests can swap in fakes. Gradle builds the app; know minSdk, compileSdk and targetSdk, which Play requires to be 36 from August 31, 2026.","core":true},{"t":"The first app, assembled","gist":"In the assembled app, Room emits, the repository maps, the ViewModel turns it into UI state, and Compose redraws only the rows whose data changed. Adding a note writes to Room and the list updates itself.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"In Kotlin, String can never be null; String? must be handled with ?., ?: or a smart cast, and !! throws a NullPointerException on null.","part":0},{"p":"remember survives recomposition only; rememberSaveable survives rotation and process death for small values; everything else belongs in a ViewModel.","part":1},{"p":"A ViewModel survives configuration changes like rotation, not process death; durable data goes in Room, small restorable values in SavedStateHandle.","part":2},{"p":"Room checks the SQL in @Query annotations at build time, so a wrong column name is a compile error rather than a crash.","part":3},{"p":"Play requires targetSdk 36 for all new apps and updates from August 31, 2026; minSdk is the oldest device you support.","part":4}]},"p39m3":{"big":"In SwiftUI the screen is a function of state, so the key skill is putting state in the right place, with structs for data and classes for shared models.","why":"iOS users spend the most on apps and iOS skills are scarcer than Android ones; knowing where state lives and the honest no-Mac routes lets you start now.","parts":[{"t":"Swift essentials","gist":"Swift handles missing values with optionals, unwrapped with if let or ??. Structs are copied and suit data; classes are shared and suit models many views watch. Swift 6 makes data races, unsafe sharing between threads, a compile error.","core":true},{"t":"SwiftUI: views are a function of state","gist":"A SwiftUI view declares what to show for the current state, and the whole skill is where state lives: @State for one view, @Binding to lend a child write access, and an @Observable class for shared model state.","core":true},{"t":"Data and networking","gist":"SwiftData persists @Model classes, and @Query in a view gives live results that refresh the UI, like Room on Android. URLSession with async/await and Codable fetches JSON; tokens belong in the Keychain, never UserDefaults.","core":true},{"t":"The tools: Xcode, Simulator, App Store Connect, TestFlight","gist":"Xcode needs an Apple-silicon Mac. The Simulator is fast but not a real device, so test performance and camera on an iPhone. TestFlight betas take 100 internal and up to 10,000 external testers, and builds expire after 90 days.","core":true},{"t":"What you can do without a Mac","gist":"Without a Mac you can learn Swift on Linux or Windows, build with Swift Playground on an iPad, rent a cloud Mac or build in CI. Running macOS on non-Apple hardware violates its license and isn't a professional path.","core":true},{"t":"The first app, assembled","gist":"The weather screen puts it together: the view owns an @Observable model, .task(id: city) cancels and restarts loading whenever the city changes, and separate branches draw loading, success and failure, so no state is left undrawn.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Structs are copied on assignment and suit data like a Forecast; classes are shared references and suit models like a WeatherModel that many views observe.","part":0},{"p":"@State is view-local, @Binding lends read-write access to a child, and an @Observable class holds model state shared across views.","part":1},{"p":"SwiftData's @Query fetches live results that update the UI when data changes; store tokens in the Keychain, never in UserDefaults.","part":2},{"p":"TestFlight: up to 100 internal testers immediately, up to 10,000 external after Beta App Review of the first build; every build expires after 90 days.","part":3},{"p":"Lawful no-Mac routes: Swift on Linux or Windows (free), Swift Playground on iPad, a cloud Mac (roughly $20–100+ a month), CI runners or Xcode Cloud.","part":4}]},"p39m4":{"big":"Flutter, React Native and KMP each bet on sharing something different (pixels, web skills or logic), and the right choice depends on hardware needs, team skills and lifespan.","why":"Many mobile jobs are cross-platform and both Meta certificates include React Native; justifying a framework choice with real trade-offs is a senior-level skill.","parts":[{"t":"Flutter: a rendering engine with a language","gist":"Flutter draws every pixel itself with its Impeller engine, so screens look identical everywhere, and hot reload shows code changes in under a second. The catch: native look must be chosen, and the engine adds size and startup time.","core":true},{"t":"React Native and Expo: React, but the components are native","gist":"React Native runs your JavaScript or TypeScript on the Hermes engine but renders real native views, and its New Architecture replaced the old asynchronous bridge. Expo adds a curated SDK and cloud builds, even for iOS without a Mac.","core":true},{"t":"Kotlin Multiplatform: share the logic, keep the UI native","gist":"Kotlin Multiplatform shares logic, such as models, networking, storage and view models, across Android and iOS, while the UI stays native or uses Compose Multiplatform. It suits Android-first teams adding iOS one module at a time.","core":true},{"t":"Where each wins and loses","gist":"Native wins for games, AR, camera work and day-one OS features; React Native for web teams; Flutter often for small design-led teams; KMP for Android-first companies. For long-lived apps, remember frameworks have broken compatibility before.","core":true},{"t":"Testing on both platforms","gist":"Cross-platform doesn't mean test once: permissions, keyboards and fonts differ, and bugs hide there. Run Android and unit tests on Linux CI, iOS tests on macOS runners, and real devices in a device farm.","core":true},{"t":"Sharing code with the web (Domain 5)","gist":"Share code below the UI: types, business rules and API clients. React Native with Expo shares most, through a TypeScript package used by both app and web; Flutter and Kotlin can also target the web.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Flutter draws every pixel itself with Impeller instead of using platform widgets; calling setState tells it a widget's state changed and it should rebuild.","part":0},{"p":"React Native renders real platform views: a Text component becomes a TextView on Android and a UILabel on iOS. The New Architecture is the only one since 0.82.","part":1},{"p":"Kotlin Multiplatform shares business logic between Android and iOS; the UI is written natively or once in Compose Multiplatform.","part":2},{"p":"Games, AR, camera pipelines and anything fighting for the last frame go native: the framework fastest to start is slowest at the hardware edge.","part":3},{"p":"The code sharing that pays sits below the UI: types, business rules, API contracts and tests.","part":5}]},"p39m5":{"big":"What separates a shippable app from a toy is structure and evidence: clear layers, a local source of truth, measured performance, accessibility and tests.","why":"These qualities separate a portfolio toy from an app a company would ship, and they are exactly what mobile system-design interviews ask about.","parts":[{"t":"Layers and unidirectional data flow","gist":"Split the app into a UI layer, an optional domain layer and a data layer that owns storage and network. State flows down as one value, events flow up as calls, and only the state holder changes state.","core":true},{"t":"Offline-first sync, caching and pagination","gist":"Offline-first means the local database is the truth the UI reads. Edits go into an outbox table, a background worker syncs them with backoff and unique IDs that block duplicates, and you choose a conflict policy on purpose.","core":true},{"t":"Lists, images and the frame budget","gist":"Each frame must be ready in 16.7 ms at 60 Hz or 8.3 ms at 120 Hz, or the screen stutters. A decoded image costs width × height × 4 bytes, so decode photos at display size, not full size.","core":true},{"t":"Accessibility and localization","gist":"Screen readers read the labels your code exposes, so label icons, let text scale, make targets 48 dp or 44 pt, and keep contrast at least 4.5:1. Put all visible text in resources, with real plurals and right-to-left support.","core":true},{"t":"Testing and CI","gist":"Build a test pyramid: many fast unit tests, fewer UI tests, a few end-to-end flows. CI runs them on every pull request, Linux for Android and macOS for iOS, and fastlane scripts signing and store uploads.","core":true},{"t":"Crash reporting and analytics, ethically","gist":"Crash reporters show field crashes, and Android vitals flags apps above a 1.09% crash rate or a 0.47% app-not-responding (ANR) rate. Collect analytics minimally, keep personal data out of events, honor consent and declare it all honestly.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Unidirectional data flow: state flows down as one immutable value, events flow up as function calls, and only the state holder mutates state.","part":0},{"p":"Offline-first: the UI reads only the local database; each outbox edit carries a client-generated UUID as its idempotency key, so a retry can't create a duplicate.","part":1},{"p":"A decoded image costs width × height × 4 bytes: a 12-megapixel photo takes 48 MB in memory even if the JPEG is 3 MB.","part":2},{"p":"Touch targets are at least 48 × 48 dp on Android and 44 × 44 pt on iOS; text contrast is at least 4.5:1 under WCAG 2.2 AA.","part":3},{"p":"Android vitals treats a user-perceived crash rate above 1.09% or an ANR rate above 0.47% as bad behavior.","part":5}]},"p39m6":{"big":"Apply least privilege to data and energy: the narrowest capability, for the shortest time, when needed, with keys locked in hardware the app can only ask to use.","why":"It lets you use the camera, location and radios without draining the battery, leaking the owner's life or being killed by the OS, and building to MASVS earns an authorized tester's respect.","parts":[{"t":"Camera, location and sensors","gist":"Take the least you need: a photo picker needs no permission, approximate location often suffices, and background location is a separate grant. Strip GPS data from photos before upload unless location is the point.","core":true},{"t":"Bluetooth LE and NFC","gist":"Bluetooth LE devices expose services and characteristics you read, write or subscribe to. Android 12 split Bluetooth into separate scan, connect and advertise permissions, and negotiating a 247-byte MTU, the packet size, moves twelve times more data per packet.","core":true},{"t":"Push notifications and background work","gist":"Push works by token: the device registers, your server stores the token and asks FCM or APNs to deliver. Background work runs when the operating system allows, so use WorkManager or BGTaskScheduler and batch network transfers to save battery.","core":true},{"t":"Secrets: the Keystore and the Keychain","gist":"The Android Keystore and iOS Secure Enclave keep keys inside hardware; your app only asks them to encrypt or sign. Never put tokens in plain preferences or logs, or an API secret in the binary, which anyone can decompile.","core":true},{"t":"Transport security and certificate pinning","gist":"Both platforms require TLS by default. Certificate pinning accepts only your server's known key, which stops a rogue certificate authority but not someone who controls the device, and a careless pin can lock every installed copy out.","core":true},{"t":"OWASP MASVS, privacy labels and permission UX","gist":"OWASP MASVS is the checklist every mobile assessment cites, in eight groups from storage to privacy. Privacy labels and the Data safety form must match the code, and ask for permissions in context, with a path for every answer.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Since Android 12 users may grant only approximate location, and background location is a separate grant requested after foreground access.","part":0},{"p":"The default BLE ATT MTU of 23 bytes carries 20 bytes of payload; negotiating 247 carries 244, twelve times more per packet.","part":1},{"p":"From Android 15, dataSync and mediaProcessing foreground services may run only 6 hours in any 24; periodic WorkManager work runs at most every 15 minutes.","part":2},{"p":"Keep refresh tokens in the iOS Keychain or encrypted under an Android Keystore key, never in SharedPreferences, UserDefaults or logs.","part":3},{"p":"OWASP MASVS v2.1 has eight control groups: STORAGE, CRYPTO, AUTH, NETWORK, PLATFORM, CODE, RESILIENCE and PRIVACY.","part":5}]},"p39m7":{"big":"Shipping is its own discipline: sign builds so you can always update, release in small staged steps, and treat review, fees and budgets as engineering work.","why":"Shipping is where most first apps die, from a lost key or a bad build rolled to everyone; a shipped app with real users and a clean rollout is your strongest interview evidence.","parts":[{"t":"Signing: proving the app is yours","gist":"On Android you sign uploads with an upload key while Google keeps the app signing key, so a lost upload key can be reset. iOS ties builds to your team through Apple-issued certificates and provisioning profiles.","core":true},{"t":"Two store flows, side by side","gist":"Both stores give you test tracks before production: Play's internal, closed and open tracks, and Apple's TestFlight. Play reviews mostly automatically and rolls out by percentage; Apple uses human review and offers a 7-day phased release.","core":true},{"t":"Review guidelines and common rejections","gist":"Over 40% of Apple's unresolved review issues are guideline 2.1, app completeness: crashes, placeholders, broken links or a login the reviewer can't pass. Treat a rejection as a conversation: reply with the specific fix, demo credentials and a screen recording.","core":true},{"t":"Rollouts, monetization and fees","gist":"Roll out in steps, 1% up to 100%, gated on crash-free users, and keep risky features behind remote-config flags, because halting doesn't remove a build people already have. Your server verifies purchases, and fees depend on store and market.","core":true},{"t":"Size and startup budgets","gist":"Store limits are generous, so the real limit is a size and startup budget that CI enforces. Android vitals flags cold starts of 5 seconds or more; Apple's guidance is a first frame within 400 ms.","core":true},{"t":"The mobile career","gist":"Each rung of the ladder widens your scope, from shipping features to owning areas to setting direction. Interviews test coding, platform knowledge, mobile system design and shipped stories, and two or three installable apps beat ten tutorials.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"With Play App Signing a lost upload key can be reset; if you manage the app signing key yourself and lose it, that app can never be updated again.","part":0},{"p":"Apple's phased release runs over 7 days (1, 2, 5, 10, 20, 50, 100%) and can be paused for up to 30 days in total.","part":1},{"p":"Guideline 2.1, app completeness, accounts for over 40% of Apple's unresolved review issues: crashes, placeholder text, broken links, unusable logins.","part":2},{"p":"Halting a staged rollout stops new installs, but everyone who already has the build keeps it; prepare remote-config flags in advance.","part":3},{"p":"Google's 2026 fees in the US, UK and EEA: 10% on subscriptions and on the first $1 million, plus 5% when Play Billing processes the payment.","part":3}]},"p40m1":{"big":"A game is a loop that must deliver a finished frame every 16.7 ms at 60 fps, so every system has to fit its share of that millisecond budget.","why":"Every bug and every optimization in the rest of this domain is about where work sits in this loop and how many milliseconds it costs.","parts":[{"t":"The loop: input, update, render","gist":"Every game repeats one loop: read input, update the world, render into a hidden back buffer, then swap it onto the screen. In a framework you write this loop; an engine owns it and calls your code.","core":true},{"t":"Time: variable steps, fixed steps and interpolation","gist":"Multiplying by frame time fixes simple motion but not physics. Instead, step the simulation in fixed slices using an accumulator, draw between the last two states, and clamp frame time so a slow step can't spiral into a freeze.","core":true},{"t":"The frame budget","gist":"Turn every frame-rate target into milliseconds: 1000 ÷ fps, so 16.7 ms at 60 fps and 8.33 ms at 120. All systems share that budget, and frame-time spikes matter more than the average.","core":true},{"t":"Engines and frameworks","gist":"An engine bundles editor, renderer, physics, audio and export, and runs the loop for you; a framework gives you libraries and leaves the architecture to you. Learn the loop in a framework once, then ship with an engine.","core":true},{"t":"The industry, honestly","gist":"Studios range from AAA teams of hundreds to indies of one to twenty, with many programming specialties. Crunch, mandatory pre-milestone overtime, and big layoff waves are real, so ask how employers handled their last milestone.","core":false},{"t":"Set up your workbench","gist":"Install Unity Hub with the 6.3 LTS editor, or download Godot with no installer or account. Use Git with LFS for large assets from day one, and pygame-ce for the first lab.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Double buffering draws into a hidden back buffer and swaps it in; vsync waits for the display's refresh, preventing tearing at up to one refresh of extra latency.","part":0},{"p":"Fixed timestep: an accumulator collects real time and runs whole steps; alpha = accumulator ÷ step sets how far to interpolate the drawn position.","part":1},{"p":"Clamp frame time to avoid the spiral of death. Unity's FixedUpdate defaults to 0.02 s; Godot runs physics 60 times per second.","part":1},{"p":"Frame budget = 1000 ÷ fps: 33.3 ms at 30, 16.7 ms at 60, 11.1 ms at 90 and 8.33 ms at 120.","part":2},{"p":"Unity Personal is free up to US$200,000 in revenue and funding; Unreal takes a 5% royalty above US$1 million gross; Godot is MIT licensed with no fees.","part":3}]},"p40m2":{"big":"Rendering is a fixed pipeline: matrices move each vertex onto the screen, the rasterizer finds the covered pixels, and a shader colors each one, millions of times in parallel.","why":"This is your matrices and GPU parallelism doing their day job, and knowing it lets you read a frame capture and say exactly why a scene is slow.","parts":[{"t":"The pipeline in one pass","gist":"The CPU fills vertex buffers and issues a draw. The GPU then transforms each vertex, finds the pixels each triangle covers, colors each pixel, and keeps the nearest with a depth test. You write the vertex and fragment shaders.","core":true},{"t":"Coordinate spaces and the matrices that join them","gist":"A vertex moves from object to world to view to clip space through the model, view and projection matrices, in that order. Dividing by w gives normalized device coordinates (NDC), where visible x and y span −1 to 1.","core":true},{"t":"Cameras and projection","gist":"Perspective projection divides by depth, so something twice as far looks half as large. Orthographic projection skips the divide for 2D, UI and shadow maps, and moving the near plane out cures most z-fighting, flicker between overlapping surfaces.","core":true},{"t":"Light: Lambert, Blinn–Phong and PBR","gist":"Diffuse brightness follows the angle to the light, max(0, N·L). Blinn–Phong adds a highlight whose tightness an exponent sets, and PBR uses energy-conserving math driven by albedo, metallic and roughness maps. Light in linear space, encode sRGB last.","core":true},{"t":"Textures, UVs and memory","gist":"UVs tell each pixel where to sample its texture, and mipmaps store shrunken copies so distant surfaces don't shimmer, for a third more memory. A 2048 × 2048 RGBA8 texture is 16 MiB; block compression cuts that to 2–4 MiB.","core":true},{"t":"A first shader","gist":"A shader is a small program the GPU runs for one vertex or one pixel. You write it for a single pixel, with no loop, and the GPU runs it for every pixel covered.","core":true},{"t":"What the GPU does per frame, and why draw calls matter","gist":"The GPU runs shaders in lockstep groups, 32 threads on NVIDIA, so a diverging branch runs both paths. The CPU's limit is draw calls, each costing setup work, so batch with merged meshes, instancing and atlases.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"clip = P * V * M * vec4(pos, 1.0), read right to left; dividing by w gives NDC, where visible x and y span −1 to 1.","part":1},{"p":"Lambert diffuse = albedo × light × max(0, N·L); a light 45° off the normal gives cos 45° = 0.707, about 71% brightness.","part":3},{"p":"Do lighting in linear space and encode to sRGB only at output; mixing the two makes colors washed out or too dark.","part":3},{"p":"A 2048 × 2048 RGBA8 texture is 16 MiB, or 21.3 MiB with mipmaps; BC7 block compression brings it to 4 MiB.","part":4},{"p":"Each draw call costs CPU time binding state; a few thousand per frame can saturate a core while the GPU idles, so batch and instance.","part":6}]},"p40m3":{"big":"How you build game objects, from deep classes, small components or ECS data, decides how fast the design can change, how many you can afford and how often it stutters.","why":"Clean architecture decides how fast a team can change the game, and the garbage-collection and save rules are exactly what the Unity exams test.","parts":[{"t":"From inheritance to components","gist":"Deep class hierarchies break when designers want odd combinations, so engines use composition: an entity holds a transform plus small components that each do one job. Unity calls the entity a GameObject, Godot uses node trees, Unreal uses Actors.","core":true},{"t":"ECS and data-oriented design","gist":"In an ECS, entities are IDs, components plain data, and systems sweep all entities holding certain components. Packing the data lets the cache stream it: 100,000 scattered objects can cost 10 ms, packed well under a millisecond.","core":true},{"t":"Scripting: C# for Unity, GDScript for Godot","gist":"Engines call your scripts in a fixed order. In Unity, cache references in Awake, read input in Update and move physics bodies in FixedUpdate; Godot's _physics_process plays FixedUpdate's role. Exposed fields let designers tune numbers without touching code.","core":true},{"t":"Scene graphs, prefabs, input and UI","gist":"In a scene graph, a child's world position follows its parent's, so moving a ship moves its turrets. Prefabs are reusable templates, variants inherit and override them, and shared data like weapon stats lives in ScriptableObjects or Resources.","core":true},{"t":"Serialization and saves","gist":"Save data, not objects. Version every file, write to a temp file and rename it so a crash can't destroy the only copy, and validate on load. Treat saves as untrusted input, and never use BinaryFormatter.","core":true},{"t":"Scripting performance: garbage collection and pooling","gist":"Every allocation brings the next garbage collection closer, and a 5 ms pause in a 16.7 ms frame is a visible hitch. Aim for zero allocations per frame, and reuse objects from a pool instead of creating and destroying them.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Scattered objects risk a DRAM miss of about 100 ns each: 100,000 × 100 ns = 10 ms. Packed contiguously, the same update costs well under a millisecond.","part":1},{"p":"Unity order: Awake, OnEnable, Start, then each frame zero or more FixedUpdate calls, one Update and one LateUpdate. Read input in Update, move bodies in FixedUpdate.","part":2},{"p":"A prefab variant inherits every change to its base prefab but overrides chosen values, like a FastSoldier that overrides Soldier's speed.","part":3},{"p":"Crash-safe saves: version the file, write a temp file and rename it over the old one, validate on load, and never use BinaryFormatter.","part":4},{"p":"Size a pool as fire rate × lifetime × shooters, plus headroom: 40 bullets per second living 6 seconds is 240, so a pool of 300.","part":5}]},"p40m4":{"big":"A physics engine is a fixed-rate integrator wrapped around a collision detector, and knowing its parts lets you predict and fix jitter, tunneling and drifting replays.","why":"Physics bugs are the most visible bugs a game can have; understanding the steps, shapes and solver lets you fix them instead of fiddling with settings.","parts":[{"t":"Rigid bodies and integration","gist":"Each step, forces become acceleration and an integrator, the update rule, moves bodies forward. Explicit Euler gains energy and drifts; semi-implicit Euler, velocity first then position, behaves far better and is the game standard; Verlet suits cloth and ropes.","core":true},{"t":"Collision detection: narrow phase and broad phase","gist":"The narrow phase tests whether two shapes touch, with box checks or the separating axis theorem: convex shapes are apart exactly when, on some axis, their projections don't overlap. The broad phase uses grids or trees to skip most pairs.","core":true},{"t":"Tunneling, resolution and the solver","gist":"A fast object can be on one side of a wall in one step and past it in the next, never overlapping: tunneling. Fix it with continuous collision detection, raycasts, smaller steps or thicker colliders.","core":true},{"t":"Physics engines and character controllers","gist":"Most games move the player with a kinematic controller that sweeps a capsule, not a floaty dynamic body. Tune jumps from height h and time to apex t: g = 2h ÷ t² and v₀ = 2h ÷ t.","core":true},{"t":"Determinism and why networking cares","gist":"Determinism means the same inputs always give bit-identical results, and floating point makes that hard because the order of operations can change the answer. Replays, lockstep and rollback networking depend on it; server-authoritative games tolerate its absence.","core":true},{"t":"Procedural generation","gist":"Procedural generation builds worlds from rules and a seed, so the same seed rebuilds the same world. Noise makes terrain, cellular automata carve caves, wave function collapse fits tiles by adjacency rules, and you validate the result is completable.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Semi-implicit Euler updates velocity first, then position with the new velocity; it is the game standard, while explicit Euler steadily adds energy.","part":0},{"p":"n bodies form n(n−1)/2 pairs, so 1,000 bodies make 499,500; a broad phase such as a grid, sweep-and-prune or AABB tree prunes them.","part":1},{"p":"Tunneling: a 300 m/s bullet with a 0.02 s step moves 6 m per step and never meets a 0.3 m wall; use continuous detection or raycasts.","part":2},{"p":"Jump tuning: g = 2h ÷ t² and v₀ = 2h ÷ t, so a 3 m jump peaking in 0.4 s needs g = 37.5 m/s² and v₀ = 15 m/s.","part":3},{"p":"Floating-point addition is not associative, so determinism must be engineered; input replays, lockstep and rollback all need it.","part":4}]},"p40m5":{"big":"Animation, audio, AI and game feel turn a correct simulation into something that feels alive, and accessibility decides who gets to play at all.","why":"These systems decide whether players love or abandon a game in its first minute, and they are where most gameplay programmers spend their days.","parts":[{"t":"Animation: state machines, blending and IK","gist":"An animation state machine picks which clip plays, with guarded transitions and short crossfades; a blend tree mixes clips by a value like speed. Inverse kinematics works backward from where a foot or hand must go to find joint angles.","core":true},{"t":"Audio: the invisible half","gist":"Game audio runs at 48 kHz; one minute of 16-bit stereo is 11.52 MB uncompressed, so music streams. Buses mix sounds and lower music under dialogue, 3D sources are positioned in space, and FMOD or Wwise often handle authoring.","core":true},{"t":"Game AI: state machines, behavior trees, utility","gist":"Game AI aims for fun, not intelligence: readable, fair, beatable enemies. State machines are simple to debug; behavior trees scale better, with Sequences acting as AND and Selectors as OR, and are often ticked 5–10 times a second.","core":true},{"t":"Pathfinding: A* on a navmesh","gist":"A* expands the node with the lowest cost-so-far plus estimated cost-to-go, and if the estimate never overestimates, the path is optimal. 3D games run it over a baked navmesh of walkable polygons, then smooth the path with the funnel algorithm.","core":true},{"t":"Juice and game feel","gist":"Game feel is engineering with numbers: keep input latency under about 100 ms, forgive slightly late or early jump presses, freeze heavy hits for 50–100 ms, and smooth cameras independently of frame rate.","core":true},{"t":"Accessibility is a feature","gist":"Accessibility is part of the job: full control remapping, good subtitles, never color alone, toggles for shake and flashing, scalable text, and visual versions of audio cues. About 1 in 12 men have a color-vision deficiency.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Two-bone IK uses the law of cosines: a 0.5 m thigh and shin with a target 0.8 m away give a knee angle of about 106°.","part":0},{"p":"One minute of 16-bit stereo at 48 kHz is 48,000 × 2 channels × 2 bytes × 60 = 11.52 MB uncompressed.","part":1},{"p":"In a behavior tree a Sequence fails on its first failing child (AND), and a Selector succeeds on its first succeeding child (OR).","part":2},{"p":"A* expands the lowest f(n) = g(n) + h(n); an admissible heuristic, one that never overestimates, guarantees an optimal path.","part":3},{"p":"Coyote time (80–150 ms) lets a jump just after leaving a ledge still work; a jump buffer (100–150 ms) counts a press just before landing.","part":4}]},"p40m6":{"big":"Online games feel instant by predicting locally while an authoritative server decides the truth, and performance comes from measuring where the milliseconds go before cutting.","why":"Netcode and profiling are two of the best-paid specialties in games, and the cheating side connects straight to your security and reverse-engineering goals, practiced only on your own servers.","parts":[{"t":"Topologies: client–server, lockstep, rollback","gist":"Most action games run a server that owns the truth and ticks at a fixed rate, while clients send inputs. Lockstep and rollback send only inputs but need every machine to simulate identically. Games use UDP to avoid TCP's stalls.","core":true},{"t":"Authority, prediction and reconciliation","gist":"Your own input shows instantly through prediction, and when the server's state arrives, the client rewinds to it and replays inputs the server hasn't acknowledged. Other players are drawn about 100 ms in the past, between two snapshots.","core":true},{"t":"Lag compensation","gist":"Because you see enemies slightly in the past, the server rewinds their hitboxes by about your round trip plus interpolation delay to judge your shot. That favors the shooter, and a rewind cap makes very laggy players lead targets.","core":true},{"t":"Netcode libraries and budgets","gist":"Each engine has its own netcode libraries; avoid welding your game to one hosting vendor. Size bandwidth before building, then shrink it: quantize values to fewer bits, send changes against acknowledged snapshots, and send each client only nearby entities.","core":true},{"t":"Cheating and server authority","gist":"The attacker owns the client, so never trust it: send inputs, not positions, validate on the server, and send only what each client must know. Anti-cheat backs this up; test only on your own server or sanctioned targets.","core":true},{"t":"Profiling: measure, then cut","gist":"Profile a development build on target hardware, then find what bounds the frame: if lowering resolution raises fps, the GPU is pixel-bound; if not, look at the CPU. Then work an optimization checklist against a per-subsystem budget.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Prediction shows your input at once; reconciliation rewinds to the server's authoritative state and replays every input it hasn't acknowledged yet.","part":1},{"p":"Lag compensation rewinds hitboxes by about RTT plus interpolation delay; servers cap the rewind, often at 200–250 ms.","part":2},{"p":"Snapshots of about 432 bytes at 30 Hz cost about 13 KB/s per client; a position quantized to 1 cm across ±512 m needs 17 bits per axis, not 32.","part":3},{"p":"Never trust the client: send inputs, not positions, and check speed, cooldowns, line of sight and inventory ownership on the server.","part":4},{"p":"If lowering the resolution raises fps, you are pixel-bound on the GPU; if it changes nothing, look at the CPU threads.","part":5}]},"p40m7":{"big":"A shipped small game beats an unfinished big one, so ship with automated builds, honest monetization and real playtests, and turn each release into public proof.","why":"Employers hire people who finish and ship; this module turns your labs into public proof and a certification plan.","parts":[{"t":"Build pipelines for PC, web and mobile","gist":"Automate builds on every push, with engine command-line exports and CI such as GameCI. Mobile adds signing with an upload key you must never lose, and keep debug symbols so crash reports stay readable.","core":true},{"t":"Storefronts and their numbers","gist":"Steam charges US$100 per game, recouped after US$1,000, and takes 30%, falling to 20% at scale; itch.io lets you choose its cut. Put up a Steam page months before launch to gather wishlists.","core":true},{"t":"Playtesting and design documents","gist":"Playtest early: watch silently, measure, and trust players to find problems more than to propose fixes. Write a one-page pitch and living design docs, and reach a vertical slice, one level at final quality, before full production.","core":true},{"t":"Monetization ethics and law","gist":"Regulators now police manipulative monetization: the FTC's US$520 million Epic settlement covered children's privacy and tricks that caused unwanted purchases, and some countries restrict loot boxes. Show real prices, disclose odds, avoid fake urgency and protect minors.","core":true},{"t":"Portfolio, career paths and jams","gist":"Pick a path, such as gameplay, engine, graphics or tools, and show three to five finished small games, each browser-playable with a short video, source and a write-up with numbers. Game jams are the fastest way to practice finishing.","core":false},{"t":"The Unity certification and the alternatives","gist":"Unity's path for you is Associate: Programmer on Unity 6, then Professional: Programmer, through Pearson VUE, valid two years. Unreal has no equivalent professional exam, and Godot has no certification; finished games are the credential.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"IL2CPP compiles C# ahead of time to C++ and is required for iOS, but it is not protection: reverse-engineering tools still open it.","part":0},{"p":"Steam: US$100 per game, recouped after US$1,000; a 30% share, 25% above US$10 million and 20% above US$50 million.","part":1},{"p":"Players find problems reliably but propose fixes poorly; about five fresh testers surface most usability issues.","part":2},{"p":"A vertical slice is one level at final quality across every system, proving the whole experience; it comes after the prototype and before alpha.","part":2},{"p":"Apple and Google require games to disclose the odds of every loot box, and Belgium treated paid loot boxes as illegal gambling in 2018.","part":3}]},"p41m1":{"big":"A qubit is a unit vector of complex amplitudes, and quantum speedup comes from making those amplitudes interfere, which helps only a short list of problems.","why":"This is the threat behind the post-quantum migration you will run as a security engineer, and the honest picture you need to judge every quantum headline.","parts":[{"t":"Bits, probabilistic bits and qubits","gist":"A qubit is a pair of complex numbers called amplitudes, α|0⟩ + β|1⟩, with |α|² + |β|² = 1. Unlike probabilities, amplitudes can be negative and cancel, so |+⟩ and |−⟩ give the same 50/50 odds yet are perfectly distinguishable.","core":true},{"t":"Superposition and measurement, stated precisely","gist":"Measuring gives 0 with probability |α|² and 1 with probability |β|², and the state becomes what you saw. Each run yields one bit per qubit, so programs repeat many shots and return counts.","core":true},{"t":"The Bloch sphere","gist":"Any single-qubit state is a point on the Bloch sphere: |0⟩ at the north pole, |1⟩ at the south, |+⟩ and |−⟩ on the equator. Gates rotate the sphere. The picture works for one qubit only.","core":true},{"t":"What a quantum computer can and cannot speed up","gist":"Equal superposition alone gives a random answer when measured. Speedup comes from interference: the circuit makes wrong answers' amplitudes cancel and right ones add. Known big wins are few: Shor's factoring, Grover's search, and simulating quantum systems.","core":true},{"t":"How we got here","gist":"Feynman proposed quantum computers in 1981 to simulate physics; Shor found factoring in 1994 and Grover search in 1996. IBM put a five-qubit processor on the cloud in 2016, and Preskill named today's noisy era NISQ in 2018.","core":false},{"t":"Today's hardware","gist":"Four main platforms compete: superconducting circuits, trapped ions, neutral atoms and photonics, with different gate speeds and connectivity. All are still at the NISQ stage: impressive physics, no useful error-corrected computation yet.","core":false},{"t":"The real timeline for a cryptographically relevant machine","gist":"Estimates for breaking RSA-2048 have fallen from about 20 million noisy qubits to under one million, while today's machines hold roughly 100–1,000. Most experts expect such a machine in the 2030s, but recorded traffic can be decrypted later.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"|+⟩ and |−⟩ both measure 50/50, yet one Hadamard turns |+⟩ into |0⟩ and |−⟩ into |1⟩: the sign of an amplitude matters.","part":0},{"p":"Born rule: measuring α|0⟩ + β|1⟩ gives 0 with probability |α|² and 1 with probability |β|²; one run gives one bit per qubit.","part":1},{"p":"Shor breaks RSA, Diffie–Hellman and elliptic curves in polynomial time; Grover gives only a quadratic √N gain, so AES-256 survives.","part":3},{"p":"BQP, the class of problems quantum computers solve efficiently, contains P and is believed not to contain NP-complete problems.","part":3},{"p":"NIST deprecates 112-bit RSA and ECC after 2030 and disallows them after 2035; most experts place a cryptographically relevant machine in the 2030s.","part":6}]}});
